// Book introductions: the Pentateuch
Object.assign(BOOK_INTRO,{
genesis:{era:"primeval",when:"From creation to Joseph's death in Egypt",genre:"Sacred history in figurative and narrative form",
 lede:"Genesis means 'beginning'. It tells how everything began (the world, humanity, sin, marriage, nations, and God's plan to save), and it introduces the family through whom God will bless all the earth.",
 story:`<p><strong>Chapters 1–11, the first ages.</strong> God creates by his word in six 'days' and rests on the seventh. Man and woman are made in his image and placed in a garden as friends of God. Tempted by the serpent, they disobey, and harmony is lost: shame, blame, pain and death enter. Sin spreads to the first murder (Cain and Abel) and violence fills the earth until the Flood. God saves Noah's family and makes a covenant with every living creature. Humanity tries again to make a name for itself at Babel and is scattered.</p>
<p><strong>Chapters 12–50, the patriarchs.</strong> God calls Abram to leave his country and promises land, offspring and blessing for all nations. The rest of Genesis is the story of that promise surviving barrenness, family rivalry, deceit and famine: Abraham and Sarah's long wait for Isaac; the near-sacrifice of Isaac; Jacob's stolen blessing, exile, marriages and wrestling with God; and Joseph, sold by his brothers, who rises to rule Egypt and forgives them. The book ends with Israel's family settled in Egypt, waiting.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,2,"Creation: the six days and the garden"],[3,3,"The fall"],[4,11,"From Cain to Babel: the spread of sin, the Flood, the nations"],[12,25,"Abraham: call, covenant, Isaac"],[26,26,"Isaac"],[27,36,"Jacob and Esau"],[37,50,"Joseph and his brothers"]],
 people:[["Adam and Eve","The first man and woman, made in God's image and joined in marriage (2:24). Their disobedience is the 'original sin' whose effects all inherit.","Paul reads Adam as a 'figure of him who was to come' (Rom 5:14); the Fathers see Mary as the new Eve."],
  ["Noah","A righteous man who obeys God, builds the ark and receives the first universal covenant (ch. 9).","The ark is a figure of the Church, and the Flood of baptism (1 Pet 3:20–21)."],
  ["Abraham (Abram)","Called from Ur at 75, the 'father of all who believe'. He trusts God's promises and is willing to offer Isaac.","Faith 'reputed to him unto justice' (15:6) is the text Paul builds Romans 4 on; the Roman Canon calls him 'our father in faith'."],
  ["Sarah","Abraham's wife, barren until in old age she bears Isaac, the child of promise.","God keeps promises beyond human possibility (Heb 11:11)."],
  ["Isaac","The son of the promise, bound on Mount Moriah and spared (ch. 22).","The Fathers read the binding of Isaac as a type of the sacrifice of Christ, the beloved Son carrying the wood."],
  ["Jacob (Israel)","Isaac's younger son, a schemer who becomes a man transformed by God after wrestling at the Jabbok (ch. 32). Father of the twelve tribes.","God chooses the unlikely and works through flawed people; his new name becomes the name of the people."],
  ["Joseph","Jacob's favourite son, sold into slavery, unjustly imprisoned, raised to rule Egypt, who forgives his brothers.","'You thought evil against me: but God turned it into good' (50:20) is the Bible's great statement of providence; the Fathers see Joseph as a type of Christ."],
  ["Judah","The brother who proposes selling Joseph, later offers himself in Benjamin's place.","The royal blessing (49:10) makes Judah the tribe of David and of the Messiah."]],
 themes:[["Creation is good","God declares creation 'very good' (1:31). Matter is not evil; the world is ordered and intelligible because it comes from one wise Creator."],["The image of God","Every human being, male and female, bears God's image (1:27), the root of human dignity (CCC 355–357)."],["Sin and its spread","Sin breaks four relationships: with God, self, others and creation. Each sin in chs 3–11 grows from the last."],["Covenant and promise","God binds himself by covenant to Noah and Abraham. Promise, not human merit, drives the story."],["Providence","God brings good from evil without causing the evil, as in the Joseph story."]],
 christ:"Genesis 3:15, God's word that the woman's offspring will crush the serpent's head, is called the 'Protoevangelium', the first announcement of the Gospel (CCC 410–411). Christ is the new Adam, Melchizedek's bread and wine foreshadow the Eucharist (14:18; Heb 7), Isaac carrying wood up Moriah prefigures the Cross, and Jacob's ladder is the image Jesus applies to himself (Jn 1:51).",
 church:"Genesis grounds the doctrines of creation from nothing, the goodness of creation, the unity of the human race, the image of God, marriage as one flesh between man and woman (2:24; CCC 1605), original sin (CCC 396–409) and the promise of redemption. Pius XII (Humani Generis, 1950) and the Catechism (CCC 283–284, 390) allow scientific accounts of the world's development while holding that the human soul is created directly by God and that humanity's fall was a real event at the beginning.",
 thomas:"Thomas treats the six days in ST I qq.65–74, distinguishing the work of creation, distinction and adornment, and he follows Augustine in allowing more than one reading of the 'days'. On Adam's state of original justice, see ST I qq.94–102; on original sin, I-II qq.81–83. Augustine wrote a whole Literal Commentary on Genesis; Ambrose's Hexaemeron preaches the six days.",
 hard:[["Does Genesis 1 contradict evolution and the age of the universe?","The Church does not read Genesis 1 as a scientific textbook. It is a theological account in a figurative form (CCC 337, 390). Augustine already argued that the 'days' need not be 24-hour periods. What the text teaches without error is that God created everything, freely and good, and that humans are made in his image. Catholic teaching permits evolutionary biology for the body while affirming that each human soul is directly created by God (Humani Generis 36)."],["Were Adam and Eve real people?","The Church teaches that the fall was 'a primeval event… at the beginning of the history of man' (CCC 390) and Humani Generis 37 rejected polygenism as not reconcilable with original sin. Theologians continue to discuss how this fits with population genetics; the teaching itself is that humanity's sin had a real beginning."],["Why does God command Abraham to sacrifice Isaac?","The text says God 'tempted' (tested) Abraham and stops him before any harm (22:12). It teaches Abraham's complete trust and God's rejection of child sacrifice, which neighbouring religions practised, while foreshadowing the Father who 'spared not even his own Son' (Rom 8:32)."]],
 keys:[["Gen 1:1, 27, 31","creation, the image of God, 'very good'"],["Gen 2:24","the institution of marriage, quoted by Jesus (Mt 19:5)"],["Gen 3:15","the Protoevangelium"],["Gen 12:1–3","the call of Abram and the blessing for all nations"],["Gen 15:6","faith reckoned as righteousness"],["Gen 22","the binding of Isaac"],["Gen 28:12","Jacob's ladder"],["Gen 49:10","the sceptre shall not depart from Judah"],["Gen 50:20","God turned it into good"]],
 author:"Jewish and Christian tradition attributes the Pentateuch to Moses. Most scholars hold that it reached its final form over centuries, drawing on older traditions and edited after the exile. The Pontifical Biblical Commission (1948) allowed that Moses is the book's principal source while later material was added; inspiration does not depend on resolving this question."},

exodus:{era:"exodus",when:"Israel in Egypt to the building of the Tabernacle at Sinai",genre:"Narrative and law",
 lede:"Exodus means 'going out'. It is the Old Testament's central story of salvation: God frees his people from slavery, makes them his own by covenant, and comes to live among them.",
 story:`<p>A new Pharaoh enslaves the growing Hebrew people and orders their baby boys killed. Moses, saved in a basket and raised in Pharaoh's court, flees after killing an Egyptian. At the burning bush God reveals his name, 'I AM WHO AM', and sends Moses back. Ten plagues show the Lord's power over Egypt's gods. On the night of the tenth plague Israel eats the Passover lamb, marks its doors with blood, and leaves. God parts the sea and destroys Pharaoh's army.</p>
<p>In the desert God feeds them with manna and water from the rock. At Mount Sinai he makes a covenant: 'you shall be to me a priestly kingdom, and a holy nation' (19:6). He gives the Ten Commandments and the laws of the covenant, which Israel ratifies with sacrificial blood. While Moses is on the mountain, the people make a golden calf; Moses intercedes and the covenant is renewed. The book ends with the Tabernacle built and the glory of the Lord filling it.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,6,"Slavery, Moses' birth and call, the divine name"],[7,11,"The plagues"],[12,15,"Passover and the crossing of the sea"],[16,18,"Into the desert: manna, water, Jethro"],[19,24,"Covenant at Sinai: Ten Commandments and the Book of the Covenant"],[25,31,"Instructions for the Tabernacle and priesthood"],[32,34,"The golden calf and the covenant renewed"],[35,40,"The Tabernacle built; the glory descends"]],
 people:[["Moses","Hebrew raised as an Egyptian prince, shepherd in Midian, then the mediator of the covenant and lawgiver. He speaks with God 'face to face, as a man is wont to speak to his friend' (33:11).","The model of the prophet and intercessor (CCC 2574–2577); Christ is the prophet 'like Moses' (Deut 18:15; Acts 3:22) and greater than him (Heb 3)."],
  ["Aaron","Moses' elder brother and spokesman, consecrated the first high priest, but also the maker of the golden calf.","The Aaronic priesthood is the background to the Letter to the Hebrews' teaching on Christ's priesthood."],
  ["Miriam","Moses' sister, who watched over him as a baby and led the song of victory at the sea (15:20–21).","One of the first women called a prophetess in Scripture."],
  ["Pharaoh","The unnamed king of Egypt who 'hardens his heart' against God.","A study in how repeated refusal hardens the will; Paul discusses him in Romans 9."],
  ["Jethro","Moses' father-in-law, priest of Midian, who advises him to delegate judgment.","Practical wisdom from outside Israel; an early model of shared governance."]],
 themes:[["The divine name","'I AM WHO AM' (3:14) reveals God as the one who simply is (CCC 205–213). Thomas calls it the most proper name of God (ST I q.13 a.11)."],["Passover and redemption","Salvation by the blood of a spotless lamb, eaten in a memorial meal (ch. 12)."],["Covenant","God's free choice of Israel, sealed in blood (24:8), with the Law as the way of life for a redeemed people."],["God dwelling with his people","The Tabernacle is a portable heaven on earth; the book ends with glory filling it."],["Intercession","Moses pleads for the people after the calf and God relents (32:11–14)."]],
 christ:"Jesus is the true Passover Lamb (Jn 1:29; 1 Cor 5:7), dying at the hour the lambs were sacrificed in John's Gospel and with no bone broken (Ex 12:46; Jn 19:36). At the Last Supper he speaks of 'the blood of the new testament' (Mt 26:28), echoing 'the blood of the covenant' (Ex 24:8). He is the bread from heaven greater than the manna (Jn 6:32–35), the rock from which water flows (1 Cor 10:4), and the Word who 'tabernacled' among us (Jn 1:14).",
 church:"The Church reads the Exodus as a type of baptism (crossing the water, 1 Cor 10:1–2; the Easter Vigil Exsultet) and of the Eucharist (CCC 1334, 1340, 1363). The Ten Commandments are the summary of the natural law and remain binding (CCC 2056–2082). Exodus 3:14 is foundational for the metaphysics of God as subsistent being.",
 thomas:"Thomas uses Exodus 3:14 for the claim that in God essence and existence are identical (ST I q.13 a.11). He analyses the Old Law's moral, ceremonial and judicial precepts at length in ST I-II qq.98–105, explaining why the ceremonies foreshadowed Christ. Gregory of Nyssa's Life of Moses reads the ascent of Sinai as the soul's ascent to God.",
 hard:[["Did God make Pharaoh sin by hardening his heart?","The text alternates between 'Pharaoh hardened his heart' and 'the Lord hardened Pharaoh's heart'. Thomas explains that God does not cause sin, but withdraws grace that Pharaoh had refused, so that his own malice runs its course (ST I-II q.79 a.3)."],["Did the Exodus really happen?","There is no Egyptian record of the event, which is not surprising for a story of defeat; the details (Egyptian names, Semitic slaves in the Delta) fit an Egyptian setting. Scholars debate scale and date (13th or 15th century BC). The Church holds that God truly acted to free Israel; the text's main purpose is to proclaim that act."]],
 keys:[["Ex 3:14","'I AM WHO AM'"],["Ex 12","the Passover"],["Ex 14","the crossing of the sea"],["Ex 16","manna"],["Ex 19:5–6","a priestly kingdom and holy nation"],["Ex 20:1–17","the Ten Commandments"],["Ex 24:8","the blood of the covenant"],["Ex 32","the golden calf"],["Ex 34:6–7","God's self-description: merciful, gracious, patient"],["Ex 40:34","the glory fills the Tabernacle"]],
 author:"Traditionally Moses; final form reached over time (see Genesis). Scholarly dates for the events range from c. 1446 BC (from 1 Kgs 6:1) to c. 1250 BC (Ramesses II)."},

leviticus:{era:"exodus",when:"At Sinai, in the first month after the Tabernacle is raised",genre:"Priestly law and ritual",
 lede:"Leviticus is the manual of Israel's worship and holiness, written for the priests of the tribe of Levi. Its question is simple: how can a sinful people live with a holy God in their midst?",
 story:`<p>Leviticus has little narrative. God speaks to Moses from the Tabernacle and gives laws for five kinds of sacrifice (burnt, grain, peace, sin and guilt offerings). Aaron and his sons are ordained; two of them, Nadab and Abihu, die for offering 'strange fire'. Laws follow on clean and unclean animals, childbirth, skin diseases and bodily discharges.</p>
<p>At the centre stands the Day of Atonement (ch. 16), when the high priest enters the Holy of Holies with blood and a scapegoat carries the people's sins into the wilderness. The 'Holiness Code' (chs 17–26) applies holiness to daily life: sexual ethics, justice for the poor and the stranger, honest weights, the Sabbath, the feasts and the jubilee year. It contains the command 'thou shalt love thy friend as thyself' (19:18).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,7,"The sacrifices"],[8,10,"Ordination of the priests; Nadab and Abihu"],[11,15,"Clean and unclean"],[16,16,"The Day of Atonement"],[17,26,"The Holiness Code: holiness in daily life, feasts, sabbath and jubilee"],[27,27,"Vows and offerings"]],
 people:[["Aaron","The first high priest, ordained with his sons in ch. 8.","His mediation and entry into the Holy of Holies are the background for Christ's high priesthood (Heb 9)."],["Nadab and Abihu","Aaron's sons, struck down for offering unauthorised fire (10:1–2).","Worship is to be offered as God wills, not as we invent; reverence matters."]],
 themes:[["Holiness","'Be holy because I am holy' (11:44; 19:2), quoted in 1 Pet 1:16. Holiness means being set apart for God and reflecting his character."],["Sacrifice and atonement","Blood represents life given to God (17:11); sacrifice restores communion broken by sin."],["Purity","Clean/unclean laws taught Israel that life and death, wholeness and disorder matter to God; they are ritual categories, not moral ones."],["Justice and neighbour-love","Leaving gleanings for the poor (19:9–10), loving the stranger (19:34), and the jubilee's release of debts (ch. 25)."]],
 christ:"Hebrews reads Leviticus as a 'shadow of the good things to come' (Heb 10:1): Christ is high priest and victim, entering the true sanctuary 'by his own blood' once for all (Heb 9:11–12). Jesus touches lepers and the woman with a haemorrhage, reversing the flow of impurity: his holiness heals rather than being defiled. He names Lev 19:18 the second great commandment (Mk 12:31).",
 church:"Leviticus explains the language of sacrifice, priesthood and atonement used for the Mass (CCC 1366–1367: the one sacrifice of Christ re-presented). The ceremonial laws are fulfilled and no longer binding (Council of Florence, Cantate Domino, 1442); the Old Law as a whole is 'a preparation for the Gospel' (CCC 1964), while the moral content, summed up in love of neighbour, remains. Jubilee years in the Church draw on Leviticus 25.",
 thomas:"Thomas gives one of the most detailed medieval explanations of the ceremonial law: its literal reasons (to keep Israel from idolatry, to teach reverence) and figurative reasons (to foreshadow Christ) (ST I-II qq.101–103). He holds that observing these ceremonies after Christ's Passion would be a mortal sin, since it would profess that Christ had not yet come (q.103 a.4).",
 hard:[["Why should Christians care about rules on mould and skin disease?","They are not moral rules for us, but they shaped Israel's imagination of holiness and wholeness. Knowing them makes sense of Jesus touching lepers and of the Gospel's language of cleansing."],["Do Christians pick and choose from Leviticus?","No: the Church distinguishes moral precepts (binding because they express natural law, e.g. 18–20 on sexual ethics and justice) from ceremonial and judicial precepts (fulfilled in Christ). This distinction is ancient and explained at length by Thomas."]],
 keys:[["Lev 11:44; 19:2","'Be holy'"],["Lev 16","the Day of Atonement"],["Lev 17:11","the life is in the blood"],["Lev 19:18","love thy neighbour as thyself"],["Lev 23","the feasts"],["Lev 25","sabbath and jubilee years"]],
 author:"Traditionally Moses; scholars assign it to the priestly tradition, finalised in or after the exile."},

numbers:{era:"wilderness",when:"From Sinai to the plains of Moab, about 39 years",genre:"Narrative, census lists and law",
 lede:"Numbers takes its name from the two censuses that frame it. Between them a whole generation is lost in the desert through unbelief, and a new one is formed to enter the land.",
 story:`<p>Israel is counted and arranged around the Tabernacle and sets out from Sinai. The journey is marked by complaining: about food, about Moses' leadership (even from Aaron and Miriam). At Kadesh, twelve spies scout Canaan; ten report that the inhabitants are giants, and the people refuse to go up. God sentences that generation to wander for forty years until it dies out; only Joshua and Caleb, who trusted, will enter.</p>
<p>Korah's rebellion against the priesthood ends in judgment; Aaron's rod blossoms. Moses strikes the rock in anger and is barred from the land. When serpents bite the people, God tells Moses to lift a bronze serpent on a pole; whoever looks lives. The pagan seer Balaam, hired to curse Israel, can only bless it and foresees 'a star out of Jacob' (24:17). A second census counts the new generation, and Joshua is appointed Moses' successor.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,10,"At Sinai: census, camp, departure"],[11,14,"Complaints and the refusal at Kadesh"],[15,20,"Laws, Korah's rebellion, Aaron's rod, Moses' failure"],[21,25,"Toward Moab: the bronze serpent, Balaam, Baal-peor"],[26,36,"The new generation: census, inheritance, preparations"]],
 people:[["Moses","Burdened leader and intercessor, 'a man exceeding meek above all men' (12:3), yet excluded from the land after striking the rock.","Even great servants of God are accountable; mercy and justice meet in his story."],["Caleb and Joshua","The two faithful spies who urge the people to go up and take the land (13:31; 14:6–9).","Faith sees God's power where others see giants."],["Korah","A Levite who leads a revolt against Moses and Aaron's priesthood (ch. 16).","Jude 11 cites him as a type of those who rebel against legitimate authority in the Church."],["Balaam","A foreign prophet hired by Balak of Moab to curse Israel; his donkey sees the angel he cannot (ch. 22).","God's word cannot be bought; his 'star out of Jacob' oracle was read as messianic."],["Phinehas","Aaron's grandson, zealous for the Lord at Baal-peor (ch. 25).","Given 'a covenant of an everlasting priesthood' (25:13)."]],
 themes:[["Faith and unbelief","The refusal at Kadesh is the paradigm of unbelief (Heb 3:7–4:11; Ps 94 [95])."],["Providence in the desert","Daily manna, cloud and fire, water from the rock."],["Holiness of the camp","God dwells at the centre; the tribes are arranged around him."],["Leadership and rebellion","Grumbling against God's chosen leaders is grumbling against God."]],
 christ:"Jesus applies the bronze serpent to himself: 'as Moses lifted up the serpent in the desert, so must the Son of man be lifted up' (Jn 3:14). The water from the rock is Christ (1 Cor 10:4), and the 'star out of Jacob' was linked by the Fathers with the star of the Magi (Mt 2:2).",
 church:"Paul reads the wilderness generation as a warning for the baptised: they all ate spiritual food and drank spiritual drink, yet most fell (1 Cor 10:1–12); grace received can be lost through sin. The bronze serpent shows that images are not idols when they point to God (CCC 2130). The priestly blessing (6:24–26) is still used in the liturgy.",
 thomas:"Thomas cites the bronze serpent as a figure of Christ's Passion in his commentary on John 3 and discusses Moses' sin at the rock as a fault of doubt or impatience. Origen's Homilies on Numbers read the journey as the stages of the soul's progress to God.",
 hard:[["Why such severe punishments?","Numbers presents the holiness of God's presence among a covenant people; rebellion against it is deadly, as in the parallel stories of Ananias and Sapphira in Acts 5. The punishments are paired throughout with intercession and mercy: the people are not destroyed, and their children enter the land."]],
 keys:[["Num 6:24–26","the priestly blessing"],["Num 13–14","the spies and the refusal"],["Num 20:1–13","water from the rock; Moses' failure"],["Num 21:8–9","the bronze serpent"],["Num 24:17","a star out of Jacob"]],
 author:"Traditionally Moses; final form, like the rest of the Pentateuch, reached later."},

deuteronomy:{era:"wilderness",when:"The last month of Moses' life, on the plains of Moab",genre:"Covenant sermons and law",
 lede:"Deuteronomy ('second law') is Moses' farewell: three great speeches that retell the journey, restate the Law and renew the covenant before Israel enters the land. Jesus quotes it more than almost any other book.",
 story:`<p>Moses addresses the new generation. He recalls God's faithfulness from Horeb (Sinai) to Moab and the people's failures. He repeats the Ten Commandments and gives the Shema: 'Hear, O Israel: The Lord our God is one Lord. Thou shalt love the Lord thy God with thy whole heart' (6:4–5). He teaches that God chose Israel out of love, not because of its greatness (7:7–8).</p>
<p>The central law code (chs 12–26) covers worship at one chosen place, care for the poor, the widow and the orphan, just courts, kingship and prophecy, including the promise of a future prophet like Moses (18:15). Blessings and curses follow; Moses sets before Israel 'life and death… choose therefore life' (30:19). He commissions Joshua, sings a final song, blesses the tribes, and dies on Mount Nebo after seeing the land.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,4,"First speech: remembering the journey"],[5,11,"Second speech: the Ten Commandments and the Shema"],[12,26,"The Deuteronomic law code"],[27,30,"Blessings, curses and covenant renewal"],[31,34,"Joshua commissioned; Moses' song, blessing and death"]],
 people:[["Moses","Now an old man of 120, preaching his last words; he sees the land from Nebo and dies, buried by God in an unknown grave (34:6).","His prophetic office points to the prophet 'like me' whom God will raise (18:15), fulfilled in Christ (Acts 3:22)."],["Joshua","Moses' aide, now commissioned to lead the people into the land (ch. 31).","Leadership passes by the laying on of hands (34:9), a pattern later echoed in ordination."]],
 themes:[["Love and obedience","God's love comes first; obedience is the response of love (7:7–9; 10:12)."],["One God, one place of worship","Monotheism and the centralisation of worship."],["Remember","Israel must not forget what God has done; forgetfulness leads to idolatry (8:11–18)."],["Choose life","The two ways of blessing and curse (ch. 30)."],["Justice for the vulnerable","Repeated care for the stranger, the fatherless and the widow (10:18; 24:17–22)."]],
 christ:"When tempted in the desert, Jesus answers Satan three times from Deuteronomy (8:3; 6:16; 6:13): he succeeds where Israel failed in its forty years. He names Deut 6:5 the first commandment (Mk 12:29–30). He is the prophet like Moses who speaks God's words face to face. Paul applies 'the word is nigh thee' (30:14) to the Gospel of faith (Rom 10:6–8).",
 church:"The Shema grounds the first commandment and the Church's teaching on the one God (CCC 201, 228, 2083). Deuteronomy's covenant shape (history, law, blessing and curse, renewal) is the pattern of the liturgy of the Word and the renewal of baptismal promises. Its insistence on teaching the faith to children (6:6–7) underlies the parents' role as first catechists (CCC 2223).",
 thomas:"Thomas uses Deuteronomy throughout the treatise on law, especially on judicial precepts (ST I-II q.105), and on the love of God with the whole heart (II-II q.44 aa.4–5). Augustine's distinction between the Law as letter and as spirit draws on Deut 30.",
 hard:[["What about the commands to destroy the Canaanites (ch. 7, 20)?","See Joshua. The Pontifical Biblical Commission's 2014 document The Inspiration and Truth of Sacred Scripture reads these texts within their ancient context and literary form, as uncompromising rejection of idolatry, and insists they must be read in the light of Christ, who forbids such violence."]],
 keys:[["Deut 6:4–9","the Shema"],["Deut 7:7–8","chosen out of love"],["Deut 8:3","not by bread alone"],["Deut 18:15","a prophet like Moses"],["Deut 30:15–20","choose life"],["Deut 34","the death of Moses"]],
 author:"Presented as Moses' words. Many scholars link its present form to the reform of King Josiah (622 BC), when 'the book of the law' was found in the Temple (2 Kgs 22), with later additions."}
});

// Book introductions: historical books
Object.assign(BOOK_INTRO,{
joshua:{era:"judges",when:"Israel enters and settles Canaan under Joshua",genre:"Narrative history",
 lede:"Joshua tells how God kept his promise of the land. Israel crosses the Jordan, takes Jericho, and the land is divided among the twelve tribes. It ends with a solemn choice: 'as for me and my house we will serve the Lord' (24:15).",
 story:`<p>After Moses' death God commissions Joshua: 'Take courage, and be strong' (1:6). Two spies are sheltered in Jericho by Rahab, a prostitute who confesses faith in Israel's God. The Jordan stops flowing as the priests carry the Ark across, and Israel sets up twelve memorial stones. Jericho's walls fall after seven days of procession with the Ark. Achan's theft of devoted goods brings defeat at Ai until it is confessed and judged.</p>
<p>Campaigns in the south and north follow, including the day the sun 'stood still' at Gibeon. Chapters 13–21 record the allotment of the land and the cities of refuge and of the Levites. Before he dies, Joshua gathers the tribes at Shechem, retells their history, and renews the covenant.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,5,"Preparation and crossing the Jordan"],[6,12,"The conquest: Jericho, Ai, Gibeon, the southern and northern campaigns"],[13,21,"Dividing the land"],[22,24,"Farewells and covenant renewal at Shechem"]],
 people:[["Joshua","Moses' aide and successor, son of Nun of Ephraim; one of the two faithful spies.","His name (Yehoshua, 'the Lord saves') is the same as Jesus'; the Fathers saw him leading God's people into the promised rest as a type of Christ (Heb 4:8)."],["Rahab","A Canaanite woman of Jericho who hides the spies and is spared with her family.","Commended for faith (Heb 11:31) and works (Jas 2:25), she appears in Jesus' genealogy (Mt 1:5): outsiders are brought into the covenant by faith."],["Achan","An Israelite who keeps plunder 'devoted' to God, bringing defeat on all Israel (ch. 7).","Sin is never purely private; it harms the whole community."],["Caleb","The faithful spy, given Hebron at 85 (ch. 14).","Faithfulness rewarded."]],
 themes:[["God keeps promises","'Not so much as one word, which he promised to perform unto them, was made void' (21:43)."],["Courage from God's presence","'I will be with thee' (1:5)."],["Holy war and its limits","The conquest is presented as God's judgment on Canaanite idolatry; obedience, not military strength, wins."],["Covenant choice","Every generation must choose whom to serve (ch. 24)."]],
 christ:"Joshua and Jesus share one name. As Joshua led Israel across the Jordan into the land, Jesus, baptised in the Jordan, leads his people into the true rest (Heb 4). Rahab's scarlet cord (2:18) was read by Clement of Rome and the Fathers as a sign of salvation through Christ's blood.",
 church:"The crossing of the Jordan is a traditional type of baptism. Joshua's covenant renewal anticipates the renewal of baptismal promises. The book raises the question of violence in Scripture, which the Church answers by reading the Old Testament in the light of Christ and its literary genre (Dei Verbum 12, 15; Verbum Domini 42).",
 thomas:"Origen's Homilies on Joshua read the conquest spiritually as the Christian's warfare against vices. Augustine and Thomas treat the miracle at Gibeon as described according to appearances (Thomas, ST I q.70 a.1 ad 3 on Scripture speaking 'as it appears to the senses').",
 hard:[["How can God command the destruction of whole cities?","This is one of the hardest questions in Scripture. Catholic interpreters note that: (1) the 'ban' language is part of an ancient Near Eastern genre of war reports that often used hyperbole (Judges shows the Canaanites still present); (2) the texts present God's judgment on cultures practising child sacrifice (Deut 12:31); (3) God alone is Lord of life, but this does not license human imitation; and (4) Benedict XVI notes that such texts belong to a stage of revelation purified by Christ (Verbum Domini 42). None of this makes the texts comfortable; it places them."],["Did the sun really stand still?","The text quotes an older poem (the Book of Jashar, 10:13). Whether it describes a miracle of light or uses poetic language, Scripture describes nature as it appears to observers, not in astronomical terms."]],
 keys:[["Josh 1:5–9","be strong and courageous"],["Josh 2","Rahab"],["Josh 3–4","crossing the Jordan"],["Josh 6","Jericho"],["Josh 24:15","choose whom you will serve"]],
 author:"Anonymous; Jewish tradition credits Joshua. Most scholars see it as part of a 'Deuteronomistic History' (Deuteronomy to Kings) compiled around Josiah's reign and completed in the exile."},

judges:{era:"judges",when:"From Joshua's death to the rise of Samuel, c. 1200–1050 BC",genre:"Narrative history",
 lede:"Judges shows what happens when a people forgets God: the same cycle repeats and worsens. It ends in moral chaos, preparing the reader to long for a faithful king.",
 story:`<p>The tribes fail to drive out the Canaanites and begin to worship their gods. The book's framework repeats: Israel does evil, God hands them to oppressors, they cry out, God raises a 'judge' (a charismatic deliverer), the land has peace, and then the cycle begins again.</p>
<p>Twelve judges appear. Ehud kills a Moabite king; Deborah, prophetess and judge, with Barak defeats Sisera, whom Jael kills; Gideon tears down Baal's altar and routs Midian with 300 men; Jephthah makes a rash vow that costs his daughter; Samson, a Nazirite of enormous strength, is undone by his passions and Delilah but dies destroying the Philistine temple. The last chapters (17–21) tell of private idolatry, the tribe of Dan's migration, and a horrific crime at Gibeah that leads to civil war.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,2,"Incomplete conquest and the cycle explained"],[3,5,"Othniel, Ehud, Shamgar, Deborah and Barak"],[6,9,"Gideon and Abimelech"],[10,12,"Tola, Jair, Jephthah and minor judges"],[13,16,"Samson"],[17,21,"Two appendices: idolatry and civil war"]],
 people:[["Deborah","A prophetess who judged Israel under a palm tree and summoned Barak to battle (chs 4–5).","One of Scripture's great women leaders; her song is among the oldest poems in the Bible."],["Gideon","A fearful farmer called 'most valiant of men' by an angel, who tests God with a fleece and defeats Midian with 300 men (chs 6–8).","God's strength in weakness; but he later makes an ephod that becomes an idol."],["Jephthah","A rejected son who becomes a judge and vows to sacrifice whatever first greets him (ch. 11).","A warning about rash vows: Thomas says a vow whose result would be evil must not be kept (ST II-II q.88 a.2 ad 2)."],["Samson","A Nazirite from birth with extraordinary strength, but undisciplined in his desires (chs 13–16).","Gifts without virtue are squandered; yet God still uses him, and he is named among the heroes of faith (Heb 11:32)."]],
 themes:[["The cycle of sin","Sin, judgment, repentance, deliverance, relapse."],["Leadership","Each judge is more flawed than the last."],["Need for a king","'In those days there was no king in Israel: but every one did that which seemed right to himself' (21:24)."],["God's faithfulness despite Israel's","God keeps raising deliverers."]],
 christ:"The judges are partial saviours raised by the Spirit; Christ is the final deliverer and the true king the book longs for. Samson's birth announced by an angel to a barren woman foreshadows the annunciations of John and Jesus, and his death, by which he defeated more enemies than in life (16:30), was read by Augustine as a figure of the Cross.",
 church:"Judges is a mirror for any community that drifts from God: moral relativism ('right in his own eyes') leads to violence against the weak (ch. 19). It shows the need for legitimate authority and for virtue in leaders.",
 thomas:"Thomas holds that Jephthah should not have kept his vow (ST II-II q.88 a.2 ad 2), and, quoting Augustine, that Samson's death can be excused only because the Holy Spirit secretly commanded it (II-II q.64 a.5 ad 4).",
 hard:[["Does the Bible approve of Jephthah sacrificing his daughter or the crime at Gibeah?","No. Judges reports these events to show how far Israel had fallen. The narrator's verdict is the refrain about doing what is right in one's own eyes. Narrative description is not moral endorsement."]],
 keys:[["Judg 2:11–19","the cycle explained"],["Judg 5","the Song of Deborah"],["Judg 6–7","Gideon"],["Judg 13–16","Samson"],["Judg 21:24","every man did what seemed right to himself"]],
 author:"Anonymous; Jewish tradition credits Samuel. Part of the Deuteronomistic History, using older sources (the Song of Deborah may date to the 12th century BC)."},

ruth:{era:"judges",when:"'In the days of the judges'",genre:"Short story",
 lede:"Ruth is a four-chapter story of love, loyalty and providence. A Moabite widow who refuses to abandon her mother-in-law becomes the great-grandmother of King David and an ancestor of Jesus.",
 story:`<p>Famine drives Elimelech, his wife Naomi and their two sons from Bethlehem to Moab. The men die. Naomi decides to return home and urges her Moabite daughters-in-law to stay. Orpah goes back; Ruth clings to her: 'whither thou shalt go, I will go… thy people shall be my people, and thy God my God' (1:16).</p>
<p>In Bethlehem Ruth gleans barley in the field of Boaz, a wealthy relative, who treats her with kindness. Following Naomi's plan, Ruth asks Boaz at night on the threshing floor to act as kinsman-redeemer. Boaz settles the matter honourably at the city gate, marries Ruth, and their son Obed becomes the grandfather of David. Naomi, who called herself 'Mara' (bitter), holds the child in her arms.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"Loss in Moab and Ruth's loyalty"],[2,2,"Gleaning in Boaz's field"],[3,3,"The threshing floor"],[4,4,"Redemption at the gate; the line of David"]],
 people:[["Ruth","A Moabite widow who chooses Israel's God and people out of love for Naomi.","A Gentile in the line of the Messiah (Mt 1:5); her hesed (loyal love) mirrors God's."],["Naomi","An Israelite widow, emptied by loss, who is restored.","Honest lament ('call me Mara') and the hidden providence of God."],["Boaz","A just and generous landowner who becomes Ruth's go'el, kinsman-redeemer.","A type of Christ the Redeemer, who takes a foreign bride and restores her inheritance."]],
 themes:[["Hesed","Loyal, covenant love beyond obligation, shown by Ruth, Boaz and God."],["Providence","God is barely mentioned, yet his hand is in every 'chance' (2:3)."],["The redeemer","The go'el who buys back land and raises up a family."],["Inclusion","A Moabite (Deut 23:3) is welcomed through faith."]],
 christ:"Boaz the redeemer prefigures Christ, who redeems a bride from among the nations. The story takes place in Bethlehem ('house of bread') at harvest, where the Bread of Life will be born of David's line.",
 church:"The Catechism cites Ruth with Tobit as 'moving witness to an elevated sense of marriage and to the fidelity and tenderness of spouses' (CCC 1611). Ruth's choice ('thy God my God') is a model of conversion and of the universality of salvation.",
 thomas:"Ambrose and Jerome comment on Ruth's place in Christ's genealogy as a sign that the Gentiles are called.",
 hard:[["What happens on the threshing floor?","Some read sexual innuendo; the text stresses Boaz's integrity and that the matter is settled lawfully before marriage. Ruth asks Boaz to 'spread his covering' over her (3:9), a request for marriage and protection (compare Ezek 16:8)."]],
 keys:[["Ruth 1:16–17","'thy people shall be my people'"],["Ruth 2:12","under whose wings thou art come"],["Ruth 4:13–22","Obed and the line of David"]],
 author:"Anonymous; Jewish tradition credits Samuel. Dates proposed range from the monarchy to after the exile."},

"1-samuel":{era:"kingdom",when:"From Samuel's birth to Saul's death, c. 1100–1010 BC",genre:"Narrative history (Douay-Rheims: 1 Kings)",
 lede:"1 Samuel tells how Israel moved from judges to kings: the prophet Samuel, the tragic first king Saul, and the rise of David, 'a man according to God's own heart'.",
 story:`<p>Hannah, childless and mocked, prays in the sanctuary at Shiloh; God gives her Samuel, whom she dedicates to the Lord, singing a hymn that Mary will echo (ch. 2). The boy Samuel hears God's call at night: 'Speak, Lord, for thy servant heareth' (3:10). The corrupt priests, Eli's sons, lose the Ark to the Philistines, but the Ark humbles the god Dagon and returns.</p>
<p>The people demand a king 'like all the nations'. Samuel warns them, then anoints Saul, a tall Benjaminite. Saul begins well but disobeys, offering sacrifice himself and sparing what was devoted to destruction: 'obedience is better than sacrifices' (15:22). God sends Samuel to anoint the youngest son of Jesse, David. David kills Goliath, wins Jonathan's friendship and the people's love, and Saul, consumed by jealousy, hunts him. Twice David spares Saul's life. The book ends with Saul consulting a medium and dying with Jonathan on Mount Gilboa.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,7,"Samuel: birth, call, the Ark"],[8,15,"Saul made king and rejected"],[16,20,"David anointed; Goliath; Jonathan"],[21,31,"David the fugitive; Saul's end"]],
 people:[["Hannah","Elkanah's wife who pours out her soul in prayer and dedicates her son.","Her song (2:1–10) is the model for Mary's Magnificat (Lk 1:46–55); CCC 2578."],["Samuel","Prophet, priest and last judge, who anoints both kings.","The bridge from judges to monarchy; the prophet who listens."],["Saul","Israel's first king, gifted but impulsive and disobedient, tormented by jealousy.","A study in how insecurity and disobedience corrode a vocation."],["David","Shepherd, musician, warrior; anointed while still a boy.","God looks on the heart (16:7); the beginning of the messianic line."],["Jonathan","Saul's son, who loves David 'as his own soul' and gives up his claim to the throne.","Scripture's great portrait of friendship (Aristotle's and Thomas's 'friendship of virtue')."],["Goliath","The Philistine champion killed by David's sling.","God saves 'not with sword and spear' (17:47)."]],
 themes:[["God looks on the heart","'Man seeth those things that appear, but the Lord beholdeth the heart' (16:7)."],["Obedience over ritual","Saul's sacrifice without obedience is rejected (15:22)."],["Kingship under God","The king must himself obey God; human kingship is a concession and a promise."],["Friendship","David and Jonathan."]],
 christ:"Hannah's song announces that the Lord 'shall give empire to his king, and shall exalt the horn of his Christ' (2:10), the first use of 'anointed' (messiah) for a king. David, born in Bethlehem, a shepherd anointed by the Spirit, persecuted before he reigns, is the clearest Old Testament type of Christ.",
 church:"Samuel's call is a classic text for vocation and prayerful listening. Hannah's prayer is a model of petition (CCC 2578). Saul's consultation of the medium of Endor is cited against divination and spiritism (CCC 2116–2117).",
 thomas:"Thomas discusses whether Samuel truly appeared at Endor. In one place he gives Augustine's two possibilities, a real appearance by God's hidden dispensation or a demonic phantom (ST II-II q.95 a.4 ad 2); in another he says that 'by God's will the soul itself of Samuel' foretold the outcome (q.174 a.5 ad 4).",
 hard:[["Why did God reject Saul for seemingly small sins?","Saul's sins reveal a pattern: impatience, self-justification and partial obedience (15:20–21). The king was meant to model covenant obedience; Saul treated God's word as negotiable."],["Was it really Samuel at Endor?","The text says the woman saw Samuel and that he prophesied truly (ch. 28). Sirach 46:23 affirms it. Many Fathers held God permitted it as judgment; it does not validate necromancy, which Scripture forbids (Deut 18:10–12)."]],
 keys:[["1 Sam 2:1–10","the Song of Hannah"],["1 Sam 3","'Speak, Lord'"],["1 Sam 8","the demand for a king"],["1 Sam 15:22","obedience better than sacrifice"],["1 Sam 16:7","God sees the heart"],["1 Sam 17","David and Goliath"],["1 Sam 18:1–4","David and Jonathan"]],
 author:"Anonymous. Jewish tradition credits Samuel, Nathan and Gad (1 Chr 29:29). Scholars see it as part of the Deuteronomistic History using older court sources. 1–2 Samuel were one book; the Septuagint and Vulgate count them as 1–2 Kings."},

"2-samuel":{era:"kingdom",when:"David's reign, c. 1010–970 BC",genre:"Narrative history (Douay-Rheims: 2 Kings)",
 lede:"2 Samuel is the story of David as king: his triumphs, God's everlasting covenant with him, his terrible sin with Bathsheba, his repentance, and the tragedies that follow in his family.",
 story:`<p>David mourns Saul and Jonathan, is anointed king over Judah at Hebron and then over all Israel. He captures Jerusalem, makes it his capital and brings up the Ark, dancing before it. When he plans to build God a house, the prophet Nathan brings God's promise instead: God will build David a 'house', a dynasty whose throne will stand for ever (ch. 7).</p>
<p>At the height of his power David commits adultery with Bathsheba and arranges the death of her husband Uriah. Nathan confronts him with a parable; David confesses, 'I have sinned against the Lord' (12:13). He is forgiven, but the sword does not leave his house: his son Amnon rapes his half-sister Tamar, Absalom kills Amnon and later leads a rebellion that drives David from Jerusalem. Absalom dies, and David weeps, 'my son Absalom, would to God that I might die for thee' (18:33). The book closes with appendices, including David's song and the census that brings plague.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,5,"David becomes king of Judah, then all Israel; Jerusalem"],[6,7,"The Ark and the Davidic covenant"],[8,10,"Victories and kindness to Mephibosheth"],[11,12,"Bathsheba, Uriah and Nathan"],[13,20,"Family tragedy and Absalom's rebellion"],[21,24,"Appendices: songs, heroes, the census"]],
 people:[["David","King, poet and sinner who repents. He unites the nation and receives the promise of an eternal kingdom.","The covenant of ch. 7 is the foundation of messianic hope (CCC 709); his repentance is the model of contrition (Ps 50 [51])."],["Nathan","The court prophet who announces the covenant and confronts David's sin.","Truth spoken to power with wisdom (the parable of the ewe lamb)."],["Bathsheba","Wife of Uriah, taken by David, later mother of Solomon.","Named in Jesus' genealogy as 'her that had been the wife of Urias' (Mt 1:6)."],["Uriah","A loyal Hittite soldier more upright than the king.","Innocence betrayed; his integrity exposes David's sin."],["Absalom","David's charming son who steals the people's hearts and rebels.","Consequences of sin and failed fatherhood."],["Mephibosheth","Jonathan's lame son, received at David's table (ch. 9).","Covenant kindness (hesed) for Jonathan's sake."]],
 themes:[["The Davidic covenant","God's unconditional promise of a lasting dynasty (7:12–16)."],["Sin and repentance","Even God's anointed falls; forgiveness is real, consequences remain."],["Worship","The Ark brought to Zion with joy."],["Family and power","Unchecked desire destroys a household."]],
 christ:"God's promise, 'I will be to him a father, and he shall be to me a son' (7:14), is applied to Christ (Heb 1:5). The angel tells Mary that her son will receive 'the throne of David his father… and of his kingdom there shall be no end' (Lk 1:32–33). David leaving Jerusalem across the Kidron and up the Mount of Olives, weeping (15:23, 30), is retraced by Jesus on the night of his betrayal.",
 church:"The covenant with David underlies the title 'Son of David' and the Church's teaching on Christ's kingship (CCC 436, 439, 709). David's repentance is a model for the sacrament of penance; Psalm 50 (51) is the Church's great penitential psalm. David's dancing before the Ark is often cited for joyful worship.",
 thomas:"Thomas uses David's sin and repentance as an example in the treatise on penance (ST III q.84ff.) and on how a just man can fall gravely. Ambrose wrote an Apology of the Prophet David addressing his sin.",
 keys:[["2 Sam 6","the Ark brought to Jerusalem"],["2 Sam 7:12–16","the Davidic covenant"],["2 Sam 11–12","Bathsheba and Nathan"],["2 Sam 18:33","'Absalom my son'"],["2 Sam 22","David's song (= Ps 17 [18])"]],
 author:"See 1 Samuel."},

"1-kings":{era:"kingdom",when:"Solomon to Ahaziah of Israel, c. 970–850 BC",genre:"Narrative history (Douay-Rheims: 3 Kings)",
 lede:"1 Kings moves from glory to division. Solomon builds the Temple and is famed for wisdom, but idolatry splits the kingdom. In the north the prophet Elijah confronts King Ahab and Queen Jezebel.",
 story:`<p>The aged David names Solomon king. Solomon asks God for 'an understanding heart' and receives wisdom, wealth and honour; his judgment between two mothers becomes legendary. He builds the Temple, and at its dedication God's glory fills it. The Queen of Sheba visits. But Solomon's many foreign wives turn his heart to other gods.</p>
<p>After his death, his son Rehoboam's harshness causes the ten northern tribes to break away under Jeroboam, who sets up golden calves at Bethel and Dan. A series of kings follows, judged by their faithfulness. Under Ahab and Jezebel, Baal worship is promoted. Elijah announces drought, is fed by ravens and by a widow of Zarephath whose son he raises, defeats the prophets of Baal on Mount Carmel, flees in despair to Horeb, and meets God in 'a whistling of a gentle air' (19:12). He calls Elisha as his successor. Ahab's theft of Naboth's vineyard brings judgment.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,2,"Solomon becomes king"],[3,4,"Solomon's wisdom"],[5,8,"The Temple built and dedicated"],[9,11,"Solomon's glory and fall"],[12,16,"The kingdom divided; kings of Israel and Judah"],[17,19,"Elijah: drought, Carmel, Horeb"],[20,22,"Ahab, Naboth's vineyard, Ahab's death"]],
 people:[["Solomon","David's son by Bathsheba, the wise king who builds the Temple but ends in idolatry.","Wisdom is a gift that must be kept by fidelity; Jesus is 'more than Solomon' (Mt 12:42)."],["Jeroboam","First king of the northern kingdom, who sets up rival shrines with golden calves.","His 'sin which made Israel to sin' becomes the refrain for every northern king."],["Ahab and Jezebel","King of Israel and his Phoenician queen who promote Baal worship and murder Naboth.","Power used against God's law and the poor."],["Elijah","The fiery prophet from Tishbe who stands alone against Baal.","With Moses he appears at the Transfiguration; John the Baptist comes 'in the spirit and power of Elias' (Lk 1:17); CCC 2582–2583."],["The widow of Zarephath","A Gentile widow who shares her last bread with Elijah.","Jesus cites her (Lk 4:25–26) to show God's care for outsiders."]],
 themes:[["Temple","God's dwelling among his people (8:27: 'heaven… cannot contain thee, how much less this house')."],["Wisdom and its loss","Gifts can be squandered."],["Idolatry divides","Spiritual infidelity leads to political collapse."],["The prophet versus the king","God's word judges rulers."]],
 christ:"Christ is greater than Solomon in wisdom and greater than the Temple (Mt 12:6, 42); his body is the true Temple (Jn 2:21). Elijah's raising of the widow's son and multiplying of food foreshadow Jesus' miracles (Lk 7:11–17).",
 church:"Solomon's prayer at the dedication (ch. 8) is a model for the Church's prayer in its churches (CCC 2580). Elijah is a model of contemplative and prophetic prayer, venerated by the Carmelites as their spiritual father (CCC 2582–2583). Solomon's request for wisdom is the model of asking for what matters most.",
 thomas:"Augustine and later tradition debated whether Solomon was saved after his fall, and Scripture leaves the question open. The 'gentle air' at Horeb was read by the Fathers as God's presence in peace rather than force.",
 keys:[["1 Kgs 3:5–14","Solomon's prayer for wisdom"],["1 Kgs 8","dedication of the Temple"],["1 Kgs 11","Solomon's heart turned away"],["1 Kgs 12","the kingdom divided"],["1 Kgs 18","Elijah on Carmel"],["1 Kgs 19:11–13","the gentle breeze at Horeb"]],
 author:"Anonymous; Jewish tradition credits Jeremiah. Part of the Deuteronomistic History, finalised in the exile (after 561 BC, the last event in 2 Kings)."},

"2-kings":{era:"divided",when:"c. 850 BC to the fall of Jerusalem (587/586 BC) and beyond",genre:"Narrative history (Douay-Rheims: 4 Kings)",
 lede:"2 Kings tells the long decline of both kingdoms despite the prophets' warnings: Israel falls to Assyria in 722 BC, Judah to Babylon in 587 BC. Yet the book ends with a sliver of hope for David's line.",
 story:`<p>Elijah is taken up to heaven in a whirlwind and Elisha receives a double portion of his spirit. Elisha's many miracles include purifying water, multiplying oil, raising a Shunammite woman's son and healing Naaman the Syrian of leprosy in the Jordan. Jehu wipes out Ahab's house and Baal worship, but the northern kings continue in Jeroboam's sin. Assyria conquers Samaria in 722 BC and deports the northern tribes (ch. 17), explaining the fall as covenant judgment.</p>
<p>Judah continues with good and bad kings. Hezekiah trusts God when Assyria besieges Jerusalem, and the city is spared (chs 18–19). His son Manasseh is the worst of all. Josiah finds the book of the Law in the Temple and leads a great reform (chs 22–23), but it is too late. Babylon deports Jehoiachin (597 BC) and finally destroys Jerusalem and the Temple (587/586 BC). In the last verses the exiled king Jehoiachin is released from prison and eats at the Babylonian king's table.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,8,"Elisha's ministry"],[9,10,"Jehu's revolution"],[11,17,"Kings of Israel and Judah; the fall of Samaria"],[18,21,"Hezekiah and Manasseh"],[22,23,"Josiah's reform"],[24,25,"The fall of Jerusalem and exile"]],
 people:[["Elisha","Elijah's successor, a prophet of compassion and miracles.","His healing of Naaman (ch. 5) is cited by Jesus (Lk 4:27) and read as a figure of baptism."],["Naaman","A Syrian general healed of leprosy after washing seven times in the Jordan.","Humility and faith; salvation for the Gentiles."],["Hezekiah","A reforming king of Judah who trusts God against Assyria.","Prayer in crisis (19:14–19); Isaiah's support."],["Josiah","Judah's last great reformer, who finds the Law and renews the covenant.","Scripture rediscovered renews a people."],["Nebuchadnezzar","King of Babylon who destroys Jerusalem.","The instrument of judgment; also a figure in Daniel."]],
 themes:[["Judgment for covenant unfaithfulness","Exile is the curse of Deuteronomy fulfilled."],["The prophetic word","Every prophecy comes true."],["God's patience","Centuries of warnings."],["Hope for David's line","Jehoiachin's release (25:27–30)."]],
 christ:"Elijah's ascension anticipates Christ's, and Elisha's double portion anticipates the Spirit given to the apostles. Elisha's feeding of a hundred men with twenty loaves, with some left over (4:42–44), foreshadows the multiplication of loaves.",
 church:"Naaman's washing is read in the liturgy as a type of baptism. The book shows that sin has social and historical consequences, and that the Church is sustained not by political success but by God's faithfulness to his promises.",
 thomas:"Thomas holds that Elijah was taken up into the 'atmospheric heaven', not into the heaven of the blessed, which only Christ's Passion opened (ST III q.49 a.5 ad 2).",
 keys:[["2 Kgs 2","Elijah taken up; Elisha's double portion"],["2 Kgs 5","Naaman healed"],["2 Kgs 17","why Samaria fell"],["2 Kgs 19","Hezekiah's prayer"],["2 Kgs 22–23","Josiah finds the Law"],["2 Kgs 25","the fall of Jerusalem"]],
 author:"See 1 Kings."},

"1-chronicles":{era:"return",when:"Adam to David, written after the exile (c. 400–300 BC)",genre:"Theological history (Douay-Rheims: 1 Paralipomenon)",
 lede:"Chronicles retells Israel's history for the community that returned from exile, focusing on David, the Temple and right worship. 1 Chronicles begins with Adam and ends with David preparing for the Temple.",
 story:`<p>Nine chapters of genealogies run from Adam to the returned exiles, showing that the restored community is still Israel, connected to the beginning. The narrative then focuses on David: Saul's death briefly, David's anointing, the capture of Jerusalem, and above all the bringing of the Ark to Zion with Levites singing and playing (chs 13–16).</p>
<p>God makes his covenant with David (ch. 17). David's wars are briefly told; his sin with Bathsheba is omitted. Instead the book dwells on David's preparations for the Temple: materials, the organisation of priests, Levites, singers and gatekeepers, and his charge to Solomon. It ends with David's great prayer of praise (29:10–19) and his death.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,9,"Genealogies from Adam to the return"],[10,12,"Saul's end and David's rise"],[13,16,"The Ark comes to Jerusalem"],[17,17,"God's covenant with David"],[18,21,"David's wars and the census"],[22,29,"Preparations for the Temple; David's last words"]],
 people:[["David","Presented almost wholly as founder of Israel's worship.","The ideal king is a worshipper."],["The Levites and singers","Asaph, Heman and Jeduthun lead praise.","Liturgical music as part of Israel's identity."],["Jabez","A man whose short prayer is answered (4:10).","God hears the humble."]],
 themes:[["Worship at the centre","The Temple, Levites and music."],["Continuity","Genealogies link the returned community to Adam and the patriarchs."],["Retribution and repentance","Faithfulness brings blessing; repentance brings mercy."],["All Israel","The unity of the twelve tribes."]],
 christ:"The genealogies anticipate Matthew's and Luke's. The emphasis on David's house and the Temple prepares for Christ, Son of David and true Temple.",
 church:"Chronicles shapes the Church's understanding of liturgy as a royal and priestly work, with ordered ministries and sacred music. David's prayer, 'all things are thine: and we have given thee what we received of thy hand' (29:14), is echoed in the offertory prayers of the Mass.",
 thomas:"The Greek name Paralipomena means 'things left out', reflecting the Fathers' view of it as a supplement to Kings. Jerome praised it as an 'epitome of the Old Testament'.",
 keys:[["1 Chr 16:8–36","David's psalm of thanksgiving"],["1 Chr 17","the covenant with David"],["1 Chr 29:10–19","David's prayer"]],
 author:"Anonymous 'Chronicler', probably a Levite in Jerusalem, 4th century BC; Jewish tradition credits Ezra."},

"2-chronicles":{era:"return",when:"Solomon to Cyrus's decree (538 BC), written after the exile",genre:"Theological history (Douay-Rheims: 2 Paralipomenon)",
 lede:"2 Chronicles follows the kings of Judah from Solomon's Temple to the exile and ends with Cyrus inviting the exiles to go home and rebuild. Its message: seek the Lord, and he will be found.",
 story:`<p>Solomon builds and dedicates the Temple; fire falls from heaven and glory fills the house (chs 1–9). After the division the book follows Judah only. Each king is judged by whether he sought the Lord: Asa, Jehoshaphat, Hezekiah and Josiah lead reforms and celebrate great Passovers; others fall into idolatry. Manasseh, the worst king, repents in captivity and is restored (ch. 33), a story told only here.</p>
<p>The end comes because the people 'mocked the messengers of God' (36:16): Jerusalem is burned and the people exiled 'until the land enjoyed her sabbaths' (36:21). The final verses give Cyrus's decree allowing the return, ending the Hebrew Bible in hope.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,9,"Solomon and the Temple"],[10,20,"The division; Rehoboam to Jehoshaphat"],[21,28,"From Jehoram to Ahaz"],[29,32,"Hezekiah's reform"],[33,35,"Manasseh's repentance; Josiah"],[36,36,"Fall and Cyrus's decree"]],
 people:[["Hezekiah","Restores the Temple and celebrates Passover with all Israel.","Reform begins with worship."],["Manasseh","Judah's most wicked king, who repents in chains and is restored.","No sinner is beyond mercy (the apocryphal Prayer of Manasseh grew from this)."],["Josiah","Celebrates the greatest Passover since Samuel.","Fidelity to the word."],["Cyrus","The Persian king whose decree ends the book.","God works through pagan rulers (Isa 45:1)."]],
 themes:[["Seek the Lord","'If my people… shall seek my face… then will I hear from heaven' (7:14)."],["Immediate retribution","Faithfulness and sin bear fruit in each generation."],["Repentance","Even Manasseh is restored."],["Temple and Passover","Worship as the heart of national renewal."]],
 christ:"The Temple filled with glory points to the Word made flesh in whom glory dwells (Jn 1:14). Cyrus, the 'anointed' liberator, is a distant figure of the true liberator.",
 church:"2 Chronicles 7:14 is often used in prayers for national renewal. Manasseh's repentance illustrates the power of contrition.",
 thomas:"Thomas teaches that every sin, however grave, can be taken away by true penance in this life (ST III q.86 a.1); Manasseh is the Old Testament's great example.",
 keys:[["2 Chr 7:14","if my people humble themselves"],["2 Chr 20","Jehoshaphat: 'the battle is not yours'"],["2 Chr 33:10–13","Manasseh repents"],["2 Chr 36:22–23","Cyrus's decree"]],
 author:"See 1 Chronicles."},

ezra:{era:"return",when:"538–c. 458 BC",genre:"History with documents (Douay-Rheims: 1 Esdras)",
 lede:"Ezra tells of two returns from Babylon: the first under Zerubbabel to rebuild the Temple, the second under Ezra the priest-scribe to rebuild the people around God's Law.",
 story:`<p>Cyrus decrees the rebuilding of the Temple, and about 50,000 exiles return under Zerubbabel and the high priest Jeshua. They restore the altar and lay the foundation; the old weep and the young shout for joy. Local opponents halt the work for years until the prophets Haggai and Zechariah stir the people, and Darius confirms the decree. The Temple is completed and dedicated in 515 BC and Passover is kept.</p>
<p>Decades later Ezra, 'a ready scribe in the law of Moses', comes from Babylon with royal support. He discovers that many, including leaders, have married women who worship other gods. Ezra mourns and prays a great prayer of confession (ch. 9), and the community resolves to separate from these marriages.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,2,"Cyrus's decree; the first return"],[3,6,"Rebuilding the Temple amid opposition"],[7,8,"Ezra's return"],[9,10,"Ezra's prayer and the mixed marriages"]],
 people:[["Zerubbabel","Governor of Judah, of David's line.","He appears in Jesus' genealogy (Mt 1:12); Haggai calls him God's signet ring."],["Jeshua (Joshua) the high priest","Rebuilds the altar with Zerubbabel.","Crowned in a symbolic act in Zech 6:11."],["Ezra","Priest and scribe who teaches and applies the Law.","Called the father of later Judaism; model of the teacher of Scripture."],["Cyrus and Darius","Persian kings who permit and fund the work.","God directs the hearts of kings (Prov 21:1)."]],
 themes:[["Restoration","God brings his people home."],["Temple","Worship rebuilt first."],["Word of God","The Law as the community's constitution."],["Identity and holiness","Keeping faith in a pluralist empire."]],
 christ:"The return from exile is a type of redemption from sin; Christ's resurrection is the true restoration of the temple 'in three days'.",
 church:"Ezra is a model for the ministry of the word and catechesis. The mixed-marriage crisis concerned the preservation of the faith from idolatry, not ethnicity; the Church's discipline on marriage with non-Christians likewise aims to protect the faith of spouse and children (CCC 1633–1637).",
 thomas:"Jerome and Bede comment on Ezra and Nehemiah as figures of the building of the Church; Bede's On Ezra and Nehemiah reads the rebuilding allegorically.",
 hard:[["Was it right to dissolve the marriages?","The concern was idolatry, which had brought the exile (Deut 7:3–4). The text records a drastic communal decision in a crisis; it is not presented as a model for Christian marriage, which the Church teaches is indissoluble when sacramental (CCC 1640)."]],
 keys:[["Ezra 1:1–4","Cyrus's decree"],["Ezra 3:10–13","weeping and joy"],["Ezra 7:10","Ezra set his heart to study, do and teach the Law"],["Ezra 9:5–15","Ezra's prayer"]],
 author:"Traditionally Ezra; Ezra and Nehemiah were one book in the Hebrew Bible. Probably compiled c. 400 BC from memoirs and Persian documents (some in Aramaic)."},

nehemiah:{era:"return",when:"445–c. 430 BC",genre:"Memoir and history (Douay-Rheims: 2 Esdras)",
 lede:"Nehemiah, a Jewish official at the Persian court, returns to rebuild Jerusalem's walls in 52 days despite opposition, then with Ezra renews the covenant as the people hear the Law read aloud.",
 story:`<p>Nehemiah, cupbearer to King Artaxerxes, hears that Jerusalem's walls lie in ruins. He prays and fasts, and the king sends him as governor. He inspects the walls by night, organises families to rebuild sections, and keeps working with 'one hand… and with the other he held a sword' against enemies led by Sanballat and Tobiah. He also stops the rich from exploiting the poor (ch. 5).</p>
<p>Ezra reads the Law to the assembled people from morning to midday; the Levites explain it, the people weep, and Nehemiah tells them: 'the joy of the Lord is our strength' (8:10). They celebrate Tabernacles, confess their sins (ch. 9) and sign a covenant. On a second visit Nehemiah reforms Sabbath abuses and Temple neglect.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,2,"Nehemiah's prayer and mission"],[3,7,"Rebuilding the walls amid opposition"],[8,10,"The Law read; confession and covenant"],[11,13,"Resettlement, dedication, reforms"]],
 people:[["Nehemiah","A layman of prayer and action who leads by example.","Model of faithful leadership in public life; prays before acting (2:4)."],["Ezra","Reads and teaches the Law.","Word and worship together."],["Sanballat and Tobiah","Opponents who mock and conspire.","Every work of God meets resistance."]],
 themes:[["Prayer and work","Nehemiah prays constantly and acts decisively."],["The word heard and understood","Reading, explanation, response."],["Justice","Cancelling debts and usury."],["Covenant renewal","A people recommitting to God."]],
 christ:"The rebuilding of Jerusalem points to the building of the Church, the new Jerusalem (Rev 21).",
 church:"Nehemiah 8 is a pattern for the Liturgy of the Word: proclamation, explanation (homily) and response. Nehemiah's care for the poor reflects Catholic social teaching on the common good.",
 thomas:"Bede's commentary reads Nehemiah's builders as preachers and teachers who build up the Church while guarding against error.",
 keys:[["Neh 1:4–11","Nehemiah's prayer"],["Neh 4","building with sword in hand"],["Neh 5","justice for the poor"],["Neh 8:1–10","the joy of the Lord"],["Neh 9","the great confession"]],
 author:"Largely Nehemiah's own memoir, edited with Ezra."},

tobit:{era:"divided",when:"Set among the exiles in Nineveh after 722 BC; written c. 225–175 BC",genre:"Edifying narrative (deuterocanonical; Douay-Rheims: Tobias)",
 lede:"Tobit is a family story of faith in exile: an old man blinded while doing good, a young woman tormented by a demon, and the archangel Raphael who guides Tobias on a journey that heals both families.",
 story:`<p>Tobit, a devout Israelite exiled in Nineveh, buries the dead and gives alms at great risk. He is blinded by bird droppings and, humiliated, prays for death. On the same day, in Ecbatana, a young woman named Sara also prays for death: seven husbands have been killed by the demon Asmodeus on their wedding night.</p>
<p>God sends the angel Raphael, disguised as a kinsman named Azarias, to accompany Tobit's son Tobias on a journey to recover money. On the way Tobias catches a fish whose organs will heal. He marries Sara; they pray together before their wedding night and the demon is driven away. Returning home, Tobias cures his father's blindness. Raphael reveals himself as 'one of the seven, who stand before the Lord' (12:15), and Tobit sings a hymn of praise for Jerusalem's future glory.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,3,"Tobit's trials and Sara's distress; two prayers"],[4,6,"Tobit's counsel; the journey with Raphael"],[7,9,"The marriage of Tobias and Sara"],[10,12,"Return, healing, and Raphael revealed"],[13,14,"Tobit's hymn and last words"]],
 people:[["Tobit (Tobias the elder)","A faithful Israelite in exile, known for almsgiving and burying the dead.","Corporal works of mercy as acts of faith."],["Tobias","His son, who obeys, travels, marries and heals.","A model of young faith, marriage and filial piety."],["Sara","Raguel's daughter, afflicted but faithful.","Her prayer and marriage show God's healing of the wounded."],["Raphael","Archangel whose name means 'God heals'.","The basis of the Church's belief in guardian angels and Raphael's feast (29 Sept, with Michael and Gabriel)."],["Anna","Tobit's wife, who worries and waits.","An honest picture of a marriage under stress."]],
 themes:[["Providence and angels","God's care is hidden but real."],["Almsgiving","'Alms deliver from all sin, and from death' (4:11; 12:9)."],["Marriage and prayer","Tobias and Sara's prayer (8:5–10) is used in the Catholic wedding liturgy."],["Healing","Of body, family and spirit."]],
 christ:"Raphael, the companion who heals and is recognised only at the end, foreshadows the hidden companion of the road to Emmaus (Lk 24).",
 church:"Tobit is cited for the guardian angels (CCC 336 speaks of angels' care; the Church celebrates Raphael), for almsgiving (CCC 2447) and for marriage (CCC 1611). It is one of the deuterocanonical books in the Catholic canon (Council of Rome 382; Florence 1442; Trent 1546), received from the Septuagint used by the early Church.",
 thomas:"Thomas cites Raphael's words that the angel only appeared to eat (12:19) in discussing angels' assumed bodies (ST I q.51 a.3). Tobit 4:16, 'see thou never do to another what thou wouldst hate to have done to thee by another', is one of the Bible's earliest statements of the golden rule.",
 hard:[["Is the fish-liver remedy magic?","The text attributes healing to God through Raphael's instruction; the remedy is the instrument, prayer the context. It teaches that God uses created means."],["Is Tobit historical?","Many Catholic scholars read it as an inspired edifying story (some historical details are loose), a legitimate literary genre that teaches truth without error about God and the moral life (DV 12)."]],
 keys:[["Tob 4","Tobit's counsel to his son"],["Tob 8:5–10","Tobias and Sara's prayer"],["Tob 12:6–15","Raphael reveals himself"],["Tob 13","Tobit's hymn"]],
 author:"Anonymous; written in Aramaic or Hebrew (fragments found at Qumran), c. 3rd–2nd century BC."},

judith:{era:"maccabees",when:"Set during an Assyrian/Babylonian threat; written c. 150–100 BC",genre:"Edifying narrative (deuterocanonical)",
 lede:"Judith is the story of a devout widow who saves her town and her people when its leaders are ready to surrender. Her name means 'Jewish woman', and she embodies Israel's faithful trust in God.",
 story:`<p>Nebuchadnezzar's general Holofernes marches west with a vast army, demanding worship of the king as god. The small town of Bethulia blocks the pass to Jerusalem. Under siege and without water, the elders promise to surrender in five days if God does not help.</p>
<p>Judith, a wealthy, beautiful and prayerful widow, rebukes them for putting God to the test. She prays (ch. 9), then goes with her maid into the enemy camp, wins Holofernes' trust, and when he lies drunk beheads him with his own sword. She returns with his head; the Assyrians flee. Judith leads Israel in a hymn of praise and lives the rest of her life in honour and chastity.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,7,"Holofernes' campaign and the siege of Bethulia"],[8,9,"Judith rebukes the elders and prays"],[10,13,"Judith in the enemy camp"],[14,16,"Victory and Judith's song"]],
 people:[["Judith","A widow devoted to prayer and fasting, courageous and wise.","Liturgical tradition applies to Mary the praise given to Judith: 'thou art the glory of Jerusalem… the honour of our people' (15:10)."],["Holofernes","The arrogant general who demands worship for his king.","Pride brought low by the weak."],["Achior","An Ammonite who tells Holofernes the truth about Israel's God and later converts.","Gentile witness and conversion."]],
 themes:[["Trust in God","Not testing God but trusting him."],["God saves through the weak","The Almighty delivers the enemy 'into the hands of a woman' (16:7)."],["Prayer and fasting","Judith's strength comes from her life of prayer."],["Resistance to idolatry","No human ruler is God."]],
 christ:"The Fathers saw Judith, who crushes the head of the enemy, as a type of Mary (Gen 3:15) and of the Church triumphing over evil.",
 church:"Judith's praise is used in Marian feasts. Her rebuke of the elders is a classic text on not putting God to the test (CCC 2119). Judith is in the Catholic canon (Trent 1546).",
 thomas:"Thomas, asked whether Judith lied to Holofernes, answers that she is praised for her love of her people and courage, not for any deception (ST II-II q.110 a.3 ad 3).",
 hard:[["Is Judith historical?","The book mixes historical names deliberately (Nebuchadnezzar as king of Assyria), which signals to readers that it is a didactic story. Most Catholic scholars read it as inspired teaching in narrative form."],["Isn't her deception and killing wrong?","Thomas distinguishes what Judith is praised for (zeal, courage) from what is merely reported. The book belongs to the genre of the holy warrior-deliverer, like Jael or David with Goliath."]],
 keys:[["Jdt 8:11–27","Judith's rebuke"],["Jdt 9","Judith's prayer"],["Jdt 13","the death of Holofernes"],["Jdt 15:9–10","'thou art the glory of Jerusalem'"],["Jdt 16","Judith's song"]],
 author:"Anonymous; probably written in Hebrew in the Hasmonean period. Survives in Greek."},

esther:{era:"return",when:"Set at the Persian court of Ahasuerus (Xerxes I, 486–465 BC)",genre:"Narrative (Catholic Bibles include the Greek additions)",
 lede:"Esther, a Jewish orphan who becomes queen of Persia, risks her life to save her people from a decree of extermination. The feast of Purim celebrates the reversal.",
 story:`<p>King Ahasuerus deposes Queen Vashti and chooses Esther, cousin and ward of Mordecai, who keeps her Jewish identity secret. Mordecai uncovers a plot against the king. The proud official Haman, enraged that Mordecai will not bow to him, persuades the king to decree the destruction of all Jews on a day chosen by lot (pur).</p>
<p>Mordecai urges Esther to intercede: 'who knoweth whether thou art not therefore come to the kingdom, that thou mightest be ready in such a time as this?' (4:14). After three days of fasting and prayer (her prayer appears in the Greek additions), Esther approaches the king unbidden. At two banquets she exposes Haman, who is hanged on the gallows he built for Mordecai. A counter-decree allows the Jews to defend themselves, and Purim is instituted.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,2,"Esther becomes queen"],[3,4,"Haman's plot and Mordecai's appeal"],[5,7,"Esther's banquets and Haman's fall"],[8,10,"Deliverance and Purim"],[11,16,"Greek additions: dreams, prayers, decrees"]],
 people:[["Esther (Hadassah)","An orphan made queen who intercedes for her people at the risk of her life.","Read by the Fathers and liturgy as a type of Mary, who intercedes with the King."],["Mordecai","Esther's cousin, faithful and refusing idolatrous honour.","Integrity under pressure; providence rewards him."],["Haman","An Agagite official consumed by pride.","Pride before a fall; the persecutor undone."],["Ahasuerus","The Persian king, powerful but easily manipulated.","God's providence works through even a capricious ruler."]],
 themes:[["Hidden providence","God is not named in the Hebrew text, yet reversals reveal his hand."],["Intercession","Esther's approach to the king."],["Courage and identity","Standing with one's people."],["Reversal","The proud brought low, the lowly raised (cf. Lk 1:52)."]],
 christ:"Esther, who offers herself to save her people, and the reversal from death to life on the 'third day' (5:1), foreshadow Christ's self-offering and resurrection. The Church also sees in Esther an image of Mary's intercession.",
 church:"The Catholic canon includes the Greek additions (chs 11–16 in the Vulgate order, often placed as A–F in modern Bibles), which make God and prayer explicit. Esther's prayer ('Lord, who alone art our King') is used in the liturgy of Lent.",
 thomas:"Jerome moved the Greek additions to the end of his Vulgate, which explains the odd numbering. Thomas uses Esther's intercession as an example of prayer to God through those he favours.",
 keys:[["Est 4:14","'for such a time as this'"],["Est 14 (Greek C)","Esther's prayer"],["Est 7","Haman's fall"],["Est 9:20–32","Purim"]],
 author:"Anonymous; the Hebrew c. 4th–3rd century BC, Greek additions 2nd–1st century BC."},

"1-maccabees":{era:"maccabees",when:"175–134 BC",genre:"History (deuterocanonical)",
 lede:"1 Maccabees is a sober history of the Jewish revolt against the Seleucid king Antiochus IV, who tried to stamp out the Law, and of the Maccabee brothers who won religious freedom and independence.",
 story:`<p>After Alexander the Great, Judea falls under the Seleucid kings. Antiochus IV Epiphanes plunders the Temple, forbids circumcision and the Sabbath, and sets up 'the abominable idol of desolation' on the altar (1:57; 167 BC). Some Jews embrace Greek ways; others die rather than eat unclean food.</p>
<p>The priest Mattathias refuses to sacrifice to idols and flees to the hills with his five sons. His son Judas, called Maccabeus ('the hammer'), wins astonishing victories, recaptures Jerusalem and purifies the Temple; the dedication is celebrated for eight days (4:36–59), the origin of Hanukkah (Jn 10:22). After Judas dies in battle, his brothers Jonathan and Simon continue, securing alliances and independence. Simon is made high priest and ruler, founding the Hasmonean dynasty.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,2,"Antiochus's persecution and Mattathias's revolt"],[3,9,"Judas Maccabeus and the rededication of the Temple"],[10,12,"Jonathan"],[13,16,"Simon and independence"]],
 people:[["Antiochus IV Epiphanes","Seleucid king who persecutes faithful Jews and desecrates the Temple.","A type of the persecutor; Daniel's 'abomination of desolation'."],["Mattathias","Priest of Modein who starts the revolt with zeal for the Law.","Fidelity over accommodation."],["Judas Maccabeus","Military leader, prayerful and brave, who restores the Temple.","Model of the faithful warrior; also central to 2 Maccabees."],["Jonathan and Simon","Judas's brothers who succeed him as leaders and high priests.","Political success and its ambiguities."]],
 themes:[["Fidelity under persecution","Refusing apostasy at the cost of life."],["Zeal for the Law","Mattathias's zeal (2:27)."],["Rededication","Purifying worship."],["Faith and politics","Religious freedom won; later compromises."]],
 christ:"Jesus attended the feast of the Dedication in Jerusalem (Jn 10:22) and declared himself the one whom the Father 'sanctified', the true consecrated Temple. Jesus speaks of the 'abomination of desolation' (Mt 24:15).",
 church:"1 Maccabees is in the Catholic canon (Trent). It informs the Church's teaching on religious liberty and on just resistance to persecution, and the martyrs who 'chose rather to die than to be defiled' (1:66) are models of fidelity.",
 thomas:"Thomas cites the Maccabees when discussing whether it is lawful to fight on holy days (ST II-II q.40 a.4), drawing on 1 Macc 2:41.",
 keys:[["1 Macc 1:57–66","persecution and the faithful"],["1 Macc 2:19–28","Mattathias's refusal"],["1 Macc 2:41","fighting on the Sabbath"],["1 Macc 4:36–59","the rededication of the Temple"]],
 author:"An anonymous Jewish historian writing in Hebrew c. 100 BC; survives in Greek."},

"2-maccabees":{era:"maccabees",when:"c. 180–161 BC",genre:"Theological history (deuterocanonical)",
 lede:"2 Maccabees is not a sequel but a parallel, more theological account of the same crisis. It gives the Bible's clearest Old Testament teaching on the resurrection of the body, creation from nothing, the intercession of the saints and prayer for the dead.",
 story:`<p>The book opens with letters urging Egyptian Jews to keep the feast of the Temple's purification, then condenses a five-volume history by Jason of Cyrene. It begins with Heliodorus, prevented by an angelic vision from robbing the Temple, then describes high priests who sell out to Greek culture and the persecution under Antiochus IV.</p>
<p>Two martyr stories stand at the heart: the aged scribe Eleazar, who refuses even to pretend to eat pork, and a mother and her seven sons who die one by one, confessing that God will raise them (ch. 7). Judas Maccabeus leads the revolt, purifies the Temple and wins victories. After a battle he finds idolatrous amulets on fallen soldiers and sends money for a sin offering, 'that they might be loosed from sins' (12:46). Judas sees in a vision the dead high priest Onias and the prophet Jeremiah praying for the people (ch. 15). The book ends with the defeat of Nicanor.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,2,"Letters and the author's preface"],[3,5,"Heliodorus; corrupt high priests"],[6,7,"Persecution: Eleazar and the seven brothers"],[8,10,"Judas's victories and the Temple's purification"],[11,15,"Further wars; prayer for the dead; Nicanor's defeat"]],
 people:[["Eleazar","A 90-year-old scribe who dies rather than give scandal by pretending (ch. 6).","Integrity and avoiding scandal to the young."],["The mother and her seven sons","Martyrs who confess the resurrection and God's creation from nothing (ch. 7).","Venerated as the Holy Maccabean Martyrs (1 August)."],["Judas Maccabeus","Leader who prays and offers sacrifice for the dead.","Witness to the practice of praying for the dead."],["Onias and Jeremiah","Seen in a vision interceding for Israel (15:12–14).","Witness to the intercession of the departed righteous."]],
 themes:[["Resurrection of the body","'The King of the world will raise us up… in the resurrection of eternal life' (7:9)."],["Creation from nothing","'God made them out of nothing' (7:28)."],["Prayer for the dead","'a holy and wholesome thought to pray for the dead' (12:46)."],["Intercession of the saints","Jeremiah 'prayeth much for the people' (15:14)."],["Martyrdom","Faith stronger than death."]],
 christ:"The martyrs' hope in resurrection is fulfilled in Christ, the firstfruits of those who sleep (1 Cor 15:20). Hebrews 11:35 alludes to those who 'were racked, not accepting deliverance, that they might find a better resurrection', almost certainly referring to 2 Macc 6–7.",
 church:"2 Maccabees 12:46 is the classic scriptural text for prayer for the dead and purgatory (CCC 1032); 7:28 for creation ex nihilo (CCC 297); 7 for the resurrection of the body (CCC 992). The intercession of Onias and Jeremiah supports the communion of saints. Its place in the canon was affirmed at Rome (382), Carthage (397), Florence (1442) and Trent (1546); Protestant reformers removed it partly because of these doctrines.",
 thomas:"Thomas cites 2 Macc 12:46 for suffrages for the dead (ST Suppl. q.71 a.2) and 2 Macc 15:14 for the prayers of the saints (ST II-II q.83 a.11).",
 hard:[["Was 2 Maccabees in the early Church's Bible?","Yes. It was part of the Septuagint, the Greek Old Testament most quoted in the New Testament, and is listed as canonical by the councils of Rome (382), Hippo (393) and Carthage (397). Some Fathers (e.g. Jerome) were hesitant about the deuterocanonical books, but the Church's practice and councils received them."],["The author apologises for his style (15:38–39). Does that undermine inspiration?","No. Inspiration does not remove the human author's personality or limitations of style (DV 11–12); God used the author's own effort and words."]],
 keys:[["2 Macc 6:18–31","Eleazar"],["2 Macc 7","the seven brothers and their mother"],["2 Macc 7:28","creation out of nothing"],["2 Macc 12:38–46","prayer for the dead"],["2 Macc 15:12–16","Jeremiah intercedes"]],
 author:"An anonymous epitomist summarising Jason of Cyrene's five books, written in Greek c. 124 BC or later."}
});

// Book introductions: wisdom and poetry
Object.assign(BOOK_INTRO,{
job:{era:"patriarchs",when:"Set in patriarchal times in the land of Hus (Uz); written perhaps 6th–4th century BC",genre:"Wisdom drama in poetry",
 lede:"Job asks the question every sufferer asks: why do the innocent suffer? A righteous man loses everything, refuses both to curse God and to accept his friends' easy answers, and finally meets God himself.",
 story:`<p>In a prose prologue, Satan ('the adversary') suggests to God that Job is righteous only because he is blessed. God permits a test: Job loses his children, wealth and health, yet says, 'the Lord gave, and the Lord hath taken away… blessed be the name of the Lord' (1:21). He sits on the ash heap; his wife tells him to curse God and die.</p>
<p>Three friends, Eliphaz, Bildad and Sophar, come to comfort him and sit in silence for seven days. Then the long poetic dialogues begin. The friends insist that suffering must be punishment for sin; Job protests his innocence, laments, accuses, and demands a hearing with God, yet also confesses, 'I know that my Redeemer liveth' (19:25). A young man, Eliu, speaks of suffering as discipline. Finally God answers 'out of a whirlwind', not with an explanation but with questions about creation that reveal his wisdom and power. Job repents 'in dust and ashes'. God rebukes the friends for not speaking rightly as Job did, Job prays for them, and his fortunes are restored.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,2,"Prologue: the wager in heaven and Job's losses"],[3,31,"Dialogues with the three friends"],[32,37,"Eliu's speeches"],[38,41,"God speaks from the whirlwind"],[42,42,"Job's response and restoration"]],
 people:[["Job","A blameless man from Uz, not an Israelite, who loses everything.","The model of patience (Jas 5:11) and of honest lament that stays within faith."],["Satan","'The adversary' in the heavenly court, who can act only within God's permission.","Evil is real but not sovereign."],["Eliphaz, Bildad and Sophar","Friends who defend a rigid theory of retribution.","Correct principles misapplied can wound; God rebukes them (42:7)."],["Eliu (Elihu)","A younger man who argues that suffering can purify and teach.","Partial truth: suffering can be medicinal."]],
 themes:[["Innocent suffering","Not all suffering is punishment for personal sin (cf. Jn 9:3)."],["Providence beyond comprehension","God's answer reveals a wisdom too vast for us to audit."],["Honest prayer","Job's complaints are addressed to God, and God vindicates him."],["The redeemer","Job's longing for a vindicator."]],
 christ:"Job is the innocent sufferer who is vindicated, a figure of Christ. 'I know that my Redeemer liveth, and in the last day I shall rise out of the earth' (19:25, DR) is read in the Office of the Dead as a prophecy of resurrection. Job longs for a mediator between God and man (9:33), fulfilled in Christ (1 Tim 2:5).",
 church:"Job is central to the Christian understanding of suffering (John Paul II, Salvifici Doloris 11–12, 1984) and of providence (CCC 309–314: God permits evil to draw forth greater good; the full answer is the whole of Christian faith). Its prayers are used in the Office of the Dead.",
 thomas:"Thomas wrote a full literal commentary, Expositio super Iob ad litteram (c. 1263–65), whose stated purpose is to show 'that human affairs are ruled by divine providence'. He reads the debate as a disputation and holds that Job's hope rests on the future life, where justice is fully done. Gregory the Great's Moralia in Job, a vast moral commentary, shaped medieval spirituality.",
 hard:[["Does God make bets with Satan about human beings?","The prologue is a dramatic scene in the heavenly court, a literary device that lets the reader know what Job cannot: that his suffering is not punishment. It teaches that God permits trials within limits and for purposes we do not see."],["Is Job a real person?","Ezekiel 14:14 and James 5:11 mention him. Many scholars read the book as a wisdom drama built around a traditional figure. Its inspired teaching does not depend on settling the question."]],
 keys:[["Job 1:21","'The Lord gave, and the Lord hath taken away'"],["Job 3","Job curses his birth"],["Job 19:25–27","'I know that my Redeemer liveth'"],["Job 28","where is wisdom found?"],["Job 38–41","God speaks from the whirlwind"],["Job 42:5–6","'now my eye seeth thee'"]],
 author:"Anonymous. Jewish tradition credits Moses; scholars date it between the 7th and 4th century BC."},

psalms:{era:"kingdom",when:"Composed over many centuries, from David (c. 1000 BC) to after the exile",genre:"Prayers and songs (Vulgate numbering used here)",
 lede:"The Psalms are Israel's prayer book and hymnal: 150 poems of praise, lament, thanksgiving, trust and royal hope. Jesus prayed them, and the Church prays them every day in the Liturgy of the Hours.",
 story:`<p>The Psalter is not a story but a collection, arranged in five books (1–40, 41–71, 72–88, 89–105, 106–150 in Vulgate numbering), perhaps echoing the five books of Moses, each closing with a doxology. Many psalms are headed 'of David'; others are attributed to Asaph, the sons of Core (Korah), Solomon, Moses (89 [90]) and others.</p>
<p>The Psalms cover every human situation: the joy of creation (8, 18 [19], 103 [104]); trust (22 [23], 90 [91]); lament and anguish (21 [22], 87 [88]); repentance (50 [51]); the king and Zion (2, 44 [45], 109 [110]); love of the Law (118 [119]); pilgrimage (119–133 [120–134]); and pure praise (145–150). Their movement, broadly, is from lament to praise.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,40,"Book I (mostly David)"],[41,71,"Book II"],[72,88,"Book III (Asaph and Core)"],[89,105,"Book IV (the Lord reigns)"],[106,150,"Book V (praise, ascents, Hallel)"]],
 people:[["David","Shepherd-king and 'the excellent psalmist of Israel' (2 Sam 23:1), to whom 73 psalms are attributed in the Hebrew.","His experiences of persecution, sin and deliverance give the Psalms their voice; Jesus is the Son of David who prays them."],["Asaph, Heman, Ethan, the sons of Core","Temple musicians and Levitical guilds.","The Psalms belong to the liturgy of a whole people."],["The anointed king","Royal psalms celebrate the Davidic king.","Read by the Church as prophecies of Christ the King (Ps 2; 109 [110])."]],
 themes:[["Praise","The Hebrew title is Tehillim, 'praises'."],["Lament","Honest complaint brought to God; about a third of the Psalter."],["Torah","Delight in God's law (Ps 1, 18 [19], 118 [119])."],["Kingship of God and of the Messiah","The Lord reigns (92–98 [93–99])."],["Trust","God as shepherd, refuge and rock."]],
 christ:"Jesus prays the Psalms on the Cross: 'My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?' (Ps 21 [22]:1) and 'Into thy hands I commend my spirit' (Ps 30 [31]:6). Psalm 109 [110] ('The Lord said to my Lord… Thou art a priest for ever according to the order of Melchisedech') is the Old Testament text most quoted in the New. Augustine taught that in the Psalms we hear the 'whole Christ', Head and members, praying.",
 church:"The Psalms are 'the masterwork of prayer in the Old Testament' (CCC 2585–2589) and the backbone of the Liturgy of the Hours and the responsorial psalm at Mass. Psalm 50 (51) is the great prayer of repentance; Psalm 94 (95) opens the Office each day.",
 thomas:"Thomas's Postilla super Psalmos covers Psalms 1–54 and was his last scriptural commentary (1272–73). In its prologue he says the Psalter contains the whole of theology in the form of prayer and praise. Augustine's Enarrationes in Psalmos and Cassiodorus's Expositio are the great patristic commentaries. See the Reader note on Vulgate and Hebrew numbering.",
 hard:[["What about the cursing psalms (e.g. 108 [109], 136 [137]:9)?","They give voice to the cry for justice of the oppressed, addressed to God rather than acted out. The Church reads them in the light of Christ, who prays for his enemies, and applies them to spiritual enemies: sin and the devil. A few verses are omitted in the Liturgy of the Hours for pastoral reasons."],["Why are the numbers different from my Bible?","The Douay-Rheims and Vulgate follow the Greek numbering, which combines and splits some psalms differently. From Psalm 10 to 147 the Hebrew number is usually one higher."]],
 keys:[["Ps 1","the two ways"],["Ps 2","the Lord's anointed"],["Ps 8","what is man?"],["Ps 21 [22]","the Passion psalm"],["Ps 22 [23]","the Lord is my shepherd"],["Ps 50 [51]","Miserere: the penitent's prayer"],["Ps 109 [110]","priest for ever after Melchisedech"],["Ps 118 [119]","love for God's law"],["Ps 150","everything that breathes, praise the Lord"]],
 author:"Many authors over some 600 years; the collection was completed after the exile."},

proverbs:{era:"kingdom",when:"Solomonic core (c. 950 BC); collected under Hezekiah (c. 700 BC); final form after the exile",genre:"Wisdom sayings",
 lede:"Proverbs is a school of practical wisdom: how to live well before God in work, speech, money, friendship, family and sex. Its foundation: 'the fear of the Lord is the beginning of wisdom' (9:10).",
 story:`<p>Chapters 1–9 are a father's instructions to his son, warning against violent men and the seductive 'strange woman', and personifying Wisdom as a noble lady who calls in the streets and who was with God before creation (ch. 8). Wisdom builds her house and invites the simple to her banquet (ch. 9).</p>
<p>Chapters 10–29 are collections of short sayings, mostly two-line contrasts, attributed to Solomon and to 'the men of Ezechias'. They cover diligence and laziness, honest speech and gossip, generosity, anger, pride, friendship, parenting and justice. Shorter collections by Agur and King Lamuel follow, and the book ends with an acrostic poem praising 'the valiant woman' (31:10–31).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,9,"A father's instructions; Lady Wisdom"],[10,22,"Proverbs of Solomon"],[22,24,"Words of the wise"],[25,29,"Solomon's proverbs copied under Hezekiah"],[30,31,"Agur, Lamuel and the valiant woman"]],
 people:[["Solomon","The king famed for wisdom, traditional source of the collection.","Wisdom is a gift from God that must be sought."],["Lady Wisdom","Wisdom personified as a woman who was with God at creation (8:22–31).","The Fathers read her as the eternal Word, through whom all was made."],["The valiant woman","An industrious, wise, charitable wife and mother (31:10–31).","An ideal of virtue, applied in the liturgy to holy women and to Mary."]],
 themes:[["Fear of the Lord","Reverent awe is wisdom's foundation."],["Two ways","Wisdom and folly, life and death."],["Speech","'Death and life are in the power of the tongue' (18:21)."],["Character and consequence","Habits shape destiny; Proverbs gives general truths, not guarantees."]],
 christ:"Christ is 'the wisdom of God' (1 Cor 1:24), and Proverbs 8, where Wisdom is begotten before the hills and rejoices with God in creating, was read by the Fathers as a portrait of the eternal Son. Wisdom's banquet of bread and wine (9:1–5) was read as a figure of the Eucharist.",
 church:"Proverbs is a handbook of the virtues: prudence, justice, temperance in drink and speech, fortitude in work. Proverbs 8:22 ('The Lord possessed me in the beginning of his ways') was disputed at Nicaea: Arians used it to argue the Son was created; Athanasius explained it of the incarnate Word.",
 thomas:"Thomas begins the Summa Contra Gentiles (I.1) with Proverbs 8:7: 'My mouth shall meditate truth, and my lips shall hate wickedness', defining the wise man's office as to contemplate truth and refute error.",
 hard:[["Do the proverbs promise that good people will always prosper?","They state general patterns, not guarantees. Job and Ecclesiastes balance them. Wisdom literature reads best as a conversation between books."]],
 keys:[["Prov 1:7; 9:10","the fear of the Lord"],["Prov 3:5–6","trust in the Lord"],["Prov 8:22–31","Wisdom at creation"],["Prov 9:1–6","Wisdom's banquet"],["Prov 31:10–31","the valiant woman"]],
 author:"Solomon is the traditional author of the core; the book names other contributors (Agur, Lamuel, 'the wise'). It reached final form after the exile."},

ecclesiastes:{era:"kingdom",when:"Traditionally Solomon's old age; most scholars date it c. 300–200 BC",genre:"Wisdom reflection",
 lede:"Ecclesiastes (Qoheleth, 'the Preacher') looks honestly at life 'under the sun' and finds that pleasure, work, wisdom and wealth cannot satisfy. Its refrain: 'Vanity of vanities, and all is vanity' (1:2).",
 story:`<p>The Preacher, presenting himself as a king in Jerusalem, conducts an experiment: he tries pleasure, great works, wealth and wisdom, and finds all of it 'vanity' (Hebrew hevel, vapour or breath): fleeting, elusive, unable to give lasting meaning. Death comes to wise and fool alike; injustice is everywhere; the future is unknown.</p>
<p>Yet he also commends simple gifts received from God's hand: eating, drinking, work and married love (2:24; 9:7–9). There is 'a time to be born and a time to die' (ch. 3), and God 'hath made all things good in their time, and hath delivered the world to their consideration' (3:11). The book ends with a poem on old age and the conclusion: 'Fear God, and keep his commandments: for this is all man' (12:13).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,2,"All is vanity: the Preacher's experiment"],[3,3,"A time for everything"],[4,8,"Observations on injustice, work, wealth and wisdom"],[9,11,"Enjoy life as God's gift; act despite uncertainty"],[12,12,"Remember your Creator; the conclusion"]],
 people:[["Qoheleth, the Preacher","An assembly-speaker who presents himself as a son of David.","A searching mind that refuses easy answers yet holds on to God."]],
 themes:[["Vanity","Created goods cannot be our final end."],["Death","The great leveller."],["Gift","Ordinary joys received with gratitude."],["Fear of God","The only firm ground."]],
 christ:"Ecclesiastes shows the emptiness of life without the hope of resurrection, preparing for Christ, who frees creation from its 'vanity' (Rom 8:20, using the same Greek word as the Septuagint's Ecclesiastes).",
 church:"Ecclesiastes is the Old Testament's great witness to the restless heart Augustine describes ('our heart is restless until it rests in thee'). It supports the Church's teaching that no created good can be man's last end (CCC 1723).",
 thomas:"Ecclesiastes illustrates Thomas's argument that happiness does not consist in wealth, honour, fame, power, bodily goods or pleasure (ST I-II q.2 aa.1–8). Jerome wrote a commentary for Blesilla on contempt of the world.",
 hard:[["Is the book pessimistic or even sceptical?","It is honest about life under the sun without God's full revelation, and it repeatedly returns to God as giver and judge. The Church reads it as a necessary stage of wisdom: disillusionment that opens the way to hope."]],
 keys:[["Eccl 1:2","vanity of vanities"],["Eccl 3:1–11","a time for everything"],["Eccl 4:9–12","two are better than one"],["Eccl 12:1–7","remember thy Creator in the days of thy youth"],["Eccl 12:13","fear God and keep his commandments"]],
 author:"Traditionally Solomon; the Hebrew and themes point to a later sage, c. 3rd century BC."},

"song-of-songs":{era:"kingdom",when:"Traditionally Solomon; final form uncertain",genre:"Love poetry (Douay-Rheims: Canticle of Canticles)",
 lede:"The Song of Songs ('the greatest song') is a series of love poems between a bride and her beloved. It celebrates human love and, as the Church has always read it, the love between God and his people, Christ and the Church, Christ and the soul.",
 story:`<p>There is no simple plot. The bride and the bridegroom (Solomon, or a shepherd-king) long for each other, praise each other's beauty, seek and find each other. Twice the bride searches the city at night for her beloved. Friends ('daughters of Jerusalem') form a chorus, warned not to 'stir up' love before its time.</p>
<p>The poems are full of gardens, vineyards, spices and springtime. The climax declares: 'love is strong as death… many waters cannot quench charity' (8:6–7).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,2,"Longing and first meeting"],[3,5,"Searching and finding; the wedding"],[5,6,"Separation and renewed praise"],[7,8,"Mutual delight; love strong as death"]],
 people:[["The bride (the Shulamite)","A young woman who speaks most of the Song.","Read as the Church, the soul, and especially Mary."],["The bridegroom","Her beloved, called king and shepherd.","Read as Christ, the divine Bridegroom."],["The daughters of Jerusalem","A chorus of friends.","Love must not be awakened before its time (2:7)."]],
 themes:[["Human love is good","Erotic love as God's gift within covenant."],["Seeking and finding","The soul's longing for God."],["Faithful, exclusive love","'My beloved to me, and I to him' (2:16)."],["Love stronger than death","8:6."]],
 christ:"The Church has read the Song as the love of Christ and the Church (Eph 5:25–32), building on the prophets' marriage imagery for God and Israel (Hos 2; Isa 62:5). Origen, Gregory of Nyssa and Bernard of Clairvaux (86 sermons) read it as the soul's journey to union with the Word. The liturgy applies its verses to Mary ('Thou art all fair, O my love, and there is not a spot in thee', 4:7).",
 church:"Tradition 'has always seen in the Song of Solomon a unique expression of human love, insofar as it is a reflection of God's love' (CCC 1611). John Paul II's Theology of the Body draws on it for the meaning of spousal love. Benedict XVI, Deus Caritas Est 6, cites it on eros purified into agape. John of the Cross's Spiritual Canticle is modelled on it.",
 thomas:"Early biographers report that Thomas, dying at Fossanova in 1274, expounded the Song to the monks; no text survives. Jewish tradition reads it as God's love for Israel; Rabbi Akiva called it 'the holy of holies' among the writings.",
 hard:[["Is it 'just' erotic poetry?","Its literal sense is the love of man and woman, which the Church affirms as good. Its fuller sense, recognised by Jewish and Christian tradition alike, is divine love. These readings are complementary: human married love is itself an image of God's love (CCC 1604)."]],
 keys:[["Song 1:1","Let him kiss me with the kiss of his mouth"],["Song 2:16","my beloved to me, and I to him"],["Song 3:1–4","seeking him whom my soul loveth"],["Song 4:7","all fair, no spot"],["Song 8:6–7","love strong as death"]],
 author:"Attributed to Solomon (1:1); date and author debated."},

wisdom:{era:"maccabees",when:"Written in Greek in Alexandria, c. 50–30 BC; presented as Solomon's words",genre:"Wisdom exhortation (deuterocanonical)",
 lede:"The Book of Wisdom, written by a Greek-speaking Jew, speaks to believers tempted by pagan culture. It teaches the immortality of the righteous soul, praises Wisdom as God's radiance, and shows how God saved Israel in the Exodus.",
 story:`<p>Chapters 1–5 contrast the ungodly, who think life ends at death and so 'let us crown ourselves with roses' and persecute the just man, with the righteous, whose 'souls are in the hand of God' (3:1). The ungodly plot to condemn the just man who calls himself God's son to 'a most shameful death' (2:12–20).</p>
<p>Chapters 6–9 speak as Solomon, praising Wisdom as 'the brightness of eternal light, and the unspotted mirror of God's majesty' (7:26) and ending with his prayer for Wisdom (ch. 9). Chapters 10–19 trace Wisdom's work in history, especially the Exodus, contrasting the plagues on Egypt with blessings on Israel, and include a sharp critique of idolatry and of those who fail to know the Creator from the beauty of creatures (ch. 13).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,5,"The destiny of the just and the ungodly"],[6,9,"Solomon's praise of Wisdom and prayer for her"],[10,12,"Wisdom in history; God's mercy"],[13,15,"The folly of idolatry"],[16,19,"The Exodus: Egypt and Israel contrasted"]],
 people:[["The just man","Persecuted for claiming God as his father (ch. 2).","A strikingly exact foreshadowing of Christ's Passion (cf. Mt 27:43)."],["Solomon","The speaker in chs 6–9.","Wisdom is sought by prayer."],["Lady Wisdom","God's radiance, sharing his throne.","Applied to the eternal Son (Heb 1:3)."]],
 themes:[["Immortality","God 'created man incorruptible' (2:23); death entered through the devil's envy."],["Wisdom personified","Emanation of God's glory."],["Natural knowledge of God","From the greatness and beauty of creatures the Creator can be known (13:5)."],["Mercy and order","God orders all things 'in measure, and number, and weight' (11:21)."]],
 christ:"Hebrews 1:3 echoes Wisdom 7:26 in calling the Son 'the brightness of his glory'. Wisdom 2:12–20 is read in Lent as a prophecy of the Passion. 'The souls of the just are in the hand of God' (3:1) is read at funerals and for martyrs.",
 church:"Wisdom 13:1–9 underlies Vatican I's teaching that God can be known with certainty by natural reason (Dei Filius ch. 2; CCC 32, 36), echoed by Paul in Rom 1:19–20. Wisdom 2:24 on death through the devil's envy is cited for the fall (CCC 391, 413). It is in the Catholic canon (Trent).",
 thomas:"Thomas often cites Wisdom 11:21 on God's ordering of all things by measure, number and weight, and 8:1 that Wisdom 'ordereth all things sweetly' (DR), a key text for providence and for how grace works through nature (e.g. ST I q.5 a.5 on 11:21; I-II q.110 a.2 on 8:1).",
 keys:[["Wis 1:13–15","God made not death"],["Wis 2:12–20","the just man persecuted"],["Wis 3:1–9","the souls of the just"],["Wis 7:22–8:1","Wisdom's nature"],["Wis 9","Solomon's prayer"],["Wis 13:1–9","knowing the Creator from creation"]],
 author:"An anonymous Jewish sage writing in Greek in Alexandria; the attribution to Solomon is a literary device the author's first readers would have recognised."},

sirach:{era:"maccabees",when:"Written in Hebrew c. 190–180 BC; translated into Greek by the author's grandson after 132 BC",genre:"Wisdom sayings (deuterocanonical; Douay-Rheims: Ecclesiasticus)",
 lede:"Sirach, by the Jerusalem teacher Jesus ben Sira, is the longest wisdom book: practical advice on virtue, family, speech, friendship, prayer and work, ending with a great hymn to Israel's heroes. The Latin name Ecclesiasticus ('the church's book') reflects its use in teaching catechumens.",
 story:`<p>A prologue by Ben Sira's grandson explains why he translated it. The book then gathers teaching in many short sections: the fear of the Lord, honouring parents, humility, almsgiving, choosing friends ('a faithful friend is a strong defence', 6:14), controlling the tongue, avoiding sin, the right use of wealth, medicine and physicians (ch. 38), and the dignity of the scribe who studies God's law. Wisdom speaks in praise of herself and says she made her dwelling in Jacob and in Jerusalem (ch. 24).</p>
<p>Chapters 44–50, 'Let us now praise men of renown', survey Israel's heroes from Enoch and Noah to Simon the high priest, describing his splendour in the Temple liturgy. The book ends with Ben Sira's prayer and an invitation to seek wisdom.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,23,"Wisdom and the fear of the Lord; family, friendship, speech"],[24,24,"Wisdom's self-praise"],[25,43,"Further counsels; God's works in creation"],[44,50,"Praise of the fathers"],[51,51,"Ben Sira's prayer"]],
 people:[["Jesus ben Sira","A Jerusalem scribe and teacher (50:29).","Model of the believing intellectual who unites learning and piety."],["Simon the high priest","Simon II (c. 200 BC), described in the Temple's glory (ch. 50).","Liturgy as the summit of Israel's life."],["The fathers","Enoch, Noah, Abraham, Moses, David, Elijah and others (chs 44–49).","Salvation history as a communion of the faithful across time."]],
 themes:[["Fear of the Lord","Wisdom's beginning, fulness, crown and root (ch. 1)."],["Free will","'God made man from the beginning, and left him in the hand of his own counsel' (15:14)."],["Practical virtue","Friendship, speech, family, wealth."],["Wisdom and Law","Wisdom identified with the Law of Moses (24:32–33)."]],
 christ:"Wisdom making her dwelling in Israel ('he that made me, rested in my tabernacle', 24:12) anticipates the Word who 'dwelt among us' (Jn 1:14). Jesus' invitation 'Come to me… take up my yoke' (Mt 11:28–30) echoes Sirach 51:23–26.",
 church:"Sirach 15:14 is a classic text for human freedom (CCC 1730; cited at Trent). Its teaching on honouring parents (3:1–16) underlies the fourth commandment (CCC 2215–2218). Chapter 38 on physicians affirms medicine as God's gift. In the Catholic canon (Trent).",
 thomas:"Thomas cites Sirach 15:14 in the sed contra of ST I q.83 a.1 to show that man has free choice. Augustine and Cyprian quote Sirach frequently as Scripture.",
 keys:[["Sir 1:11–20","the fear of the Lord"],["Sir 2:1","prepare thy soul for temptation"],["Sir 3:1–16","honour father and mother"],["Sir 6:14–17","a faithful friend"],["Sir 15:14–18","free will"],["Sir 24","Wisdom's dwelling"],["Sir 38:1–15","the physician"],["Sir 44:1","let us praise men of renown"]],
 author:"Jesus ben Eleazar ben Sira, c. 180 BC; Greek translation by his grandson. Large parts of the Hebrew were rediscovered in the Cairo Genizah (1896) and at Masada."}
});

// Book introductions: the prophets
Object.assign(BOOK_INTRO,{
isaiah:{era:"divided",when:"Isaiah of Jerusalem, c. 740–700 BC; chs 40–66 address the exile and return",genre:"Prophecy (Douay-Rheims: Isaias)",
 lede:"Isaiah is called 'the fifth Gospel' because no Old Testament book speaks so clearly of Christ: the virgin who conceives Emmanuel, the child called Wonderful Counsellor, the suffering Servant, and the new heavens and new earth.",
 story:`<p><strong>Chapters 1–39.</strong> Isaiah, a Jerusalem prophet, sees the Lord enthroned in the Temple with seraphim crying 'Holy, holy, holy' (ch. 6) and is sent to a people who will not listen. He confronts King Ahaz, who fears an alliance of Syria and Israel, offering a sign: 'a virgin shall conceive, and bear a son, and his name shall be called Emmanuel' (7:14). He announces a royal child (9:6) and a shoot from the stump of Jesse on whom the Spirit rests (11:1–2). Oracles against the nations follow, then the 'Isaiah apocalypse' (24–27). When Assyria besieges Jerusalem, Isaiah encourages King Hezekiah, and the city is spared (36–39).</p>
<p><strong>Chapters 40–55.</strong> 'Be comforted, be comforted, my people' (40:1): the exile is ending. God, the only Creator, will bring his people home in a new Exodus, using Cyrus as his instrument. Four 'Servant Songs' describe a mysterious Servant who brings justice to the nations and who is 'wounded for our iniquities' and 'led as a sheep to the slaughter' (52:13–53:12).</p>
<p><strong>Chapters 56–66.</strong> After the return, the prophet calls for true worship and justice, announces good news to the poor (61:1–2), and envisions Jerusalem's glory and 'new heavens and a new earth' (65:17).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,12,"Judgment and hope for Judah; Emmanuel and the Davidic child"],[13,23,"Oracles against the nations"],[24,27,"The 'Isaiah apocalypse'"],[28,35,"Woes and promises"],[36,39,"Hezekiah and Assyria"],[40,55,"The Book of Consolation; the Servant Songs"],[56,66,"Restoration, true worship, new creation"]],
 people:[["Isaiah son of Amos","A prophet of the Jerusalem court, married with children whose names are prophetic signs.","His vision of God's holiness (ch. 6) shapes Christian worship: the Sanctus of every Mass."],["Ahaz","King of Judah who refuses to trust God and asks for no sign.","The occasion of the Emmanuel prophecy."],["Hezekiah","A faithful king who trusts God against Assyria.","Prayer answered (chs 37–38)."],["The Servant of the Lord","Chosen, Spirit-filled, suffering and vindicated.","Identified by the New Testament with Jesus (Acts 8:32–35)."],["Cyrus","The Persian king called God's 'anointed' (45:1).","God directs history even through those who do not know him."]],
 themes:[["Holiness","God is 'the Holy One of Israel', Isaiah's favourite title."],["Faith","'If you will not believe, you shall not continue' (7:9)."],["Messiah","King from David's line, Emmanuel."],["Suffering Servant","Vicarious, atoning suffering."],["Universal salvation","All nations stream to Zion (2:2–4; 56:7)."]],
 christ:"The New Testament quotes Isaiah more than any other prophet. Matthew applies 7:14 to the virgin birth (Mt 1:23). John the Baptist is 'the voice of one crying in the wilderness' (40:3). Jesus begins his ministry reading 61:1–2 in Nazareth: 'This day is fulfilled this scripture' (Lk 4:21). Isaiah 53 is the clearest Old Testament prophecy of the Passion, explained by Philip to the Ethiopian (Acts 8). The ox and ass of the crib come from 1:3.",
 church:"Isaiah 7:14 underpins the doctrine of the virginal conception (CCC 497); 11:1–2 is the source of the seven gifts of the Holy Spirit (CCC 1831, following the Greek and Latin text); Isaiah 53 grounds the Church's teaching on Christ's atoning death (CCC 601, 615). Isaiah is read throughout Advent.",
 thomas:"Thomas wrote a literal commentary on Isaiah (Expositio super Isaiam ad litteram, c. 1249–52), one of his earliest works. Jerome's eighteen-book commentary is the great patristic study; Jerome said 'ignorance of Scripture is ignorance of Christ' in its prologue.",
 hard:[["Does 'almah' mean virgin or young woman?","The Hebrew 'almah means a young woman of marriageable age; the Greek Septuagint, translated by Jews centuries before Christ, rendered it parthenos, 'virgin'. Matthew follows the Septuagint. The Church reads the sign as having its full meaning in Christ, whatever its first reference in Ahaz's day."],["Was the whole book written by Isaiah?","Most scholars see chapters 40–66 as the work of later prophets in Isaiah's tradition, since they address the exile directly and name Cyrus. The Church does not require single authorship; the whole book is inspired."]],
 keys:[["Isa 6:1–8","the call of Isaiah; 'Holy, holy, holy'"],["Isa 7:14","Emmanuel"],["Isa 9:6–7","'a child is born to us'"],["Isa 11:1–9","the shoot of Jesse; the gifts of the Spirit"],["Isa 40:1–11","comfort, my people"],["Isa 52:13–53:12","the suffering Servant"],["Isa 55:1–11","come to the waters; my word shall not return void"],["Isa 61:1–3","the Spirit of the Lord is upon me"],["Isa 65:17","new heavens and a new earth"]],
 author:"Isaiah son of Amos for much of chs 1–39; chs 40–55 and 56–66 are usually attributed to disciples during and after the exile."},

jeremiah:{era:"divided",when:"627 BC to after 587 BC",genre:"Prophecy and biography (Douay-Rheims: Jeremias)",
 lede:"Jeremiah prophesied through Judah's final collapse. Called as a youth, he was rejected, beaten, imprisoned and thrown into a cistern for warning that Jerusalem would fall. He also gave the Old Testament's great promise of a new covenant.",
 story:`<p>God calls Jeremiah, a priest's son from Anathoth: 'Before I formed thee in the bowels of thy mother, I knew thee' (1:5). He condemns Judah's idolatry and injustice and its trust in the Temple as a charm (ch. 7). His 'confessions' reveal his inner anguish: 'Thou hast deceived me, O Lord… I am become a laughingstock all the day' (20:7).</p>
<p>He acts out his messages: a ruined loincloth, a smashed pot, a yoke on his neck. He tells the exiles in Babylon to settle and 'seek the peace of the city' (29:7) and promises return after seventy years. In the 'Book of Consolation' (30–33) he announces a new covenant written on the heart (31:31–34) and buys a field as a sign of hope. Kings burn his scroll; officials throw him into a muddy cistern. Jerusalem falls in 587 BC. Jeremiah is taken to Egypt against his will, where tradition says he died. Oracles against the nations and a historical appendix close the book.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"The call"],[2,25,"Oracles of judgment; Jeremiah's confessions"],[26,29,"Conflict with priests and prophets; letter to the exiles"],[30,33,"The Book of Consolation: the new covenant"],[34,45,"Jerusalem's last days and aftermath"],[46,51,"Oracles against the nations"],[52,52,"Historical appendix: the fall"]],
 people:[["Jeremiah","Prophet called young, forbidden to marry, constantly opposed.","The suffering prophet whose life foreshadows Christ's."],["Baruch","Jeremiah's scribe who writes and reads his scroll (ch. 36).","The faithful assistant; a book bears his name."],["Zedekiah","Judah's last king, who consults Jeremiah secretly but lacks courage.","Knowing the truth and failing to act on it."],["Hananiah","A false prophet promising quick deliverance (ch. 28).","Discerning true and false prophecy."],["Ebed-melech","An Ethiopian official who rescues Jeremiah from the cistern (38:7–13).","Courageous mercy from a foreigner."]],
 themes:[["Heart religion","The heart is the place of sin and of renewal."],["False security","Trust in the Temple without conversion is empty."],["Suffering in the vocation","The prophet bears the word's cost."],["New covenant","The Law written on hearts, sins forgiven (31:31–34)."],["Hope beyond judgment","Exile is not the end."]],
 christ:"Jesus quotes Jeremiah when cleansing the Temple ('a den of thieves', Jer 7:11; Mt 21:13). At the Last Supper he speaks of 'the new covenant in my blood' (Lk 22:20), fulfilling 31:31; Hebrews 8 quotes the prophecy in full. Rachel weeping for her children (31:15) is applied to the Holy Innocents (Mt 2:18). Jeremiah's rejection and suffering made some think Jesus was Jeremiah returned (Mt 16:14).",
 church:"Jeremiah 1:5 is a key text for the dignity of the unborn (CCC 2270). The new covenant (31:31–34) is fulfilled in the Eucharist and the gift of the Spirit (CCC 715, 762). Jeremiah models prophetic courage and the honest prayer of a suffering servant (CCC 2584).",
 thomas:"Thomas wrote a literal commentary on Jeremiah (chs 1–42) and on Lamentations, early in his career. He uses Jeremiah 1:5 when discussing Jeremiah's sanctification in the womb, while holding that such sanctification did not remove original sin as the Immaculate Conception does (ST III q.27 a.6).",
 keys:[["Jer 1:4–10","the call"],["Jer 7:1–15","the Temple sermon"],["Jer 18","the potter"],["Jer 20:7–18","'thou hast deceived me'"],["Jer 29:11–14","plans for peace, not affliction"],["Jer 31:31–34","the new covenant"]],
 author:"Jeremiah's oracles, written down by Baruch (ch. 36), with biographical material; the Greek text is about one-eighth shorter and arranged differently."},

lamentations:{era:"exile",when:"Shortly after the destruction of Jerusalem (587/586 BC)",genre:"Poetic laments",
 lede:"Lamentations is five poems of grief over the destroyed city of Jerusalem. Four are alphabetical acrostics, as if to grieve 'from A to Z'. At its centre is a cry of hope: 'The mercies of the Lord that we are not consumed' (3:22).",
 story:`<p>Jerusalem, personified as a widow, weeps alone at night: 'O all ye that pass by the way, attend, and see if there be any sorrow like to my sorrow' (1:12). The poems describe famine, children dying in the streets, priests and prophets slain, and the Temple desecrated, and they confess that this is the fruit of sin.</p>
<p>In chapter 3 a single sufferer speaks of being broken by God, yet remembers God's faithfulness: his mercies are new every morning. The last poem is a communal prayer ending with a plea: 'Convert us, O Lord, to thee, and we shall be converted' (5:21).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"The widowed city"],[2,2,"The Lord's anger"],[3,3,"The man who has seen affliction; hope"],[4,4,"Then and now"],[5,5,"A communal prayer"]],
 people:[["Daughter Zion","Jerusalem personified as a grieving woman.","Read in the liturgy of Holy Week and applied to Mary at the Cross ('see if there be any sorrow like to my sorrow')."],["The afflicted man (ch. 3)","An individual sufferer who represents the people.","Prefigures the suffering Christ."]],
 themes:[["Grief brought to God","Lament as faithful prayer."],["Sin and its consequences","Honest confession."],["Hope within sorrow","God's mercies do not fail."]],
 christ:"Lamentations is sung at Tenebrae in Holy Week, where Christ's Passion is mourned in Jerusalem's words, each lesson ending 'Jerusalem, Jerusalem, return to the Lord thy God'. Lam 1:12 is applied to the Crucified and to Our Lady of Sorrows.",
 church:"The book teaches that grief and lament are part of faith, not opposed to it. It is a resource for the pastoral care of the bereaved and of communities after disaster.",
 thomas:"Thomas wrote a short literal commentary on Lamentations. The Septuagint introduces the book: 'Jeremias sat weeping, and mourned with this lamentation over Jerusalem', reflecting the tradition of his authorship.",
 keys:[["Lam 1:12","is there any sorrow like my sorrow?"],["Lam 3:22–33","the Lord's mercies; it is good to wait"],["Lam 5:21","convert us, O Lord"]],
 author:"Traditionally Jeremiah (following the Septuagint preface); many scholars think of one or more poets in Judah after 587 BC."},

baruch:{era:"exile",when:"Set in Babylon after 587 BC; written perhaps 2nd century BC",genre:"Prayer, wisdom poem and exhortation (deuterocanonical)",
 lede:"Baruch, presented as the work of Jeremiah's scribe in exile, contains a prayer of confession, a poem identifying Wisdom with the Law, words of comfort for Jerusalem, and (chapter 6) the Letter of Jeremiah against idols.",
 story:`<p>Baruch reads his book to the exiles in Babylon, who weep, fast and send money for sacrifices in Jerusalem. Their prayer confesses: 'to the Lord our God belongeth justice, but to us confusion of our face' (1:15).</p>
<p>A wisdom poem asks where wisdom is found and answers that God found it and gave it to Israel: 'This is the book of the commandments of God, and the law, that is for ever' (4:1). Jerusalem, a mourning mother, comforts her exiled children and is told to put off her mourning and see her children gathered from east and west (4:5–5:9). Chapter 6, the Letter of Jeremiah, mocks idols that cannot see, speak or save.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,3,"Introduction and prayer of confession"],[3,4,"Wisdom poem: wisdom is the Law"],[4,5,"Comfort for Jerusalem"],[6,6,"The Letter of Jeremiah against idols"]],
 people:[["Baruch son of Neriah","Jeremiah's scribe and companion (Jer 36; 45).","Faithful service to God's word in exile."],["Jerusalem as mother","Weeping and then rejoicing over her children.","Image of the Church gathering her children."]],
 themes:[["Confession of sin","Justice belongs to God, shame to us."],["Wisdom given to Israel","The Law as God's gift of wisdom."],["Return and joy","Jerusalem, look toward the east."],["Folly of idolatry","Idols are nothing."]],
 christ:"Baruch 3:38 ('Afterwards he was seen upon earth, and conversed with men') was read by the Fathers as a prophecy of the Incarnation of Wisdom.",
 church:"Baruch is part of the Catholic canon (Trent) and is read at the Easter Vigil (3:9–15, 32–4:4) and in Advent (5:1–9: 'Arise, O Jerusalem').",
 thomas:"Thomas's inaugural lecture as a bachelor of Scripture in Paris (c. 1252), 'Hic est liber', on Baruch 4:1, praises Sacred Scripture as the book of God's commandments and divides the whole Bible. Ambrose and Cyril of Jerusalem cite Baruch 3:38 for the Incarnation.",
 keys:[["Bar 1:15–2:10","the confession"],["Bar 3:9–4:4","where is wisdom?"],["Bar 3:38","seen upon earth"],["Bar 5","Arise, O Jerusalem"]],
 author:"Attributed to Baruch; most scholars date the present book to the 2nd century BC, composed from several pieces."},

ezekiel:{era:"exile",when:"593–571 BC among the exiles in Babylon",genre:"Prophecy and visions (Douay-Rheims: Ezechiel)",
 lede:"Ezekiel, a priest deported to Babylon, sees God's glory on a chariot-throne far from the Temple, watches that glory leave Jerusalem, and announces God's promise of a new heart, a new spirit, risen dry bones and a new temple from which life-giving water flows.",
 story:`<p>By the river Chobar (Chebar) Ezekiel sees four living creatures and wheels within wheels bearing the glory of God (ch. 1). He eats a scroll and is made a watchman for Israel. He performs strange sign-acts: lying on his side for days, shaving his head, packing an exile's bag. In a vision he is taken to Jerusalem, sees idolatry in the Temple, and watches the glory depart east (chs 8–11).</p>
<p>After news of Jerusalem's fall, his message turns to hope. God will be Israel's shepherd and raise up a new David (ch. 34); he will give a new heart and put his spirit within them (36:26–27); a valley of dry bones comes to life (ch. 37). The book ends with a vision of a new temple and a river flowing from it that makes the Dead Sea fresh (40–48), and the city's new name: 'The Lord is there'.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,3,"The vision of glory and the call"],[4,24,"Judgment on Jerusalem; the glory departs"],[25,32,"Oracles against the nations"],[33,39,"Restoration: shepherd, new heart, dry bones, Gog"],[40,48,"The new temple and the river of life"]],
 people:[["Ezekiel son of Buzi","A priest exiled in 597 BC with King Jehoiachin; his wife dies as a sign (24:15–18).","The prophet of God's glory and of interior renewal; called 'son of man' over ninety times."],["The four living creatures","Faces of man, lion, ox and eagle (ch. 1).","Tradition assigns them to the four Evangelists."],["The bad shepherds","Leaders who feed themselves (ch. 34).","God promises to shepherd his people himself."]],
 themes:[["Glory","God's presence is not bound to a building."],["Personal responsibility","'The soul that sinneth, the same shall die' (18:4); each is judged for his own conduct."],["New heart and spirit","Interior transformation by God."],["Resurrection","The dry bones live."],["The new temple","Water of life flowing to the world."]],
 christ:"Jesus is the Good Shepherd promised in Ezekiel 34 (Jn 10). The water flowing from the Temple's side (47:1) is sung in the Easter antiphon Vidi aquam and fulfilled in the water from Christ's pierced side (Jn 19:34). The Fathers read the closed east gate through which only the Lord enters (44:2) as a sign of Mary's perpetual virginity.",
 church:"Ezekiel 36:25–27 ('I will pour upon you clean water… and I will put a new spirit within you') is read at the Easter Vigil and is a classic text for baptism and the gift of the Spirit. Ezekiel 18 underlies the teaching on personal responsibility for sin.",
 thomas:"Gregory the Great's Homilies on Ezekiel are a classic of the spiritual reading of Scripture. Irenaeus (Against Heresies III.11.8) and Jerome (prologue to his Commentary on Matthew) linked the four creatures to the four Gospels; Jerome's assignment (Matthew the man, Mark the lion, Luke the ox, John the eagle) became standard in Christian art.",
 keys:[["Ezek 1","the chariot-throne"],["Ezek 18","personal responsibility"],["Ezek 34","the shepherds and the Good Shepherd"],["Ezek 36:25–28","clean water, new heart"],["Ezek 37:1–14","the dry bones"],["Ezek 47:1–12","the river from the temple"]],
 author:"Ezekiel, with editing by disciples."},

daniel:{era:"exile",when:"Set in Babylon and Persia, 605–c. 535 BC; final form c. 165 BC",genre:"Court stories and apocalyptic visions (Catholic Bibles include the Greek parts)",
 lede:"Daniel combines stories of young Jews faithful in a pagan court (the fiery furnace, the lions' den) with visions of world empires and the everlasting kingdom given to 'one like the son of man'.",
 story:`<p>Daniel and three friends are taken to Babylon and trained for royal service, but refuse the king's food and flourish. Daniel interprets Nebuchadnezzar's dream of a statue of four metals struck by a stone that becomes a mountain. The three friends refuse to worship a golden statue and are thrown into a furnace; a fourth figure walks with them, and in the Greek text they sing the great canticle of creation praising God (3:24–90). Nebuchadnezzar is humbled; Belshazzar sees the writing on the wall; Daniel, praying three times a day despite a ban, is thrown to the lions and protected.</p>
<p>In chapters 7–12 Daniel sees four beasts from the sea and 'one like the son of man' coming with the clouds of heaven to receive an everlasting kingdom (7:13–14). Visions of rams, goats, seventy weeks and the 'abomination of desolation' follow, ending with the resurrection of the dead (12:2). The Greek chapters 13–14 tell how Daniel rescues the chaste Susanna from false accusers and exposes the priests of Bel.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,6,"Stories in the royal court"],[7,12,"Visions of the kingdoms and the end"],[13,14,"Susanna; Bel and the Dragon (Greek additions)"]],
 people:[["Daniel (Baltassar)","A young Judean noble, wise and prayerful, interpreter of dreams.","Faithful in public life under hostile rulers."],["Ananias, Misael and Azarias (Shadrach, Meshach, Abednego)","Daniel's friends who refuse idolatry.","'Our God… is able to save us… But if he will not… we will not worship thy gods' (3:17–18)."],["Nebuchadnezzar","The Babylonian king, proud then humbled.","God gives and takes kingdoms."],["Susanna","A chaste woman falsely accused and vindicated (ch. 13).","Innocence defended; a model of chastity."],["The son of man","A heavenly figure given everlasting dominion.","Jesus' chosen title for himself."]],
 themes:[["Faithfulness under pressure","Refusing compromise in food, worship and prayer."],["God's sovereignty over empires","Kingdoms rise and fall; God's kingdom endures."],["Apocalyptic hope","History moves toward God's judgment and kingdom."],["Resurrection","'Many of those that sleep in the dust… shall awake' (12:2)."]],
 christ:"'Son of man' is Jesus' favourite self-description; at his trial he quotes Daniel 7:13 ('you shall see the Son of man… coming in the clouds of heaven', Mk 14:62), which the high priest takes as blasphemy. The stone 'cut out without hands' (2:34) was read by the Fathers as Christ born of a virgin. Jesus speaks of the 'abomination of desolation' (Mt 24:15).",
 church:"Daniel 12:2 is one of the clearest Old Testament texts on bodily resurrection and eternal reward and punishment. The canticle of the three young men (Benedicite) is prayed in Lauds on Sundays and feasts. The Greek portions are canonical for Catholics (Trent).",
 thomas:"Thomas uses Daniel's experiences (Nebuchadnezzar's dream, the writing on the wall, the need for 'understanding in a vision', 10:1) to explain how prophetic knowledge works through images and intellectual light (ST II-II q.173 aa.2–3). Jerome's Commentary on Daniel answers the pagan Porphyry, who first argued the visions were written after the events.",
 hard:[["Was Daniel written in the 6th or 2nd century BC?","Most scholars date the final form to c. 165 BC, during Antiochus IV's persecution, which chs 8–11 describe in detail. Traditional readers hold that Daniel wrote in the 6th century. Catholics may hold either view; the inspired message about God's sovereignty and the coming kingdom is the same."]],
 keys:[["Dan 2:31–45","the statue and the stone"],["Dan 3","the fiery furnace and the canticle"],["Dan 6","the lions' den"],["Dan 7:13–14","the son of man"],["Dan 9:24–27","the seventy weeks"],["Dan 12:1–3","resurrection"],["Dan 13","Susanna"]],
 author:"Traditionally Daniel; the book is written partly in Hebrew, partly in Aramaic (2:4–7:28), with Greek additions."},

hosea:{era:"divided",when:"Northern kingdom, c. 750–725 BC",genre:"Prophecy (Douay-Rheims: Osee)",
 lede:"Hosea is commanded to marry an unfaithful wife and keep loving her. His broken marriage becomes a living parable of God's love for unfaithful Israel: wounded, jealous, and unwilling to let go.",
 story:`<p>Hosea marries Gomer, who is unfaithful. Their children receive names of judgment: Jezrahel, 'Without Mercy', 'Not My People'. Yet God promises to woo Israel again in the desert, betroth her 'in faith' (2:20), and rename the children 'Mercy' and 'My People'. Hosea buys Gomer back and waits for her (ch. 3).</p>
<p>The rest of the book indicts Israel: no truth, no mercy, no knowledge of God; priests who feed on sin; alliances with Egypt and Assyria; calf-worship. Yet God's heart recoils from destroying them: 'When Israel was a child, I loved him: and I called my son out of Egypt… How shall I deal with thee, O Ephraim?' (11:1, 8). The book ends with an invitation to return and a promise of healing.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,3,"Hosea's marriage as a sign"],[4,10,"Indictment of Israel"],[11,11,"God's fatherly love"],[12,14,"Judgment and the call to return"]],
 people:[["Hosea son of Beeri","A northern prophet whose own marriage becomes his message.","God's love is costly and faithful."],["Gomer","His unfaithful wife, redeemed and restored.","Israel, and every soul, loved despite infidelity."]],
 themes:[["Covenant as marriage","Idolatry is adultery."],["Hesed and knowledge of God","'I desired mercy, and not sacrifice: and the knowledge of God more than holocausts' (6:6)."],["Divine compassion","God's love overcomes his anger (11:8–9)."],["Return","'Return, O Israel, to the Lord thy God' (14:2)."]],
 christ:"Matthew applies 11:1, 'Out of Egypt have I called my son', to the Holy Family's return (Mt 2:15). Jesus twice quotes 6:6 against legalism (Mt 9:13; 12:7). Paul quotes 13:14 in his hymn of resurrection victory: 'O death, where is thy victory?' (1 Cor 15:55).",
 church:"Hosea grounds the nuptial understanding of the covenant (CCC 1611, on the prophets' image of exclusive faithful love), and of God's love as both fatherly and spousal (CCC 218–219). It is a key text for understanding mercy and conversion.",
 thomas:"Jerome's commentary on the twelve minor prophets and Theodore of Mopsuestia's and Cyril of Alexandria's are the major patristic works.",
 hard:[["Did God really command Hosea to marry a prostitute?","Interpreters differ: some think Gomer became unfaithful after marriage, others read the account as a vision or parable. Either way, the point is God's faithful love for an unfaithful people."]],
 keys:[["Hos 2:14–20","I will allure her into the wilderness"],["Hos 6:6","mercy, not sacrifice"],["Hos 11:1–9","out of Egypt I called my son"],["Hos 14","return to the Lord"]],
 author:"Hosea, with later editing in Judah."},

joel:{era:"divided",when:"Date uncertain (proposals range from the 9th to the 4th century BC)",genre:"Prophecy",
 lede:"A devastating locust plague becomes for Joel a sign of 'the day of the Lord'. He calls for fasting and repentance and promises the outpouring of the Spirit on all flesh, fulfilled at Pentecost.",
 story:`<p>Locusts have stripped the land; there is no grain or wine for offerings. Joel calls priests and people to mourn, fast and assemble: 'rend your hearts, and not your garments, and turn to the Lord your God: for he is gracious and merciful' (2:13).</p>
<p>God responds with pity, restores the land and promises: 'I will pour out my spirit upon all flesh: and your sons and your daughters shall prophesy' (2:28). The book ends with judgment on the nations in the valley of Josaphat and blessing for Zion.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"The locust plague"],[2,2,"The day of the Lord; call to repentance; promise of the Spirit"],[3,3,"Judgment of the nations"]],
 people:[["Joel son of Phatuel","A prophet of Judah, perhaps linked with the Temple.","Liturgical repentance and Pentecostal hope."]],
 themes:[["Day of the Lord","Judgment and salvation."],["Communal repentance","A solemn fast."],["The Spirit for all","Not only for prophets and kings."]],
 christ:"Peter explains Pentecost by quoting Joel 2:28–32 (Acts 2:16–21). Paul quotes 2:32: 'whosoever shall call upon the name of the Lord, shall be saved' (Rom 10:13).",
 church:"Joel 2:12–18 is the first reading on Ash Wednesday. Its promise of the Spirit underlies the Church's teaching on Pentecost and the sacrament of confirmation (CCC 1287).",
 thomas:"Thomas cites Joel 2:13, 'rend your hearts and not your garments', to show that penance consists chiefly in the heart rather than in outward signs (ST III q.84).",
 keys:[["Joel 2:12–13","rend your hearts"],["Joel 2:28–32","I will pour out my spirit"]],
 author:"Joel son of Phatuel (Pethuel)."},

amos:{era:"divided",when:"c. 760–750 BC, under Jeroboam II of Israel",genre:"Prophecy",
 lede:"Amos, a shepherd and fig-dresser from Judah, is sent north to prosperous Israel to denounce injustice, luxury and empty worship. He is the Bible's great prophet of social justice.",
 story:`<p>Amos begins with oracles against Israel's neighbours, then turns the same judgment on Judah and Israel, who 'sold the just man for silver, and the poor man for a pair of shoes' (2:6). He condemns the rich women of Samaria, the luxurious beds of ivory, and festivals without justice: 'judgment shall be revealed as water, and justice as a mighty torrent' (5:24).</p>
<p>Five visions (locusts, fire, a plumb line, a basket of summer fruit, the Lord at the altar) announce the end. The priest Amasias (Amaziah) of Bethel orders Amos to go home; Amos replies, 'I am not a prophet… but I am a herdsman… And the Lord took me' (7:14–15). The last verses promise that God will raise up 'the tabernacle of David, that is fallen'.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,2,"Oracles against the nations, then Israel"],[3,6,"Indictments of Israel"],[7,9,"Five visions and the confrontation at Bethel"],[9,9,"Restoration of David's fallen tent"]],
 people:[["Amos","A layman from Tekoa called from his flocks.","God calls ordinary people to speak truth to power."],["Amasias (Amaziah)","Priest of the royal shrine at Bethel who silences Amos.","Religion captured by political power."],["Jeroboam II","King of Israel in a time of wealth and inequality.","Prosperity without justice."]],
 themes:[["Justice for the poor","Exploitation is a sin against God."],["Worship and justice","God rejects liturgy without righteousness."],["Election means responsibility","'You only have I known… therefore I will visit upon you all your iniquities' (3:2)."],["Famine of the word","A hunger for hearing the word of the Lord (8:11)."]],
 christ:"James quotes Amos 9:11–12 at the Council of Jerusalem to show that the Gentiles' inclusion fulfils God's plan to rebuild David's tent (Acts 15:16–17).",
 church:"Amos is foundational for Catholic social teaching on justice for the poor and the universal destination of goods (CCC 2443–2449). His critique of worship divorced from justice echoes in the Church's teaching that the Eucharist commits us to the poor (CCC 1397).",
 thomas:"Thomas cites Amos 3:7 ('the Lord God doth nothing without revealing his secret to his servants the prophets') in his treatise on prophecy (ST II-II qq.171–172).",
 keys:[["Amos 2:6–8","selling the poor"],["Amos 3:2","you only have I known"],["Amos 5:21–24","justice as a mighty torrent"],["Amos 7:14–15","I am no prophet"],["Amos 8:11","a famine of hearing the word"],["Amos 9:11–15","the fallen tabernacle of David"]],
 author:"Amos of Tekoa."},

obadiah:{era:"exile",when:"Shortly after 587 BC",genre:"Prophecy (Douay-Rheims: Abdias)",
 lede:"Obadiah, the shortest book of the Old Testament (21 verses), condemns Edom for gloating over and plundering Jerusalem in its fall, and announces that 'the kingdom shall be the Lord's'.",
 story:`<p>Edom, descended from Esau, Jacob's brother, lives proudly in the rocky heights south of the Dead Sea. When Babylon sacked Jerusalem, Edom stood aside, rejoiced, looted and handed over fugitives. Obadiah announces that Edom's pride will bring it down: 'as thou hast done, so shall it be done to thee' (v. 15). The day of the Lord will restore Israel, and 'the kingdom shall be for the Lord' (v. 21).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"The whole book: Edom's pride, sin and judgment; Israel's restoration"]],
 people:[["Edom","Esau's descendants, Israel's 'brother' nation.","Betrayal by a brother is especially grave."]],
 themes:[["Pride","The proud heart deceived by its own security."],["Brotherhood betrayed","Standing by while a brother suffers."],["Reciprocal justice","What you do returns on you."],["God's kingdom","The final word belongs to the Lord."]],
 christ:"The kingdom that 'shall be for the Lord' is proclaimed by Jesus as the Kingdom of God. The Herods were Idumeans (Edomites), and the Fathers saw the conflict of Esau and Jacob replayed in Herod's hostility to the infant Christ.",
 church:"Obadiah warns against the sin of indifference and gloating at others' misfortune, a sin of omission against charity (CCC 1868 on cooperation in others' sins).",
 thomas:"Jerome comments on Obadiah in his Commentary on the Minor Prophets, reading Edom spiritually as the proud and earthly-minded.",
 keys:[["Obad 3–4","pride deceives"],["Obad 10–14","standing aloof"],["Obad 15","as thou hast done"],["Obad 21","the kingdom shall be the Lord's"]],
 author:"Obadiah, otherwise unknown."},

jonah:{era:"divided",when:"Set in the 8th century BC (2 Kgs 14:25); written probably after the exile",genre:"Prophetic narrative (Douay-Rheims: Jonas)",
 lede:"Jonah is a prophet who runs from God, is swallowed by a great fish, preaches reluctantly to Nineveh, and is angry when his enemies repent. The book is about the width of God's mercy.",
 story:`<p>God sends Jonah to preach against Nineveh, capital of Assyria, Israel's brutal enemy. Jonah boards a ship to Tarshish in the opposite direction. A storm rises; pagan sailors pray while Jonah sleeps; he admits his guilt and is thrown overboard. A great fish swallows him; after three days and nights, having prayed from its belly, he is cast onto dry land.</p>
<p>Jonah preaches one sentence: 'Yet forty days, and Ninive shall be destroyed'. The whole city, from the king to the cattle, fasts in sackcloth, and God relents. Jonah is furious: 'I know that thou art a gracious and merciful God' (4:2). God grows a plant to shade him, then lets a worm kill it, and asks: should I not spare Nineveh, with more than 120,000 people who 'know not how to distinguish between their right hand and their left'? The book ends with God's question unanswered.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"Jonah flees; the storm"],[2,2,"Jonah's prayer in the fish"],[3,3,"Nineveh repents"],[4,4,"Jonah's anger and God's question"]],
 people:[["Jonah son of Amathi","A historical northern prophet (2 Kgs 14:25), portrayed as reluctant and resentful.","A mirror for believers who want mercy for themselves but not for enemies."],["The sailors","Pagans who pray and fear the Lord.","Outsiders often more open to God than insiders."],["The Ninevites","Israel's enemies, who repent.","God wills the conversion of all peoples."]],
 themes:[["God's universal mercy","God cares for Gentiles and even their animals."],["Repentance","Even a violent empire can turn."],["Obedience and flight","One cannot outrun God's call."],["Prophetic resentment","The danger of a narrow heart."]],
 christ:"Jesus calls his resurrection 'the sign of Jonas the prophet': 'as Jonas was in the whale's belly three days and three nights: so shall the Son of man be in the heart of the earth' (Mt 12:39–41). Jonah emerging from the fish was one of the most common images in early Christian catacomb art.",
 church:"Jonah is read in Lent as a call to conversion and a sign of the resurrection. Its teaching that God desires the conversion of all (cf. 1 Tim 2:4) underlies the Church's missionary mandate.",
 thomas:"Thomas uses Jonah's 'Yet forty days, and Nineveh shall be destroyed' to explain that prophecies of threat (comminatio) foretell what a people's merits deserve, and so depend on their response (ST II-II q.171 a.6 ad 2).",
 hard:[["Did Jonah really live inside a fish?","The Church allows both a historical reading and a reading of Jonah as a didactic story (a 'prophetic parable'). Jesus' use of 'the sign of Jonah' works on either reading, since he cites the book's well-known account as Scripture. The book's point is God's mercy, not marine biology."]],
 keys:[["Jonah 1:3","flight to Tarshish"],["Jonah 2","the prayer from the deep"],["Jonah 3:4–10","Nineveh repents"],["Jonah 4:2","a gracious and merciful God"],["Jonah 4:10–11","should I not spare Nineveh?"]],
 author:"Anonymous; probably written after the exile."},

micah:{era:"divided",when:"c. 735–700 BC, contemporary with Isaiah",genre:"Prophecy (Douay-Rheims: Micheas)",
 lede:"Micah, a country prophet from Moresheth, condemns the land-grabbing rich and corrupt leaders of Judah and Samaria, foretells the Messiah's birth in Bethlehem, and sums up true religion in one verse: do justice, love mercy, walk humbly with God.",
 story:`<p>Micah announces judgment on Samaria and Jerusalem for idolatry and for the rich who 'covet fields, and take them by violence' (2:2). He confronts false prophets who preach peace for pay and rulers who 'eat the flesh of my people'. Zion will be ploughed like a field (3:12), a prophecy remembered a century later in Jeremiah's trial (Jer 26:18).</p>
<p>Yet a future is promised: nations will stream to the mountain of the Lord and beat swords into ploughshares (4:1–3). From Bethlehem Ephrata, 'a little one among the thousands of Juda', will come a ruler 'whose going forth is from the beginning, from the days of eternity' (5:2). God brings a lawsuit against his people: 'O my people, what have I done to thee?' (6:3), and answers what he requires (6:8). The book ends praising God who 'delighteth in mercy' and casts sins into the depths of the sea (7:18–19).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,3,"Judgment on Samaria and Jerusalem"],[4,5,"Zion's future and the ruler from Bethlehem"],[6,7,"God's lawsuit; confession and hope"]],
 people:[["Micah of Moresheth","A rural prophet, champion of small farmers.","A voice for the poor against the powerful."],["The ruler from Bethlehem","A shepherd-king of eternal origin.","Fulfilled in Christ (Mt 2:6)."]],
 themes:[["Justice","Against land-grabbing and bribery."],["False prophecy","Preaching for profit."],["Messianic peace","Swords into ploughshares."],["True religion","6:8."],["Mercy","Who is a God like thee?"]],
 christ:"The chief priests quote Micah 5:2 to Herod: the Messiah will be born in Bethlehem (Mt 2:5–6). Jesus' words about enmity within households (Mt 10:35–36) echo Micah 7:6.",
 church:"The Good Friday Reproaches ('My people, what have I done to you?') are built on Micah 6:3–4. Micah 6:8 is a classic summary of the moral life; 4:3 is often quoted in the Church's teaching on peace (CCC 2317).",
 thomas:"Thomas uses Micah 5:2 for Christ's eternal generation and temporal birth (ST III q.35 a.7 on the fittingness of Bethlehem).",
 keys:[["Mic 4:1–4","swords into ploughshares"],["Mic 5:2–5","Bethlehem"],["Mic 6:3–8","what does the Lord require?"],["Mic 7:18–20","who is a God like thee?"]],
 author:"Micah, with later additions likely."},

nahum:{era:"divided",when:"Between 663 BC (fall of Thebes, 3:8) and 612 BC (fall of Nineveh)",genre:"Prophecy (Douay-Rheims: Nahum)",
 lede:"Nahum is a poem celebrating the coming fall of Nineveh, the cruel capital of Assyria. Where Jonah shows God's mercy to a repentant Nineveh, Nahum shows his justice on an unrepentant one.",
 story:`<p>The book opens with a hymn to the Lord who is 'patient, and great in power, and will not cleanse and acquit the guilty' (1:3), a refuge for those who trust him. It then describes in vivid poetry the attack on Nineveh: chariots racing in the streets, gates opened by the rivers, plunder. 'Behold upon the mountains the feet of him that bringeth good tidings, and that preacheth peace' (1:15): oppression is ending. The book closes with a taunt: 'all that have heard the fame of thee, have clapped their hands over thee' (3:19).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"The Lord's justice and goodness"],[2,2,"The fall of Nineveh described"],[3,3,"Woe to the bloody city"]],
 people:[["Nahum the Elcesite","Name means 'comfort'.","Comfort to the oppressed through God's justice."],["Nineveh","Assyria's capital, notorious for cruelty.","Empires built on violence fall."]],
 themes:[["God's justice","He is slow to anger but does not ignore evil."],["Comfort for the oppressed","Good news of peace."],["Fall of tyranny","No empire is beyond judgment."]],
 christ:"'The feet of him that bringeth good tidings' (1:15, also Isa 52:7) is applied by Paul to the preachers of the Gospel (Rom 10:15).",
 church:"Nahum balances Jonah: God's mercy calls to repentance, but persistent, unrepented evil meets justice. Both are part of the full picture of God (CCC 1040 on the Last Judgment revealing God's justice).",
 thomas:"Jerome comments on Nahum in his Commentary on the Minor Prophets.",
 keys:[["Nahum 1:2–8","the Lord's justice and refuge"],["Nahum 1:15","good tidings of peace"],["Nahum 3","woe to the bloody city"]],
 author:"Nahum of Elcesh (location unknown)."},

habakkuk:{era:"divided",when:"c. 605 BC, as Babylon rises",genre:"Prophetic dialogue and psalm (Douay-Rheims: Habacuc)",
 lede:"Habakkuk argues with God: why does he tolerate injustice, and how can he use the even more wicked Babylonians to punish Judah? God's answer contains a sentence that shaped the Reformation debate and Christian theology: 'the just shall live in his faith' (2:4).",
 story:`<p>Habakkuk complains that violence and injustice go unpunished. God replies that he is raising the Chaldeans (Babylonians) to judge Judah. Habakkuk protests: they are worse! He takes his stand on the watchtower to wait. God tells him to write the vision plainly: it will come in its time; meanwhile 'the just shall live in his faith' (2:4). Five woes follow against the oppressor. The book ends with a psalm of trust (ch. 3): even if the fig tree does not blossom and the fields yield nothing, 'I will rejoice in the Lord: and I will joy in God my Jesus' (3:18, DR).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"Two complaints and God's first answer"],[2,2,"Waiting on the watchtower; the just shall live by faith; five woes"],[3,3,"Habakkuk's prayer"]],
 people:[["Habakkuk","A prophet who questions God honestly and ends in trust.","Faith that wrestles and then rests."],["The Chaldeans","Babylon, God's instrument of judgment.","God uses even the wicked, and judges them too."]],
 themes:[["Theodicy","Why does evil prosper?"],["Faith and faithfulness","Living by trust in God's promise."],["Waiting","The vision has its appointed time."],["Joy in loss","Rejoicing in God when everything else fails."]],
 christ:"Paul quotes 2:4 as the heart of the Gospel (Rom 1:17; Gal 3:11), and Hebrews 10:38 applies it to perseverance. The Vulgate of 3:18, 'in Deo Iesu meo', and the Septuagint of 3:2 ('in the midst of two living creatures thou shalt be known'), became part of Christmas tradition and of the crib.",
 church:"The Church reads 'the just shall live by faith' as faith working through charity (Gal 5:6; Trent, Session 6), in contrast to faith alone (CCC 1814–1816, 2005). In Daniel 14 (Greek), Habakkuk is carried by an angel to feed Daniel in the lions' den.",
 thomas:"Thomas comments on Romans 1:17 and Hebrews 10:38, explaining that the just live by faith 'formed' by charity, which gives the soul its spiritual life.",
 keys:[["Hab 1:2–4","how long, O Lord?"],["Hab 2:1–4","the just shall live by faith"],["Hab 2:14","the earth filled with knowledge of the Lord"],["Hab 3:17–19","though the fig tree shall not blossom"]],
 author:"Habakkuk; nothing else is known of him."},

zephaniah:{era:"divided",when:"c. 640–620 BC, under King Josiah",genre:"Prophecy (Douay-Rheims: Sophonias)",
 lede:"Zephaniah announces the 'day of the Lord' as a day of wrath on idolatrous Judah and the nations, then turns to a remnant of 'poor and needy' people and to a song of God rejoicing over Zion. His words gave the Church the Dies irae.",
 story:`<p>Zephaniah, a descendant of King Hezekiah, prophesies early in Josiah's reign, before the great reform. He announces a sweeping judgment: 'That day is a day of wrath, a day of tribulation and distress… a day of clouds and whirlwinds' (1:15). Judgment falls on Philistines, Moab, Ammon, Ethiopia and Assyria, and on Jerusalem's corrupt leaders.</p>
<p>Yet God will leave 'a poor and needy people' who trust in his name (3:12). The book ends with joy: 'Give praise, O daughter of Sion… The Lord thy God in the midst of thee is mighty… he will rejoice over thee with gladness' (3:14–17).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"The day of the Lord against Judah"],[2,2,"Seek the Lord; oracles against the nations"],[3,3,"Jerusalem judged; the remnant; God's song over Zion"]],
 people:[["Zephaniah son of Chusi","A royal descendant prophesying under Josiah.","Possibly an influence on Josiah's reform."],["The anawim","The humble, poor remnant who seek the Lord.","Fulfilled in the 'poor in spirit' (Mt 5:3) and in Mary's Magnificat."]],
 themes:[["Day of the Lord","Judgment on complacency."],["Humility","'Seek the Lord, all ye meek of the earth' (2:3)."],["The faithful remnant","God's poor."],["Divine joy","God rejoices over his people with singing."]],
 christ:"Zephaniah 3:14–17 is echoed in the angel's greeting to Mary: 'Hail [rejoice], full of grace, the Lord is with thee' (Lk 1:28); the Lord 'in the midst of thee' takes flesh in her womb. The anawim spirituality culminates in the Beatitudes.",
 church:"The medieval sequence Dies irae, dies illa (formerly sung at Requiem Masses) takes its opening from Zephaniah 1:15 (Vulgate). Zephaniah 3:14–18 is read in Advent (Gaudete Sunday, Year C).",
 thomas:"Many Fathers and modern exegetes hear Zephaniah's call to the 'daughter of Sion' behind the angel's 'Hail' (literally 'Rejoice') to Mary.",
 keys:[["Zeph 1:14–18","the day of wrath"],["Zeph 2:3","seek the Lord, ye meek"],["Zeph 3:12","a poor and needy people"],["Zeph 3:14–17","rejoice, daughter of Zion"]],
 author:"Zephaniah son of Chusi."},

haggai:{era:"return",when:"520 BC (four dated oracles over four months)",genre:"Prophecy (Douay-Rheims: Aggeus)",
 lede:"Haggai's two short chapters urge the returned exiles to stop putting their own houses first and rebuild the Lord's Temple, promising that its future glory will be greater than the first.",
 story:`<p>Eighteen years after the return the Temple lies in ruins while the people build panelled houses. Their harvests fail and their wages go 'into a bag with holes' (1:6). Haggai tells them: 'Set your hearts upon your ways… build the house' (1:7–8). Governor Zerubbabel and the high priest Jesus (Joshua) respond and work begins.</p>
<p>When the older people weep that the new Temple is nothing like Solomon's, Haggai promises that God will shake the nations, 'and the desired of all nations shall come: and I will fill this house with glory' (2:8, DR), and that its glory will exceed the first. He ends by naming Zerubbabel God's signet ring.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"Rebuild the house; the people obey"],[2,2,"The future glory; blessing; Zerubbabel the signet"]],
 people:[["Haggai","A prophet of the return.","Practical prophecy: getting the work done."],["Zerubbabel","Governor of Davidic descent.","A signet ring: the Davidic promise is not dead (cf. Jer 22:24)."],["Jesus (Joshua) son of Josedec","The high priest.","Priest and king working together."]],
 themes:[["Priorities","God first, then our houses."],["Encouragement","'Take courage… for I am with you' (2:5)."],["Future glory","The Temple's greater glory."]],
 christ:"The 'desired of all nations' (Vulgate: desideratus cunctis gentibus) is sung in Advent as a title of Christ; the greater glory of the second Temple was fulfilled when Christ entered it (Lk 2:27–32). Hebrews 12:26–28 cites Haggai 2:6 on the shaking of heaven and earth.",
 church:"Haggai encourages the building of churches and, more deeply, the building up of the Church as God's temple (1 Cor 3:16).",
 thomas:"The Fathers read the greater glory of the latter house as the Church, filled with Christ's presence.",
 keys:[["Hag 1:2–11","set your hearts upon your ways"],["Hag 2:4–9","the desired of all nations; greater glory"],["Hag 2:23","Zerubbabel, my signet ring"]],
 author:"Haggai's oracles, recorded by himself or a close disciple."},

zechariah:{era:"return",when:"520–518 BC (chs 1–8); chs 9–14 perhaps later",genre:"Visions and prophecy (Douay-Rheims: Zacharias)",
 lede:"Zechariah encourages the rebuilding of the Temple with eight night visions, then announces a humble king riding on a donkey, a pierced one mourned like an only son, and a struck shepherd. No prophet is quoted more in the Passion narratives.",
 story:`<p>In eight night visions Zechariah sees horsemen patrolling the earth, a man measuring Jerusalem, the high priest Jesus (Joshua) clothed in clean garments as Satan accuses him, a golden lampstand fed by two olive trees ('not with an army, nor by might, but by my spirit', 4:6), a flying scroll and a woman in a basket. A crown is placed on the high priest's head as a sign of 'the man whose name is the Orient [the Branch]' (6:12). Questions about fasting lead to a call for true justice and a promise that ten men of every nation will take hold of a Jew's garment saying, 'we will go with you' (8:23).</p>
<p>Chapters 9–14 announce the king coming to Zion 'poor, and riding upon an ass' (9:9), a shepherd paid thirty pieces of silver (11:12–13), the people looking on 'me, whom they have pierced' (12:10), a fountain opened for cleansing (13:1), a shepherd struck and the sheep scattered (13:7), and the Lord king over all the earth (14:9).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,6,"Eight night visions; crowning of the high priest"],[7,8,"True fasting and the joy of the future"],[9,11,"The humble king; the rejected shepherd"],[12,14,"The pierced one; the fountain; the day of the Lord"]],
 people:[["Zechariah son of Barachias","A priest-prophet who returned from Babylon.","Hope through vision."],["Jesus (Joshua) the high priest","Cleansed and crowned in the visions.","A figure of Christ, the Priest-King whose name is the same."],["Zerubbabel","Builder of the Temple by God's Spirit.","God's work is done by grace, not force."],["The Branch (the Orient)","A coming royal and priestly figure.","Messianic title (cf. Jer 23:5; Lk 1:78, 'the Orient from on high')."]],
 themes:[["Temple and Spirit","Rebuilding by God's Spirit."],["Messianic king","Humble and peaceful."],["The rejected shepherd","Betrayed for silver."],["Mourning for the pierced one","Repentance and cleansing."],["Universal kingship","The Lord king over all the earth."]],
 christ:"Jesus enters Jerusalem on a donkey (Mt 21:5; Zech 9:9). Judas's thirty pieces of silver and the potter's field fulfil 11:12–13 (Mt 27:9–10, where Matthew names Jeremiah, combining texts). Jesus quotes 13:7 at the Last Supper: 'I will strike the shepherd' (Mt 26:31). John sees 12:10 fulfilled at the piercing of Christ's side (Jn 19:37; Rev 1:7).",
 church:"Zechariah 12:10 and 13:1 ground the devotion to the pierced Heart of Christ as the fountain of grace (Pius XII, Haurietis Aquas, 1956). 9:9 is read on Palm Sunday.",
 thomas:"Thomas comments on Zech 9:9 in his lectures on Matthew 21, where the Evangelist quotes it for Christ's humble entry into Jerusalem.",
 keys:[["Zech 4:6","not by might, but by my spirit"],["Zech 8:23","we will go with you"],["Zech 9:9–10","thy king cometh, riding upon an ass"],["Zech 11:12–13","thirty pieces of silver"],["Zech 12:10","whom they have pierced"],["Zech 13:7","strike the shepherd"]],
 author:"Zechariah for chs 1–8; many scholars assign 9–14 to later disciples."},

malachi:{era:"return",when:"c. 460–440 BC, before Nehemiah's reforms",genre:"Prophetic disputation (Douay-Rheims: Malachias)",
 lede:"Malachi ('my messenger'), the last book of the Old Testament in Christian Bibles, confronts priests offering blemished sacrifices and people breaking faith in marriage, foretells a pure offering among all nations, and promises a messenger, Elijah, before the day of the Lord.",
 story:`<p>Malachi is built of six disputes in which God makes a statement, the people object ('In what…?'), and God answers. God affirms his love; rebukes priests who offer lame and blind animals; declares that 'from the rising of the sun even to the going down, my name is great among the Gentiles, and in every place there is sacrifice, and there is offered to my name a clean oblation' (1:11); condemns faithlessness to 'the wife of thy youth' (2:14); and calls for honest tithes.</p>
<p>God will send his messenger to prepare the way, and the Lord will suddenly come to his Temple, purifying the sons of Levi like gold in a furnace (3:1–3). The book closes: 'Behold I will send you Elias the prophet, before the coming of the great and dreadful day of the Lord. And he shall turn the heart of the fathers to the children' (4:5–6).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,2,"God's love; corrupt priests; the pure offering; faithless marriages"],[3,3,"The messenger; the refiner's fire; tithes"],[4,4,"The day of the Lord; Elijah"]],
 people:[["Malachi","Perhaps a title ('my messenger') rather than a name.","The last prophetic voice before John the Baptist."],["The priests","Levites who despise God's altar.","Leaders' lax worship dishonours God."],["Elijah","Promised to return before the Lord's day.","Fulfilled in John the Baptist (Mt 11:14; 17:12)."]],
 themes:[["Worthy worship","Offer God your best."],["The pure offering","A universal sacrifice."],["Faithfulness in marriage","God is witness to the marriage covenant."],["The coming Lord and his forerunner","3:1; 4:5."]],
 christ:"Mark opens his Gospel with Malachi 3:1 joined to Isaiah 40:3 (Mk 1:2). Jesus identifies John the Baptist as the promised Elijah (Mt 11:10–14), and the angel says John will go 'in the spirit and power of Elias; that he may turn the hearts of the fathers unto the children' (Lk 1:17).",
 church:"The Council of Trent (Session 22, ch. 1) and the Didache (ch. 14) see Malachi 1:11 fulfilled in the Mass, the clean oblation offered everywhere; Eucharistic Prayer III echoes it ('from the rising of the sun to its setting'). Malachi's defence of the marriage covenant prepares for Jesus' teaching on indissolubility.",
 thomas:"Justin Martyr (Dialogue with Trypho 41) and Irenaeus (Against Heresies IV.17.5) already read Malachi 1:11 as a prophecy of the Eucharist offered by the Church among the nations.",
 keys:[["Mal 1:11","a clean oblation in every place"],["Mal 2:14–16","the wife of thy youth"],["Mal 3:1–3","the messenger and the refiner"],["Mal 4:5–6","Elijah will come"]],
 author:"Anonymous prophet of the Persian period; the DR has 4 chapters (the Hebrew Bible has 3)."}
});

// Book introductions: Gospels and Acts
Object.assign(BOOK_INTRO,{
matthew:{era:"messiah",when:"Jesus' life, c. 6 BC–AD 30; written c. AD 60–90",genre:"Gospel",
 lede:"Matthew presents Jesus as the Messiah, Son of David and Son of Abraham, who fulfils the Law and the prophets, teaches with authority as a new Moses, and founds his Church on Peter. It stood first in the Church's Bible and was the most used Gospel in the early centuries.",
 story:`<p>A genealogy from Abraham through David leads to Jesus, conceived by the Holy Spirit and born of the Virgin Mary; Joseph is told in a dream to name him Jesus, 'for he shall save his people from their sins' (1:21). The Magi worship him; Herod's massacre forces the Holy Family to flee to Egypt. John baptises Jesus; tempted in the desert, Jesus answers with Deuteronomy.</p>
<p>Jesus' ministry in Galilee is organised around five great discourses: the Sermon on the Mount (5–7), the mission of the Twelve (10), the parables of the Kingdom (13), life in the Church (18), and the end of the age (24–25). Between them come miracles, controversies and growing opposition. At Caesarea Philippi Peter confesses, 'Thou art Christ, the Son of the living God', and Jesus answers: 'thou art Peter; and upon this rock I will build my church… And I will give to thee the keys of the kingdom of heaven' (16:16–19).</p>
<p>Jesus enters Jerusalem as the humble king, cleanses the Temple, condemns hypocrisy, institutes the Eucharist, is betrayed, condemned and crucified. Matthew alone reports the guards at the tomb. The risen Jesus meets the Eleven on a mountain in Galilee and sends them: 'Teach ye all nations; baptizing them in the name of the Father, and of the Son, and of the Holy Ghost… behold I am with you all days, even to the consummation of the world' (28:19–20).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,2,"Genealogy and infancy"],[3,4,"Baptism, temptation, first disciples"],[5,7,"Discourse 1: the Sermon on the Mount"],[8,9,"Ten mighty works"],[10,10,"Discourse 2: the mission of the Twelve"],[11,12,"Rising opposition"],[13,13,"Discourse 3: parables of the Kingdom"],[14,17,"Peter's confession; the Transfiguration"],[18,18,"Discourse 4: life in the Church"],[19,23,"Toward and in Jerusalem"],[24,25,"Discourse 5: the end of the age"],[26,28,"Passion, death and resurrection; the Great Commission"]],
 people:[["Jesus","The Messiah, Emmanuel ('God with us', 1:23), teacher with authority, the Son of God.","Matthew's frame: God with us at the start (1:23) and at the end ('I am with you', 28:20)."],["Joseph","A just man of David's line who receives revelation in dreams and protects Mary and the Child.","Patron of the universal Church; a model of silent obedience."],["Mary","The Virgin who conceives by the Holy Spirit (1:18–25).","Fulfils Isaiah 7:14."],["Peter","Leader of the Twelve, the rock on which Christ builds his Church, who later denies Jesus and weeps.","Matthew's Gospel is the basis of the papacy (CCC 552–553, 881)."],["Matthew (Levi)","A tax collector called from his booth (9:9).","Grace for public sinners; traditionally the author."],["The Pharisees and scribes","Experts in the Law whose hypocrisy Jesus condemns (ch. 23).","A warning to religious people of every age."]],
 themes:[["Fulfilment","'That it might be fulfilled' recurs some ten times."],["The Kingdom of heaven","Matthew's characteristic phrase."],["Righteousness","A justice exceeding that of the scribes (5:20)."],["The Church","The only Gospel to use the word ekklesia (16:18; 18:17)."],["Mission to all nations","From the Magi to the Great Commission."]],
 christ:"Matthew shows Jesus as the new Moses: saved from a murderous king as a baby, coming out of Egypt, giving the new Law on a mountain, and organising his teaching in five books. He is the Son of David who brings David's kingdom, and the suffering Son of Man who will come in glory.",
 church:"Matthew is the source of the Lord's Prayer as the Church prays it (6:9–13; CCC 2759ff.), the Beatitudes (CCC 1716ff.), Peter's primacy and the keys (CCC 553, 881), the power to bind and loose (18:18), the Trinitarian baptismal formula (CCC 1223), the Last Judgment of Matthew 25 (CCC 1038–1039, 544) and Christ's teaching on the indissolubility of marriage (19:3–9; CCC 1614).",
 thomas:"Thomas lectured on Matthew in Paris (probably 1269–70, though some date it to his first Paris regency); the text survives as a reportatio. His Catena Aurea on Matthew gathers the Fathers verse by verse; Chrysostom's 90 Homilies on Matthew are the greatest patristic commentary.",
 hard:[["Why do Matthew's and Luke's genealogies differ?","Matthew traces the royal line through Solomon; Luke traces a line through Nathan. Ancient solutions (Julius Africanus) proposed levirate marriage or that Luke gives Mary's line; Matthew's is also deliberately structured in three groups of fourteen. Both teach that Jesus is the true heir of David."],["Was Matthew written in Hebrew?","Papias (c. 120) says Matthew compiled the 'oracles' in the Hebrew language. Our Matthew is in Greek. Many scholars hold it used Mark; the early Church held it was the first Gospel. The Church has not defined the question."]],
 keys:[["Mt 1:18–25","the virginal conception"],["Mt 5:3–12","the Beatitudes"],["Mt 6:9–13","the Our Father"],["Mt 16:13–19","Peter and the keys"],["Mt 18:15–20","binding and loosing"],["Mt 25:31–46","the Last Judgment"],["Mt 26:26–28","the institution of the Eucharist"],["Mt 28:18–20","the Great Commission"]],
 author:"Traditionally the apostle Matthew (Levi), the tax collector. Many scholars date the Greek Gospel c. 80–90 and think it drew on Mark and a sayings source; others date it before 70."},

mark:{era:"messiah",when:"From John's preaching to the empty tomb; written c. AD 65–70",genre:"Gospel",
 lede:"Mark is the shortest and fastest Gospel. It opens with 'The beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God' and moves 'immediately' from scene to scene toward the Cross, where a Roman centurion confesses what the reader was told at the start.",
 story:`<p>Mark has no infancy story. John the Baptist appears, Jesus is baptised and the voice from heaven calls him 'beloved Son'. In Galilee Jesus calls fishermen, teaches with authority, casts out demons, heals the sick and forgives sins; crowds press on him; his family think him mad and scribes say he is possessed. He calms the storm, raises Jairus's daughter and feeds the multitudes, yet the disciples fail to understand. Jesus often commands silence about who he is (the 'messianic secret').</p>
<p>At Caesarea Philippi, the turning point, Peter confesses, 'Thou art the Christ' (8:29). Jesus then predicts his Passion three times, and each time the disciples misunderstand; he teaches that the Son of Man came 'to give his life a redemption for many' (10:45). In Jerusalem he cleanses the Temple, teaches, and is anointed at Bethany. He institutes the Eucharist, prays in Gethsemane, is arrested and abandoned; a young man flees naked. On the Cross he cries 'My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?', and the centurion says, 'Indeed this man was the son of God' (15:39). The women find the tomb empty and are told he is risen.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"Beginning: John, baptism, first ministry"],[2,3,"Controversies and the Twelve"],[4,5,"Parables and mighty works"],[6,8,"Mission, feedings, misunderstanding; Peter's confession"],[9,10,"The way to Jerusalem: three Passion predictions"],[11,13,"In Jerusalem: Temple, teaching, the end times"],[14,15,"Passion and death"],[16,16,"The empty tomb and the risen Lord"]],
 people:[["Jesus","The Son of God who acts with power and goes to the Cross as the suffering Son of Man.","Mark holds together glory and suffering: you understand who Jesus is only at the Cross."],["Peter","Impulsive leader, confessor and denier.","Tradition says Mark preserves Peter's preaching; its honesty about Peter's failures suggests Peter's own voice."],["The disciples","Called, sent, yet often uncomprehending and fearful.","Discipleship is learned slowly, by following."],["The centurion","A Gentile who confesses Jesus at the Cross.","The first human in Mark to call Jesus Son of God aloud and understand it."],["Mary Magdalene and the women","Witnesses of the death, burial and empty tomb.","The faithful when the Twelve flee."]],
 themes:[["Who is Jesus?","The question runs through the whole Gospel."],["The messianic secret","Jesus' identity is revealed fully only through the Cross."],["Discipleship as following","'Take up his cross, and follow me' (8:34)."],["Faith and fear","'Why are you fearful? have you not faith yet?' (4:40)."]],
 christ:"Mark's Jesus fulfils Isaiah's suffering Servant and Daniel's Son of Man: the one who serves and gives his life as a ransom (10:45) and who will come 'with the clouds of heaven' (14:62).",
 church:"Mark 10:45 is a key text for Christ's redemptive death (CCC 608, 622). Mark 16:16 underlines the necessity of faith and baptism (CCC 1257). The longer ending (16:9–20), absent from some early manuscripts, was received as canonical by the Council of Trent.",
 thomas:"Thomas wrote no separate commentary on Mark but covered it in the Catena Aurea. Papias and Irenaeus report that Mark was 'the interpreter of Peter'. Bede wrote a full commentary; Jerome's homilies on Mark survive.",
 hard:[["Why does Mark end so abruptly at 16:8 in some manuscripts?","The oldest Greek manuscripts end with the women afraid; the longer ending (16:9–20) is found in most manuscripts and was known to Irenaeus (c. 180). The Church accepts it as inspired and canonical whether or not Mark wrote it."]],
 keys:[["Mk 1:1","the beginning of the gospel"],["Mk 4:35–41","calming the storm"],["Mk 8:27–38","Peter's confession; take up your cross"],["Mk 10:45","a ransom for many"],["Mk 14:22–25","the Last Supper"],["Mk 15:34–39","the cry and the centurion"]],
 author:"John Mark, companion of Paul and Barnabas (Acts 12:12) and of Peter (1 Pet 5:13), writing in Rome according to early tradition. Most scholars think it the first Gospel written."},

luke:{era:"messiah",when:"From the annunciation to John's birth to the Ascension; written c. AD 60–90",genre:"Gospel",
 lede:"Luke, a physician and companion of Paul, writes 'an orderly account' for Theophilus. His Gospel is the Gospel of mercy, prayer, women, the poor and the Holy Spirit, and the first half of a two-volume work completed by Acts.",
 story:`<p>Luke begins with two annunciations: to Zachary, of John's birth, and to the Virgin Mary in Nazareth, of the Son of the Most High who will reign on David's throne. Mary visits Elizabeth and sings the Magnificat; Jesus is born in Bethlehem and laid in a manger; shepherds come; Simeon and Anna greet him in the Temple; at twelve he is found in his Father's house. Luke gives us the Gloria, the Benedictus, the Magnificat and the Nunc Dimittis.</p>
<p>In Nazareth Jesus announces his mission from Isaiah 61. He calls Peter after a miraculous catch, heals, forgives a sinful woman who washes his feet, and raises the widow's son at Naim. From 9:51 he 'set his face to go to Jerusalem', and the long journey contains parables found only in Luke: the Good Samaritan, the rich fool, the lost sheep, the lost coin and the prodigal son (ch. 15), Lazarus and the rich man, the Pharisee and the publican. He visits Zacchaeus.</p>
<p>In Jerusalem he institutes the Eucharist, prays on the Mount of Olives in agony (sweating blood), forgives his executioners and promises the good thief Paradise: 'Father, into thy hands I commend my spirit' (23:46). The risen Christ walks with two disciples to Emmaus, opens the Scriptures and is known 'in the breaking of bread' (24:35), then ascends from Bethany.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,2,"Infancy narratives: John and Jesus"],[3,4,"John's preaching, baptism, genealogy, temptation, Nazareth"],[5,9,"Ministry in Galilee"],[9,19,"The journey to Jerusalem (9:51–19:27)"],[19,21,"Ministry in Jerusalem"],[22,23,"Passion and death"],[24,24,"Resurrection, Emmaus, Ascension"]],
 people:[["Mary","The Virgin of Nazareth, 'full of grace', who believes the word and keeps all things in her heart (2:19).","The source of Marian doctrine and prayer: the Hail Mary (1:28, 42), the Magnificat."],["Zachary and Elizabeth","Elderly righteous parents of John the Baptist.","Elizabeth calls Mary 'the mother of my Lord' (1:43)."],["John the Baptist","Forerunner, filled with the Spirit in the womb.","Bridge between the prophets and Christ."],["Simeon and Anna","Elderly faithful who recognise the Child in the Temple.","'Now thou dost dismiss thy servant' (Nunc Dimittis) is prayed at Compline."],["Zacchaeus","A rich chief tax collector who climbs a tree and repents.","Salvation comes to his house (19:9)."],["The good thief","Crucified with Jesus, asks to be remembered.","'This day thou shalt be with me in paradise' (23:43)."]],
 themes:[["Mercy","The parables of the lost (ch. 15)."],["Prayer","Jesus prays at every turning point."],["The Holy Spirit","Spirit-filled from the infancy to the promise of power from on high."],["The poor and outsiders","Shepherds, women, Samaritans, publicans."],["Joy","'Behold, I bring you good tidings of great joy' (2:10)."]],
 christ:"Luke shows Jesus as Saviour of all, the Spirit-anointed prophet of Isaiah 61, and the merciful physician. On the road to Emmaus he teaches that 'all things must needs be fulfilled, which are written in the law of Moses, and in the prophets, and in the psalms, concerning me' (24:44): the key to reading the whole Bible.",
 church:"Luke is the main source for Marian doctrine and devotion (CCC 484–511; the Annunciation, Visitation and Presentation; the Joyful Mysteries), for the Eucharistic words 'do this for a commemoration of me' (22:19; CCC 1341), and for the Emmaus pattern of Word and Sacrament in the Mass (CCC 1347). Dante called Luke 'the scribe of the gentleness of Christ'.",
 thomas:"Thomas's Catena Aurea on Luke gathers the Fathers; Ambrose's Exposition of Luke and Bede's commentary are the main patristic works. Tradition also made Luke a painter of the Virgin.",
 hard:[["How do we date the census of Quirinius (2:2)?","Quirinius's known census was in AD 6, after Herod's death (4 BC). Proposed solutions include an earlier census, an earlier role for Quirinius, or a translation 'before Quirinius was governor'. The question is open; Luke elsewhere proves a careful historian."]],
 keys:[["Lk 1:26–38","the Annunciation"],["Lk 1:46–55","the Magnificat"],["Lk 2:1–20","the Nativity"],["Lk 4:16–21","Jesus in Nazareth"],["Lk 10:25–37","the Good Samaritan"],["Lk 15","the lost sheep, coin and son"],["Lk 22:19–20","'do this in memory of me'"],["Lk 23:39–43","the good thief"],["Lk 24:13–35","Emmaus"]],
 author:"Luke, 'the most dear physician' (Col 4:14), a Gentile companion of Paul, according to unanimous early tradition. Dates proposed range from the early 60s (before Acts ends) to c. 85."},

john:{era:"messiah",when:"Jesus' ministry over about three Passovers; written c. AD 90–100",genre:"Gospel",
 lede:"John, 'the spiritual Gospel', begins before creation with the Word who was God and became flesh. Through seven signs and long discourses it shows Jesus as the I AM who gives life, and it was written 'that you may believe' (20:31).",
 story:`<p>The prologue (1:1–18) proclaims the eternal Word, through whom all things were made, who 'was made flesh, and dwelt among us'. John the Baptist points to 'the Lamb of God'. Jesus' first sign is water made wine at Cana. He cleanses the Temple, tells Nicodemus he must be 'born again of water and the Holy Ghost' (3:5), and offers living water to a Samaritan woman.</p>
<p>The 'Book of Signs' (1–12) unfolds seven signs (Cana, the official's son, the paralytic at Bethsaida, the loaves, walking on the sea, the man born blind, the raising of Lazarus) and great 'I am' sayings: the bread of life, the light of the world, the door, the good shepherd, the resurrection and the life, the way, the truth and the life, the true vine. After the loaves Jesus says, 'my flesh is meat indeed: and my blood is drink indeed' (6:56), and many leave him. Conflict grows until, after Lazarus, the authorities resolve to kill him.</p>
<p>The 'Book of Glory' (13–20) begins with the washing of the feet and the Last Supper discourses on love, the Paraclete and unity, ending in Jesus' high-priestly prayer (ch. 17). He is lifted up on the Cross, says 'It is consummated', and from his pierced side flow blood and water. Risen, he appears to Mary Magdalene, breathes the Spirit on the disciples and gives them power to forgive sins (20:22–23), and brings Thomas to confess 'My Lord, and my God'. In the epilogue (21) he asks Peter three times 'lovest thou me?' and commands, 'Feed my sheep'.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"Prologue; the Baptist; first disciples"],[2,4,"Cana, Nicodemus, the Samaritan woman"],[5,10,"Signs and discourses: bread of life, light of the world, good shepherd"],[11,12,"Lazarus; the hour comes"],[13,17,"The Last Supper discourses and the priestly prayer"],[18,19,"Passion and death"],[20,20,"Resurrection"],[21,21,"Epilogue: Peter restored"]],
 people:[["Jesus","The eternal Word made flesh, the I AM, the Lamb of God.","John's Christology grounded the Councils of Nicaea and Chalcedon."],["The Beloved Disciple","The disciple 'whom Jesus loved', who leans on his breast, stands at the Cross and receives Mary as his mother (19:26–27).","Tradition identifies him with John son of Zebedee, the Gospel's witness (21:24)."],["Mary, the Mother of Jesus","Present at Cana and at the Cross, called 'Woman'.","At Cana she says 'Whatsoever he shall say to you, do ye' (2:5); at the Cross she is given to the Church (CCC 964, 2618)."],["Nicodemus","A Pharisee who comes by night and later helps bury Jesus.","Slow growth toward faith."],["The Samaritan woman","A woman of Samaria who becomes a witness to her town.","Living water for outsiders and sinners."],["Lazarus, Martha and Mary","Friends of Jesus at Bethany.","'I am the resurrection and the life' (11:25)."],["Thomas","The doubter who makes the Gospel's highest confession.","'Blessed are they that have not seen, and have believed' (20:29)."]],
 themes:[["Incarnation","The Word made flesh."],["Signs and faith","Signs reveal glory and call for belief."],["Life","'I am come that they may have life' (10:10)."],["Light and darkness, truth and lies","Judgment as exposure to the light."],["Love","'Love one another, as I have loved you' (15:12)."],["The Paraclete","The Spirit as advocate and teacher."]],
 christ:"John is Christ's self-revelation: his 'I AM' sayings echo the divine name of Exodus 3:14 ('before Abraham was made, I am', 8:58). He is the Lamb of God (Passover), the new Temple (2:21), the bread from heaven (manna), the bronze serpent lifted up (3:14), and the true vine (Israel).",
 church:"John grounds the doctrines of the Trinity and the divinity of Christ (CCC 241–242), baptism (3:5; CCC 1215, 1257), the Real Presence (ch. 6; CCC 1336, 1338, 1406), the forgiveness of sins by the apostles (20:22–23; CCC 976, 1485), Peter's pastoral office (21:15–17; CCC 553) and Mary's spiritual motherhood (19:26–27; CCC 964).",
 thomas:"Thomas's Lectura super Ioannem (c. 1270–72) is often counted his finest scriptural commentary and is woven into this Reader. In its prologue he calls John's Gospel the contemplation of the Word in its height, fulness and perfection. Augustine's 124 Tractates on John are the great patristic commentary.",
 hard:[["Why is John so different from the other three Gospels?","John writes later, assumes his readers know the Synoptic tradition, and selects signs and discourses to show who Jesus is. Clement of Alexandria called it 'a spiritual Gospel'. The differences are of selection and emphasis; its chronology (several Passovers, Judean ministry) is often regarded as historically valuable."],["Does Jesus really claim to be God?","Yes: 'I and the Father are one' (10:30), 'before Abraham was made, I am' (8:58), the response of stones for blasphemy (10:33), and Thomas's 'My Lord, and my God' accepted by Jesus (20:28)."]],
 keys:[["Jn 1:1–18","the Prologue"],["Jn 2:1–11","Cana"],["Jn 3:1–21","Nicodemus; God so loved the world"],["Jn 6:22–71","the bread of life"],["Jn 11:25–26","the resurrection and the life"],["Jn 14:6","the way, the truth and the life"],["Jn 17","the high-priestly prayer"],["Jn 19:25–37","Mary at the Cross; the pierced side"],["Jn 20:19–29","the Spirit, forgiveness of sins, Thomas"],["Jn 21:15–19","'Feed my sheep'"]],
 author:"Attributed by Irenaeus (c. 180) to John the apostle, the Beloved Disciple, writing in Ephesus in old age; some scholars think of a 'Johannine school' finalising the disciple's testimony."},

acts:{era:"church",when:"AD 30–c. 62",genre:"History of the early Church",
 lede:"Acts of the Apostles is Luke's second volume. It tells how the Holy Spirit, poured out at Pentecost, carried the Gospel 'in Jerusalem, and in all Judea, and Samaria, and even to the uttermost part of the earth' (1:8), from Peter in Jerusalem to Paul in Rome.",
 story:`<p>After forty days the risen Jesus ascends. The apostles, with Mary and the brethren, pray in the upper room and choose Matthias to replace Judas. At Pentecost the Spirit descends in tongues of fire; Peter preaches and three thousand are baptised. The first community devotes itself to 'the doctrine of the apostles, and in the communication of the breaking of bread, and in prayers' (2:42), holding goods in common. Peter heals, and the apostles are arrested and flogged but rejoice. Seven deacons are chosen; one of them, Stephen, becomes the first martyr, while a young man named Saul approves.</p>
<p>Persecution scatters the Church: Philip evangelises Samaria and baptises an Ethiopian official. Saul, on the road to Damascus to arrest Christians, is struck down by the risen Christ and becomes the apostle to the Gentiles (ch. 9). Peter, after a vision, baptises the Roman centurion Cornelius and his household (ch. 10). At Antioch believers are first called Christians. The Council of Jerusalem decides that Gentile converts need not be circumcised (ch. 15).</p>
<p>The second half follows Paul's three missionary journeys through Cyprus, Asia Minor and Greece: Philippi, Thessalonica, Athens (his speech at the Areopagus), Corinth, Ephesus. Arrested in Jerusalem, he defends himself before councils, governors and King Agrippa, appeals to Caesar, survives a shipwreck on Malta and reaches Rome, where he preaches for two years under house arrest 'without prohibition' (28:31).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,2,"Ascension and Pentecost"],[3,7,"The Church in Jerusalem; Stephen"],[8,12,"Samaria, Saul's conversion, Cornelius, Antioch"],[13,14,"Paul's first journey"],[15,15,"The Council of Jerusalem"],[15,18,"Paul's second journey"],[18,21,"Paul's third journey"],[21,28,"Paul's arrest, trials and journey to Rome"]],
 people:[["Peter","Leader of the apostles, preacher at Pentecost, who opens the Church to the Gentiles.","Exercises the primacy given by Christ: presides, speaks for the Twelve, and settles the Council."],["Paul (Saul)","Pharisee and persecutor turned apostle to the nations.","Author of thirteen letters; his conversion is told three times (chs 9, 22, 26)."],["Stephen","Deacon and first martyr, who sees the heavens opened and forgives his killers (7:59–60).","Model of martyrdom; his prayer echoes Christ's."],["Barnabas","'Son of consolation', who vouches for Paul and travels with him.","The encourager in the Church."],["James 'the brother of the Lord'","Leader of the Jerusalem church who gives the judgment at the Council.","Author of the Letter of James."],["Mary","Present in prayer with the apostles before Pentecost (1:14).","Mother of the Church in its birth (CCC 965)."],["Lydia, Priscilla and Aquila","Women and couples who host and teach.","The laity in mission."]],
 themes:[["The Holy Spirit","Acts has been called 'the Gospel of the Holy Spirit'."],["Witness","'You shall be witnesses unto me' (1:8)."],["The Church's unity and growth","One heart and one soul (4:32)."],["Jews and Gentiles","One people of God."],["Suffering and joy","Persecution spreads the Gospel."]],
 christ:"Acts is the continuation of 'all things which Jesus began to do and to teach' (1:1): the risen Christ now acts through his Body, the Church. Saul hears, 'why persecutest thou me?' (9:4): Christ is one with his members.",
 church:"Acts grounds the Church's teaching on Pentecost and confirmation (CCC 1287–1288; the laying on of hands, 8:14–17), apostolic succession (1:20–26; CCC 77, 861), the diaconate (6:1–6; CCC 1569ff.), the four marks of community life (2:42; CCC 2624), councils as the Church's way of settling disputes (ch. 15), and infant and household baptism (16:15, 33; CCC 1252).",
 thomas:"The Council of Jerusalem became the pattern for the Church's later councils, which also speak in the form 'it hath seemed good to the Holy Ghost and to us' (15:28). Thomas takes the name and sin of 'simony' from Simon Magus, who tried to buy the power of the Spirit (8:18–24; ST II-II q.100). Chrysostom's 55 Homilies on Acts are the main patristic commentary.",
 keys:[["Acts 1:8","witnesses to the ends of the earth"],["Acts 2","Pentecost and Peter's sermon"],["Acts 2:42–47","the first community"],["Acts 7","Stephen"],["Acts 9","Saul's conversion"],["Acts 10","Cornelius"],["Acts 15","the Council of Jerusalem"],["Acts 17:22–31","Paul at the Areopagus"],["Acts 28:30–31","Paul in Rome"]],
 author:"Luke, author of the third Gospel (1:1 refers to it); the 'we' passages (from 16:10) suggest the author travelled with Paul. Written c. 62–85."}
});

// Book introductions: the letters and Revelation
const PAUL_THOMAS="Thomas lectured on all fourteen letters then attributed to Paul (including Hebrews); his commentaries, partly revised by himself (Romans, 1 Cor 1–7) and partly reportationes, are among his most important theological works. In the prologue he says the whole teaching of the letters concerns the grace of Christ, considered in the head (Hebrews), in the principal members of his mystical body, its prelates (1–2 Timothy, Titus, Philemon), and in the mystical body itself (Romans to 2 Thessalonians).";
Object.assign(BOOK_INTRO,{
romans:{era:"church",when:"Written from Corinth, c. AD 57",genre:"Letter",
 lede:"Romans is Paul's fullest statement of the Gospel: all have sinned, and God justifies sinners freely by grace through faith in Christ, making them children of God who live by the Spirit. It has shaped Christian theology more than any other letter.",
 story:`<p>Paul writes to a church he has not yet visited, planning a mission to Spain. The Gospel is 'the power of God unto salvation to every one that believeth' (1:16). He shows that Gentiles, who suppressed the truth known from creation (1:18–32), and Jews, who had the Law but did not keep it, alike stand guilty: 'all have sinned, and do need the glory of God' (3:23). God's justice is revealed in Christ, whose blood is a propitiation; we are justified 'freely by his grace' (3:24). Abraham is the model: he believed and was justified before circumcision.</p>
<p>Chapter 5 contrasts Adam, through whom sin and death entered, with Christ, through whom grace abounds. In baptism we die and rise with Christ (ch. 6). Paul describes the struggle of the divided self (ch. 7) and the life of the Spirit, by whom we cry 'Abba, Father' and nothing can separate us from the love of God (ch. 8). Chapters 9–11 wrestle with Israel's unbelief and conclude that 'all Israel should be saved' and God's gifts are irrevocable. Chapters 12–16 apply the Gospel: present your bodies as a living sacrifice; love; obey legitimate authority; bear with the weak; greetings to many, including the deaconess Phoebe.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,3,"All need salvation; justification by grace through faith"],[4,4,"Abraham, father of believers"],[5,8,"New life: Adam and Christ, baptism, the Spirit"],[9,11,"God's faithfulness to Israel"],[12,15,"Christian life"],[16,16,"Greetings"]],
 people:[["Paul","Apostle to the Gentiles, writing his theological testament.","The architect of the theology of grace."],["Abraham","Father of all who believe.","Justified by faith that works through obedience (cf. Jas 2)."],["Adam","Type of Christ, through whom sin entered.","Basis of the doctrine of original sin."],["Phoebe","A deaconess (servant) of the church at Cenchreae who probably carried the letter (16:1).","Women's service in the apostolic Church."]],
 themes:[["Justification","God makes the sinner just by grace."],["Faith","Faith working through love is the response to grace."],["Original sin","5:12–21."],["Life in the Spirit","Adoption, freedom, hope."],["Israel","God's irrevocable call."]],
 christ:"Christ is the new Adam, the propitiation set forth by God (3:25), who died for us 'when as yet we were sinners' (5:8) and rose for our justification (4:25).",
 church:"Romans 5:12 is the key text for original sin (CCC 402–403; Trent, Session 5). Romans 1:20 grounds natural knowledge of God (CCC 32; Vatican I). Romans 3–8 underlies Trent's Decree on Justification (Session 6) and the 1999 Joint Declaration on the Doctrine of Justification with the Lutheran World Federation. Romans 6 is the classic text on baptism (CCC 1227). Romans 13:13–14 converted Augustine (Confessions VIII.12).",
 thomas:PAUL_THOMAS+" On Romans he insists that the faith which justifies is faith 'formed' by charity, which gives the soul its spiritual life.",
 hard:[["Does Romans teach justification by faith alone?","Paul says we are justified 'by faith without the works of the law' (3:28), meaning the Mosaic Law and works done to earn salvation. Catholic teaching (Trent, CCC 1987–1995) holds that justification is a free gift received by faith, which includes the beginning of hope and charity, and that it truly makes us righteous, not only declares us so. The Joint Declaration (1999) recorded broad agreement on these basic truths."]],
 keys:[["Rom 1:16–20","the Gospel; God known from creation"],["Rom 3:21–26","justified freely by grace"],["Rom 5:12–21","Adam and Christ"],["Rom 6:3–11","baptised into his death"],["Rom 8","life in the Spirit; nothing can separate us"],["Rom 11:29","the gifts of God are without repentance"],["Rom 12:1–2","a living sacrifice"]],
 author:"Paul, dictated to Tertius (16:22). Undisputed."},

"1-corinthians":{era:"church",when:"Written from Ephesus, c. AD 54",genre:"Letter",
 lede:"1 Corinthians answers the problems of a gifted but divided church in a wealthy, pagan port city: factions, sexual immorality, lawsuits, marriage, idol food, disorder at the Eucharist, spiritual gifts and doubts about the resurrection. In the middle stands the hymn to love.",
 story:`<p>Paul founded the church at Corinth (Acts 18). Now he hears of factions ('I am of Paul… I of Apollo… I of Cephas', 1:12) and answers with the folly of the Cross, 'unto the Jews indeed a stumblingblock, and unto the Gentiles foolishness' (1:23). He rebukes a man living with his stepmother and the church's tolerance, believers suing each other, and visits to prostitutes: 'your members are the temple of the Holy Ghost' (6:19).</p>
<p>Answering their letter, he treats marriage and celibacy (ch. 7), food offered to idols and the freedom that must yield to charity (8–10), head coverings and the abuses at the Lord's Supper, where he hands on the words of institution (11:23–29). He explains the many gifts of one Spirit in one Body (12), the excellence of charity (13), and the ordering of prophecy and tongues (14). Chapter 15 defends the bodily resurrection: 'if Christ be not risen again, then is our preaching vain' (15:14).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,4,"Divisions and the wisdom of the Cross"],[5,6,"Immorality and lawsuits; the body as temple"],[7,7,"Marriage, celibacy, widowhood"],[8,10,"Food offered to idols; freedom and charity; the Eucharist"],[11,14,"Worship: the Lord's Supper, gifts, love"],[15,15,"The resurrection"],[16,16,"The collection and greetings"]],
 people:[["Paul","Founder and spiritual father of the church (4:15).","Pastoral theology in action."],["Apollos","An eloquent preacher from Alexandria.","Ministers are servants, not party leaders."],["Cephas (Peter)","Named as an apostle and first witness of the risen Christ (15:5).","Evidence of Peter's standing throughout the churches."],["Chloe's people","Members who report the divisions (1:11).","The laity's role in the Church's health."]],
 themes:[["The Cross","God's wisdom and power."],["The Body of Christ","One body, many members (ch. 12)."],["Charity","The greatest of the virtues (ch. 13)."],["The Eucharist","Real participation in the body and blood of Christ (10:16; 11:27–29)."],["Resurrection","Christ the firstfruits; the body raised spiritual and incorruptible."]],
 christ:"Christ is 'the power of God, and the wisdom of God' (1:24), our Passover sacrificed (5:7), the rock that followed Israel (10:4), and the firstfruits of those who sleep (15:20).",
 church:"1 Corinthians gives the earliest account of the Eucharist (11:23–26; CCC 1329, 1356) and the warning against receiving unworthily (11:27–29; CCC 1385), teaching on virginity and marriage (ch. 7; CCC 1618–1620), the charisms (CCC 799–801), charity as the form of the virtues (ch. 13; CCC 1826) and the resurrection of the body (CCC 999–1000). The 'Pauline privilege' (7:12–15; CIC cc. 1143–1147) comes from here.",
 thomas:PAUL_THOMAS,
 keys:[["1 Cor 1:18–25","the word of the Cross"],["1 Cor 3:16","you are the temple of God"],["1 Cor 6:19–20","your body is a temple"],["1 Cor 10:16–17","one bread, one body"],["1 Cor 11:23–29","the institution of the Eucharist"],["1 Cor 12","one body, many members"],["1 Cor 13","the hymn to charity"],["1 Cor 15:3–8","the Gospel received: died, buried, rose, appeared"]],
 author:"Paul, with Sosthenes. Undisputed."},

"2-corinthians":{era:"church",when:"Written from Macedonia, c. AD 55–56",genre:"Letter",
 lede:"2 Corinthians is Paul's most personal letter. Defending his apostleship against rivals, he reveals a ministry of weakness, suffering and comfort, through which God's power is made perfect.",
 story:`<p>After a painful visit and a 'severe letter', Paul writes with relief that the Corinthians have repented. He describes the ministry of the new covenant, more glorious than Moses' (ch. 3); the treasure of the Gospel in 'earthen vessels' (4:7); and the ministry of reconciliation: 'if then any be in Christ a new creature… God was in Christ, reconciling the world to himself' (5:17–19).</p>
<p>Chapters 8–9 urge generosity in the collection for the poor of Jerusalem: 'God loveth a cheerful giver' (9:7). Chapters 10–13 confront 'super-apostles' who boast of credentials; Paul 'boasts' instead of his sufferings: beatings, shipwreck, danger, anxiety for the churches; a vision of the third heaven; and a thorn in the flesh, to which the Lord answered: 'My grace is sufficient for thee: for power is made perfect in infirmity' (12:9).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,7,"Paul's ministry: comfort, new covenant, reconciliation"],[8,9,"The collection for Jerusalem"],[10,13,"Defence against the false apostles; strength in weakness"]],
 people:[["Paul","An apostle accused of weakness who embraces it.","Authority exercised as service and suffering."],["Titus","Paul's envoy who reconciles the church.","Faithful co-worker."],["The 'super-apostles'","Rivals boasting of eloquence and status.","Counterfeit leadership."]],
 themes:[["Comfort in affliction","God comforts us so that we can comfort others (1:4)."],["New covenant ministry","The Spirit gives life."],["Reconciliation","God's initiative in Christ."],["Generosity","The grace of giving."],["Strength in weakness","12:9–10."]],
 christ:"'Him, that knew no sin, for us he hath made sin, that we might be made the justice of God in him' (5:21): the great exchange. Christ, 'being rich, he became poor for your sakes' (8:9).",
 church:"2 Corinthians 5:17–20 underlies the Church's ministry of reconciliation in the sacrament of penance (CCC 1442, 1461). 3:6 ('the letter killeth, but the spirit quickeneth') is foundational for the spiritual reading of Scripture. 8:9 grounds the Church's preferential love for the poor.",
 thomas:PAUL_THOMAS,
 keys:[["2 Cor 3:17–18","where the Spirit of the Lord is, there is liberty"],["2 Cor 4:7","treasure in earthen vessels"],["2 Cor 5:17–21","new creation; reconciliation"],["2 Cor 9:7","a cheerful giver"],["2 Cor 12:7–10","my grace is sufficient"]],
 author:"Paul, with Timothy. Some scholars think it combines several letters."},

galatians:{era:"church",when:"c. AD 48–55",genre:"Letter",
 lede:"Galatians is Paul's urgent defence of the Gospel against teachers who insisted that Gentile Christians be circumcised and keep the Law of Moses. Its themes: grace, faith, freedom, and the life of the Spirit.",
 story:`<p>Paul skips his usual thanksgiving: 'I wonder that you are so soon removed… unto another gospel' (1:6). He recounts his call by revelation, his meeting with Peter ('Cephas') in Jerusalem, the leaders' recognition of his mission, and his public rebuke of Peter at Antioch for withdrawing from table fellowship with Gentiles (ch. 2). 'I live, now not I; but Christ liveth in me' (2:20).</p>
<p>He argues from Abraham, the curse of the Law borne by Christ, and the Law as a 'pedagogue' until faith came. In Christ 'there is neither Jew nor Greek… neither male nor female' (3:28). The allegory of Hagar and Sarah contrasts slavery and freedom. 'In Christ Jesus neither circumcision availeth any thing, nor uncircumcision: but faith that worketh by charity' (5:6). Freedom is not licence: the works of the flesh are opposed to the fruit of the Spirit (5:19–23).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,2,"Paul's Gospel and authority; the incident at Antioch"],[3,4,"Faith, the Law, adoption; Hagar and Sarah"],[5,6,"Freedom, the Spirit and the life of love"]],
 people:[["Paul","Former zealot for the Law.","Freedom in Christ."],["Peter (Cephas)","Pillar of the Church, rebuked for inconsistency.","Fraternal correction even of the first pope; Peter's authority is not denied but his conduct is corrected."],["James and John","'Pillars' who give Paul the right hand of fellowship.","Apostolic communion."]],
 themes:[["Grace and Law","The Law cannot justify; Christ does."],["Faith working through love","5:6."],["Adoption","'Abba, Father' (4:6)."],["Freedom","For freedom Christ has set us free."],["Fruit of the Spirit","Charity, joy, peace and the rest (5:22–23)."]],
 christ:"'When the fulness of the time was come, God sent his Son, made of a woman, made under the law' (4:4): the Incarnation in one verse, and the earliest New Testament reference to Christ's mother.",
 church:"Galatians 5:6 is central to the Catholic teaching that justifying faith is faith formed by charity (Trent, Session 6, ch. 7; CCC 1814). 5:22–23 gives the fruits of the Spirit (twelve in the Vulgate; CCC 1832). 4:4 grounds the Church's teaching on Mary's divine motherhood (CCC 422, 484). Peter's rebuke at Antioch shows that papal primacy coexists with fraternal correction (Thomas, ST II-II q.33 a.4 ad 2).",
 thomas:PAUL_THOMAS+" Thomas comments that Paul's rebuke of Peter teaches prelates to accept correction from subjects when the faith is endangered.",
 keys:[["Gal 1:6–9","no other gospel"],["Gal 2:11–14","Peter at Antioch"],["Gal 2:20","Christ liveth in me"],["Gal 3:28","all one in Christ"],["Gal 4:4–7","born of a woman; Abba, Father"],["Gal 5:6","faith that worketh by charity"],["Gal 5:22–23","the fruit of the Spirit"]],
 author:"Paul. Undisputed. The date depends on whether it was written to southern or northern Galatia."},

ephesians:{era:"church",when:"Traditionally from Rome, c. AD 60–62",genre:"Letter",
 lede:"Ephesians unveils God's eternal plan 'to re-establish all things in Christ' (1:10), the Church as his Body and Bride, the unity of Jews and Gentiles, and the daily life of those raised with Christ, including the great mystery of marriage.",
 story:`<p>A great blessing opens the letter: before the foundation of the world God chose us in Christ to be holy, predestined us to adoption, redeemed us through his blood and sealed us with the Spirit (1:3–14). Christ is raised above every power and given as head over all things to the Church, 'which is his body, and the fulness of him who is filled all in all' (1:23). 'By grace you are saved through faith… not of works, that no man may glory', yet 'created in Christ Jesus in good works' (2:8–10). Christ has broken down the wall between Jew and Gentile, making 'one new man'.</p>
<p>The second half urges unity: 'one body and one Spirit… one Lord, one faith, one baptism; one God and Father of all' (4:4–6). Christ gives apostles, prophets, evangelists, pastors and teachers to build up the Body. Believers put off the old man and put on the new. Wives and husbands are to love as Christ and the Church: 'This is a great sacrament; but I speak in Christ and in the church' (5:32). Children, parents, slaves and masters are addressed. Finally, 'put you on the armour of God' (6:11).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,3,"God's plan in Christ: blessing, grace, unity, the mystery revealed"],[4,4,"Unity and growth in the Body"],[5,6,"Walking in love and light; the household; the armour of God"]],
 people:[["Paul","'A prisoner of Jesus Christ' (3:1).","Steward of the mystery hidden for ages."],["Tychicus","Messenger who carries the letter (6:21).","Faithful minister."]],
 themes:[["God's eternal plan","All things summed up in Christ."],["Grace","Salvation as gift."],["The Church","Body, temple, bride."],["Unity","One Lord, one faith, one baptism."],["Marriage","Image of Christ and the Church."],["Spiritual warfare","The armour of God."]],
 christ:"Christ is head of the Church and of the cosmos, our peace who reconciles Jew and Gentile in one body through the Cross (2:14–16), and the Bridegroom who 'delivered himself up' for the Church to sanctify her (5:25–27).",
 church:"Ephesians grounds the doctrine of the Church as Body and Bride (CCC 772–773, 796), the sacramentality of marriage (5:32; CCC 1616–1617, 1659), the unity of faith (4:4–6; CCC 813, 866), grace as gift (2:8–10; CCC 1996) and predestination in Christ (1:4–5; CCC 257, 492). The Vulgate's 'sacramentum' translates the Greek mysterion.",
 thomas:PAUL_THOMAS,
 hard:[["Does Ephesians 5 teach the subordination of wives?","It addresses a household code in its culture but transforms it: it begins with mutual subjection 'in the fear of Christ' (5:21) and gives husbands the heavier command, to love as Christ who died for the Church. John Paul II (Mulieris Dignitatem 24) reads it as 'mutual subjection out of reverence for Christ'."],["Did Paul write Ephesians?","Tradition says yes. Many scholars think a disciple wrote it in Paul's name, noting style and theology. The Church holds it as inspired either way."]],
 keys:[["Eph 1:3–14","the great blessing"],["Eph 2:8–10","saved by grace, created for good works"],["Eph 2:14–22","Christ our peace; the household of God"],["Eph 4:4–6","one Lord, one faith, one baptism"],["Eph 5:21–33","the great mystery of marriage"],["Eph 6:10–18","the armour of God"]],
 author:"Paul in prison, according to tradition; authorship is debated by modern scholars."},

philippians:{era:"church",when:"From prison (Rome c. 61–62, or Ephesus c. 55)",genre:"Letter",
 lede:"Philippians is Paul's letter of joy, written in chains to his most beloved church. Its centre is the hymn to Christ who 'emptied himself' and was exalted.",
 story:`<p>Paul thanks the Philippians for their partnership and gifts. His imprisonment has advanced the Gospel; 'to me, to live is Christ; and to die is gain' (1:21). He urges humility and unity, and quotes or composes a hymn: Christ Jesus, 'being in the form of God… emptied himself, taking the form of a servant… becoming obedient unto death, even to the death of the cross. For which cause God also hath exalted him… that in the name of Jesus every knee should bow' (2:6–10).</p>
<p>He commends Timothy and Epaphroditus, warns against those who trust in circumcision, and counts everything loss 'for the excellent knowledge of Jesus Christ my Lord' (3:8), pressing on toward the prize. He ends: 'Rejoice in the Lord always… Be nothing solicitous… the peace of God… keep your hearts' (4:4–7), and thanks them for their gift: 'I can do all things in him who strengtheneth me' (4:13).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"Paul's imprisonment and joy"],[2,2,"The mind of Christ: the hymn; Timothy and Epaphroditus"],[3,3,"Knowing Christ; pressing on"],[4,4,"Rejoice; peace; thanks for the gift"]],
 people:[["Paul","A prisoner content in every circumstance.","Joy independent of circumstances."],["Timothy","Paul's son in the faith, 'like-minded' (2:20).","Selfless service."],["Epaphroditus","The Philippians' messenger who nearly died serving Paul.","Risking life for the Gospel."],["Euodia and Syntyche","Two women co-workers urged to agree (4:2).","Reconciliation in ministry."]],
 themes:[["Joy","'Rejoice' appears throughout."],["Humility","The mind of Christ."],["Knowing Christ","Everything else is loss."],["Contentment and peace","4:11–13."]],
 christ:"The Christ-hymn (2:6–11) is the classic text on the kenosis: the eternal Son, equal to God, takes human nature and humbles himself to the Cross, and is exalted as Lord.",
 church:"Philippians 2:6–11 grounds the doctrine of the Incarnation (CCC 461, 472) and is sung every Saturday evening in the Liturgy of the Hours. 4:4 gives Gaudete Sunday its name. 2:12 ('with fear and trembling work out your salvation') belongs to the teaching on cooperation with grace.",
 thomas:PAUL_THOMAS+" Thomas explains 'emptied himself' as taking the form of a servant without losing the form of God.",
 keys:[["Phil 1:21","to live is Christ"],["Phil 2:5–11","the Christ-hymn"],["Phil 2:12–13","work out your salvation"],["Phil 3:7–14","knowing Christ; pressing on"],["Phil 4:4–7","rejoice; the peace of God"],["Phil 4:13","I can do all things"]],
 author:"Paul, with Timothy. Undisputed."},

colossians:{era:"church",when:"From prison, c. AD 60–62",genre:"Letter",
 lede:"Colossians answers a teaching that added angel-worship, special visions and ascetic rules to the Gospel. Paul's answer is the supremacy and sufficiency of Christ, 'the image of the invisible God, the firstborn of every creature'.",
 story:`<p>After thanksgiving and prayer, Paul presents a hymn: Christ is the image of the invisible God; in him, through him and for him all things were created; he is before all, holds all together, is head of the Church, the firstborn from the dead, and through the blood of his Cross reconciles all things (1:15–20). Paul rejoices in his sufferings and 'fill[s] up those things that are wanting of the sufferings of Christ, in my flesh, for his body, which is the church' (1:24).</p>
<p>'In him dwelleth all the fulness of the Godhead corporeally' (2:9); so believers need no 'philosophy and vain deceit', no worship of angels, no rules about food and festivals as means to fulness. Raised with Christ, they must 'seek the things that are above' (3:1), put to death earthly vices, and put on mercy, kindness, humility, patience and above all charity. A household code and greetings close the letter.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"Thanksgiving; the Christ-hymn; Paul's ministry"],[2,2,"Warnings against false teaching"],[3,4,"New life in Christ; household; greetings"]],
 people:[["Epaphras","Founder of the Colossian church, praying for them (4:12).","Pastoral intercession."],["Onesimus","The former slave of Philemon, now 'a faithful and beloved brother' (4:9).","Links with Philemon."],["Luke","'The most dear physician' (4:14).","Evidence for Luke's profession."]],
 themes:[["Supremacy of Christ","Creator, sustainer, reconciler."],["Sufficiency of Christ","Fulness in him."],["Union with Christ","Died and raised with him."],["New life","Put off, put on."]],
 christ:"Colossians 1:15–20 and 2:9 are among the highest Christological texts in the New Testament, central to the Church's confession of Christ as Creator and incarnate God.",
 church:"Colossians 1:24 grounds the Christian meaning of redemptive suffering (CCC 1508, 618; Salvifici Doloris 24). 2:12 links baptism with burial and resurrection (CCC 1227). 3:16 commends 'psalms, hymns, and spiritual canticles'.",
 thomas:PAUL_THOMAS+" Thomas explains Col 1:24 not as a lack in Christ's Passion but as what is lacking in the sufferings of the whole Christ, Head and members.",
 keys:[["Col 1:15–20","the Christ-hymn"],["Col 1:24","filling up what is lacking"],["Col 2:8–10","fulness in Christ"],["Col 3:1–4","seek the things above"],["Col 3:12–17","put on charity"]],
 author:"Paul, with Timothy, according to tradition; some scholars attribute it to a disciple."},

"1-thessalonians":{era:"church",when:"Written from Corinth, c. AD 50–51",genre:"Letter",
 lede:"1 Thessalonians is probably the oldest book of the New Testament. Paul encourages a young church under persecution and answers its grief about believers who had died before Christ's return.",
 story:`<p>Paul recalls how the Thessalonians 'turned to God from idols' and received the word 'not as the word of men, but (as it is indeed) the word of God' (2:13), and how he cared for them 'as a nurse should cherish her children'. Forced to leave, he sent Timothy, whose good report fills him with joy.</p>
<p>He urges holiness and chastity: 'this is the will of God, your sanctification' (4:3). To those grieving the dead he gives hope: the dead in Christ will rise first, and the living will be caught up together with them to meet the Lord (4:13–18). The day of the Lord comes like a thief, so stay awake. 'Pray without ceasing. In all things give thanks' (5:17–18).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,3,"Thanksgiving and Paul's relationship with the church"],[4,4,"Holiness; hope for the dead"],[5,5,"The day of the Lord; final exhortations"]],
 people:[["Paul, Silvanus and Timothy","The missionaries who founded the church.","Spiritual parenthood."]],
 themes:[["Faith, hope and charity","The triad appears first here (1:3; 5:8)."],["Sanctification","Holiness in body and life."],["The Lord's coming","Hope for the dead."],["Prayer","Without ceasing."]],
 christ:"Christ 'died for us; that, whether we watch or sleep, we may live together with him' (5:10). His return is the Church's living hope.",
 church:"1 Thessalonians 2:13 shows the Church receiving the apostolic preaching as God's word (CCC 104). 4:13–18 grounds teaching on the resurrection and Christ's return (CCC 1001), and 5:17 the call to unceasing prayer (CCC 2742).",
 thomas:PAUL_THOMAS,
 keys:[["1 Thess 2:13","the word of God"],["1 Thess 4:3–8","your sanctification"],["1 Thess 4:13–18","the dead in Christ"],["1 Thess 5:16–22","rejoice, pray, give thanks; prove all things"]],
 author:"Paul, with Silvanus and Timothy. Undisputed."},

"2-thessalonians":{era:"church",when:"c. AD 51",genre:"Letter",
 lede:"2 Thessalonians corrects a belief that 'the day of the Lord is at hand' and warns that the 'man of sin' must first be revealed. It urges steadfastness, the keeping of apostolic traditions, and honest work.",
 story:`<p>Paul encourages the persecuted church: God will give rest to the afflicted when the Lord Jesus is revealed. Some have been alarmed by a claim, perhaps in a forged letter, that the day has already come. Paul answers that first there will be a revolt and 'the man of sin' will be revealed, who exalts himself above God; something 'withholdeth' him for now, and the Lord will destroy him 'with the spirit of his mouth' (2:3–8).</p>
<p>'Stand fast; and hold the traditions, which you have learned, whether by word, or by our epistle' (2:14 DR; 2:15 in modern Bibles). Some have stopped working; Paul commands: 'if any man will not work, neither let him eat' (3:10).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"Encouragement in persecution"],[2,2,"The day of the Lord and the man of sin; hold the traditions"],[3,3,"Prayer, work and discipline"]],
 people:[["The man of sin","A figure of final rebellion against God.","Linked by tradition with the Antichrist (1 Jn 2:18)."],["The restrainer","What or who 'withholdeth' him (2:6–7).","Interpreted by the Fathers as the Roman order, or the preaching of the Gospel."]],
 themes:[["Endurance","Faith growing under trial."],["The last things","Order and sobriety about the end."],["Tradition","Oral and written."],["Work","Dignity and duty of labour."]],
 christ:"The Lord Jesus will be revealed 'from heaven, with the angels of his power' (1:7) and will overcome all evil.",
 church:"2 Thessalonians 2:15 is a key text for Sacred Tradition alongside Scripture (DV 8; CCC 78–83). The 'man of sin' passage informs teaching on the final trial (CCC 675–677). 3:10 is cited in Catholic social teaching on the duty to work (CCC 2427).",
 thomas:PAUL_THOMAS+" Commenting on 2:15, Thomas notes that much of what the Church holds was handed on by the apostles without being written.",
 keys:[["2 Thess 2:1–12","the man of sin"],["2 Thess 2:15","hold the traditions"],["2 Thess 3:10","if any man will not work"]],
 author:"Paul, with Silvanus and Timothy, according to tradition; authorship is debated."},

"1-timothy":{era:"church",when:"c. AD 63–65 (traditional)",genre:"Pastoral letter",
 lede:"1 Timothy is a letter from Paul to his co-worker in Ephesus about guarding sound doctrine and ordering the Church: worship, the qualities of bishops and deacons, widows, and the dangers of false teaching and love of money.",
 story:`<p>Paul leaves Timothy in Ephesus to stop false teachers obsessed with myths and genealogies. He recalls his own mercy received: 'Christ Jesus came into this world to save sinners, of whom I am the chief' (1:15). He asks for prayers 'for all men… for kings', since God 'will have all men to be saved, and to come to the knowledge of the truth. For there is one God, and one mediator of God and men, the man Christ Jesus' (2:4–5).</p>
<p>He lists qualities for bishops and deacons (ch. 3) and calls the Church 'the house of God… the pillar and ground of the truth' (3:15). He warns of those who forbid marriage and certain foods, gives rules for widows and presbyters, and warns that 'the desire of money is the root of all evils' (6:10). Timothy must 'fight the good fight of faith' and 'keep that which is committed to thy trust' (6:12, 20), in Greek the parathēkē, 'deposit'.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"Against false teachers; Paul's testimony"],[2,3,"Prayer, worship, bishops and deacons; the Church as pillar of truth"],[4,4,"False asceticism; Timothy's example"],[5,6,"Widows, presbyters, slaves, the rich; guard the deposit"]],
 people:[["Timothy","Young leader of mixed Jewish-Greek parentage, Paul's 'beloved son'.","'Let no man despise thy youth' (4:12)."],["Paul","The aging apostle passing on responsibility.","Apostolic succession in practice."]],
 themes:[["Sound doctrine","Guarding the deposit of faith."],["Church order","Bishops, presbyters, deacons."],["Universal salvation","God wills all to be saved."],["Godliness and contentment","Against greed."]],
 christ:"'One mediator of God and men, the man Christ Jesus: who gave himself a redemption for all' (2:5–6). An early hymn (3:16): 'manifested in the flesh, was justified in the spirit… taken up in glory'.",
 church:"1 Timothy grounds the ordained ministry (CCC 1554ff.), the Church as 'pillar and ground of the truth' (CCC 171, 2032), prayer for rulers (CCC 1900), God's universal saving will (CCC 74, 851) and Christ's unique mediation, in which Mary's and the saints' intercession participate (CCC 970). The 'deposit' (6:20) is the root of the term depositum fidei (CCC 84).",
 thomas:PAUL_THOMAS,
 keys:[["1 Tim 1:15","to save sinners, of whom I am the chief"],["1 Tim 2:1–6","prayer for all; one mediator"],["1 Tim 3:1–13","bishops and deacons"],["1 Tim 3:15–16","pillar and ground of truth; the mystery of godliness"],["1 Tim 6:10","the love of money"],["1 Tim 6:20","keep the deposit"]],
 author:"Paul, according to tradition; many scholars date the Pastoral Letters later and attribute them to a disciple."},

"2-timothy":{era:"church",when:"From Rome before Paul's martyrdom, c. AD 64–67 (traditional)",genre:"Pastoral letter",
 lede:"2 Timothy is Paul's last testament, written from prison as he faces death. He urges Timothy to stir up the gift received by ordination, guard the faith, preach the word in season and out of season, and endure.",
 story:`<p>Paul remembers Timothy's sincere faith, received from his grandmother Lois and mother Eunice, and tells him to 'stir up the grace of God which is in thee, by the imposition of my hands' (1:6). Paul is not ashamed of his chains. Timothy must be a good soldier of Christ and entrust what he has heard 'to faithful men, who shall be fit to teach others also' (2:2).</p>
<p>Hard times will come, with people 'lovers of pleasures more than of God'. Timothy has known the Scriptures from infancy: 'All scripture, inspired of God, is profitable to teach, to reprove, to correct, to instruct in justice' (3:16). 'Preach the word: be instant in season, out of season' (4:2). Paul writes: 'I have fought a good fight, I have finished my course, I have kept the faith' (4:7). Almost all have deserted him, but the Lord stood by him.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"Stir up the gift; do not be ashamed"],[2,2,"A good soldier; handing on the faith"],[3,3,"Hard times; the Scriptures"],[4,4,"Preach the word; Paul's farewell"]],
 people:[["Timothy","Pastor charged to endure.","Ordination confers a grace to be rekindled."],["Lois and Eunice","Timothy's grandmother and mother who taught him the faith (1:5).","The domestic Church."],["Luke","'Only Luke is with me' (4:11).","Faithful friendship."],["Demas","Who deserted Paul, 'loving this world' (4:10).","A warning."]],
 themes:[["Endurance","Suffering for the Gospel."],["Handing on the faith","Four generations: Paul, Timothy, faithful men, others."],["Scripture","Inspired and useful."],["Faithful finish","The crown of justice."]],
 christ:"'Remember that the Lord Jesus Christ is risen again from the dead, of the seed of David, according to my gospel' (2:8). An early hymn: 'if we be dead with him, we shall live also with him' (2:11).",
 church:"2 Timothy 1:6 shows the sacramental grace of ordination by the laying on of hands (CCC 1590). 2:2 illustrates apostolic succession (CCC 77). 3:16 is the classic text on inspiration (Dei Verbum 11; CCC 105–107).",
 thomas:PAUL_THOMAS,
 keys:[["2 Tim 1:6","stir up the grace of ordination"],["2 Tim 2:2","entrust to faithful men"],["2 Tim 3:14–17","all Scripture is inspired"],["2 Tim 4:1–5","preach the word"],["2 Tim 4:6–8","I have fought a good fight"]],
 author:"See 1 Timothy."},

titus:{era:"church",when:"c. AD 63–65 (traditional)",genre:"Pastoral letter",
 lede:"Titus is a short letter instructing Paul's delegate in Crete to appoint presbyters in every city and teach sound doctrine, because the grace of God has appeared to train us in godly lives.",
 story:`<p>Paul left Titus in Crete to 'set in order the things that are wanting, and… ordain priests in every city' (1:5), describing the qualities of a bishop. Cretans had a poor reputation and false teachers were active. Titus must teach older men, older and younger women, young men and slaves to live soberly.</p>
<p>The reason: 'the grace of God our Saviour hath appeared to all men; instructing us, that, denying ungodliness and worldly desires, we should live soberly, and justly, and godly in this world, looking for the blessed hope and coming of the glory of the great God and our Saviour Jesus Christ' (2:11–13). God saved us 'by the laver of regeneration, and renovation of the Holy Ghost' (3:5).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"Appointing presbyters; false teachers"],[2,2,"Sound teaching for every group; grace appeared"],[3,3,"Good citizens; the laver of regeneration"]],
 people:[["Titus","A Gentile convert and trusted troubleshooter (2 Cor 7–8; Gal 2:3).","A bishop's work: order and teaching."]],
 themes:[["Church order","Presbyters and bishops."],["Grace that trains","Grace forms character."],["Baptismal rebirth","3:5."]],
 christ:"Titus 2:13 calls Jesus 'the great God and our Saviour', a clear New Testament affirmation of Christ's divinity.",
 church:"Titus 2:11–14 and 3:4–7 are read at the Christmas Masses. 3:5 is a classic text on baptism as rebirth (CCC 1215). 1:5–9 shows the apostolic appointment of presbyters (CCC 1562ff.).",
 thomas:PAUL_THOMAS,
 keys:[["Titus 1:5–9","presbyters and bishops"],["Titus 2:11–14","the grace of God hath appeared"],["Titus 3:4–7","the laver of regeneration"]],
 author:"See 1 Timothy."},

philemon:{era:"church",when:"From prison, c. AD 55–62",genre:"Personal letter",
 lede:"Philemon, the shortest of Paul's letters, asks a Christian slave-owner to receive back his runaway slave Onesimus, now converted, 'not now as a servant, but… a most dear brother'.",
 story:`<p>Paul writes to Philemon, a believer in whose house a church meets. His slave Onesimus has run away, met Paul in prison and become a Christian; Paul calls him 'my son… whom I have begotten in my bands' (v. 10). Onesimus ('useful') had been unprofitable but is now profitable. Paul sends him back, asking Philemon to receive him as he would Paul himself, offering to repay any debt, and hinting that Philemon will do 'even more than I say' (v. 21).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"The whole letter: greeting, thanksgiving, appeal for Onesimus, greetings"]],
 people:[["Philemon","A Christian householder in Colossae.","Charity transforms social relationships."],["Onesimus","A runaway slave converted through Paul.","Possibly the later bishop Onesimus of Ephesus mentioned by Ignatius."],["Apphia and Archippus","Members of Philemon's household church.","The house church."]],
 themes:[["Brotherhood in Christ","Social status transformed."],["Reconciliation","Paul as mediator."],["Persuasion by love","Paul appeals rather than commands (vv. 8–9)."]],
 christ:"Paul's offer to pay Onesimus's debt ('put that to my account', v. 18) mirrors Christ's intercession for sinners; Luther and the Fathers noted the parallel.",
 church:"Philemon shows the Gospel's leaven working within ancient institutions, sowing principles of equal dignity that later led the Church to condemn slavery (CCC 2414; Gaudium et Spes 27).",
 thomas:PAUL_THOMAS,
 hard:[["Why didn't Paul condemn slavery outright?","He addressed a small, powerless community within the Roman order. He undermines slavery's logic by declaring master and slave brothers in Christ (Gal 3:28; Phlm 16). The Church later explicitly condemned slavery as gravely contrary to human dignity (CCC 2414)."]],
 keys:[["Phlm 10–16","Onesimus, a most dear brother"],["Phlm 18–19","put that to my account"]],
 author:"Paul. Undisputed."},

hebrews:{era:"church",when:"Probably before AD 70",genre:"Homily in letter form",
 lede:"Hebrews is a sermon urging Jewish Christians tempted to fall back not to abandon Christ. It shows that Christ is superior to angels, Moses and the Levitical priesthood: the eternal high priest after the order of Melchisedech, whose one sacrifice opens heaven.",
 story:`<p>'God, who, at sundry times and in divers manners, spoke in times past to the fathers by the prophets, last of all, in these days hath spoken to us by his Son', who is 'the brightness of his glory, and the figure of his substance' (1:1–3). The Son is above the angels, yet became like us in all things, tempted but without sin, to be a merciful high priest. He is greater than Moses, and Israel's failure to enter God's rest is a warning.</p>
<p>The core (chs 5–10) shows Christ as priest after the order of Melchisedech, the mysterious priest-king who blessed Abraham with bread and wine. The old covenant's priests offered repeated sacrifices in an earthly copy of the sanctuary; Christ, by his own blood, entered the heavenly sanctuary 'once', obtaining eternal redemption, and is mediator of the new covenant promised by Jeremiah. Do not abandon the assembly. Chapter 11 recalls the heroes of faith, from Abel to the martyrs; surrounded by 'so great a cloud of witnesses', we run looking on Jesus (12:1–2). 'Jesus Christ, yesterday, and to day; and the same for ever' (13:8).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,2,"The Son superior to the angels"],[3,4,"Superior to Moses; God's rest"],[5,7,"High priest after the order of Melchisedech"],[8,10,"The new covenant and the one sacrifice"],[11,11,"The heroes of faith"],[12,13,"Endurance, discipline, worship and life"]],
 people:[["Melchisedech","King of Salem and priest of God Most High (Gen 14), 'without father, without mother, without genealogy' in the text (7:3).","Type of Christ's eternal priesthood and of the Eucharist's bread and wine."],["The heroes of faith","Abel, Enoch, Noah, Abraham, Sarah, Moses, Rahab and others (ch. 11).","A communion of saints across the ages."]],
 themes:[["Christ the high priest","Merciful, faithful, eternal."],["One sacrifice","Offered once for all."],["New covenant","Better promises."],["Faith and perseverance","Do not drift away."],["Heavenly worship","We have come to Mount Sion (12:22–24)."]],
 christ:"Hebrews is the New Testament's fullest treatment of Christ's priesthood and sacrifice: Son, Word, Creator, sinless high priest, victim, and mediator, who 'always liveth to make intercession for us' (7:25).",
 church:"Hebrews underlies the Church's teaching that the Mass is not a new sacrifice but the one sacrifice of Christ made present (CCC 1366–1367; Trent, Session 22), the priesthood of Christ shared by the ordained (CCC 1544–1545), Christ's heavenly intercession (CCC 662) and the definition of faith (11:1; CCC 146). 6:4–6 and 10:26 were debated in the early Church regarding post-baptismal sin.",
 thomas:PAUL_THOMAS+" Thomas, with the tradition of his day, held that Paul was the author in substance, while noting that some attributed the style to Luke or another.",
 hard:[["Who wrote Hebrews?","Its style differs from Paul's letters. Origen said, 'who wrote the epistle, in truth God knows' (Eusebius, Church History VI.25). The Western Church long counted it among Paul's letters; the Council of Trent listed it as Paul's. Its canonicity and inspiration are not in doubt."]],
 keys:[["Heb 1:1–4","God has spoken by his Son"],["Heb 4:14–16","a high priest who can sympathise"],["Heb 7","Melchisedech"],["Heb 9:11–14","by his own blood, once"],["Heb 11:1","the definition of faith"],["Heb 12:1–2","the cloud of witnesses"],["Heb 13:8","yesterday, today and for ever"]],
 author:"Unknown; traditionally attributed to Paul in the West, with proposals including Barnabas, Apollos and Luke."},

james:{era:"church",when:"c. AD 45–62",genre:"Letter of moral exhortation",
 lede:"James is the Bible's most practical letter: faith must show itself in works, trials produce patience, the tongue must be tamed, and the rich must not oppress the poor. It also gives the scriptural basis for the anointing of the sick.",
 story:`<p>James writes 'to the twelve tribes which are scattered abroad'. Count trials as joy, for they produce patience; ask God for wisdom in faith; be 'doers of the word, and not hearers only' (1:22). 'Religion clean and undefiled' is to visit orphans and widows and keep oneself unspotted from the world (1:27). Do not favour the rich man in fine clothes over the poor man in the assembly.</p>
<p>'What shall it profit… if a man say he hath faith, but hath not works?… faith without works is dead' (2:14, 26); 'by works a man is justified; and not by faith only' (2:24). The tongue is a fire; wisdom from above is pure and peaceable. Submit to God and resist the devil. Woe to rich oppressors. Be patient for the Lord's coming. 'Is any man sick among you? Let him bring in the priests of the church, and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord… and if he be in sins, they shall be forgiven him. Confess therefore your sins one to another' (5:14–16).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"Trials, wisdom, doing the word"],[2,2,"Partiality; faith and works"],[3,3,"The tongue; true wisdom"],[4,5,"Humility, the rich, patience, the anointing of the sick, prayer"]],
 people:[["James","'The brother of the Lord' (Gal 1:19), leader of the Jerusalem church, martyred c. 62.","Practical holiness; tradition identifies him with James the Less."],["Abraham and Rahab","Examples of faith shown by works (2:21–25).","Faith that acts."],["Elijah","A man like us whose prayer was heard (5:17).","Power of prayer."]],
 themes:[["Faith and works","Living faith acts."],["Trials","Testing produces endurance."],["The tongue","Speech reveals the heart."],["Justice for the poor","God chose the poor rich in faith."],["Prayer and healing","5:13–18."]],
 christ:"James echoes the Sermon on the Mount at many points (e.g. 5:12 on oaths; Mt 5:34–37), showing the Lord's teaching lived in the early Church.",
 church:"James 5:14–15 is the scriptural basis of the sacrament of the Anointing of the Sick (CCC 1510–1511, 1526; Trent, Session 14). 2:24 balances Paul in the Catholic doctrine of justification (CCC 1815, 2006ff.; Trent, Session 6). 5:16 is cited for confession of sins. 2:14–17 grounds the Church's teaching that faith requires works of mercy.",
 thomas:"Thomas did not comment on James, but cites James 2 constantly ('faith without works is dead') for the distinction between living faith formed by charity and dead, unformed faith (ST II-II q.4 aa.3–4). Luther called James 'an epistle of straw' in his 1522 preface; the Church affirms its full canonicity.",
 hard:[["Doesn't James contradict Paul?","No. Paul denies that the works of the Mosaic Law, or works done to earn grace, justify; James denies that a bare, inactive faith justifies. Both teach faith that works through love (Gal 5:6). Augustine already harmonised them this way."]],
 keys:[["Jas 1:2–5","trials and wisdom"],["Jas 1:22–27","doers of the word; pure religion"],["Jas 2:14–26","faith without works is dead"],["Jas 3:1–12","the tongue"],["Jas 5:14–16","anointing of the sick; confess your sins"]],
 author:"James 'the brother of the Lord' according to tradition; some scholars date it later."},

"1-peter":{era:"church",when:"From 'Babylon' (Rome), c. AD 62–64",genre:"Letter",
 lede:"1 Peter encourages Christians suffering for their faith as 'strangers and pilgrims' in a hostile society. Born anew through the resurrection, they are 'a chosen generation, a kingly priesthood', called to holy conduct and patient witness.",
 story:`<p>Peter blesses God who 'hath regenerated us unto a lively hope, by the resurrection of Jesus Christ' (1:3) to an imperishable inheritance. Trials test faith like gold in fire. 'Be you holy, because I am holy'. Believers are living stones built into a spiritual house, 'a chosen generation, a kingly priesthood, a holy nation, a purchased people' (2:9).</p>
<p>They must live honourably among pagans, respect authority, and, as slaves, wives and husbands, bear witness by conduct. Christ suffered, leaving an example; 'by whose stripes you were healed' (2:24). 'Being ready always to satisfy every one that asketh you a reason of that hope which is in you' (3:15). Christ 'preached to those spirits that were in prison', and Noah's ark prefigures baptism, 'which now saveth you' (3:19–21). The elders must shepherd the flock willingly; 'your adversary the devil, as a roaring lion, goeth about seeking whom he may devour' (5:8).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,2,"New birth, holiness, a royal priesthood"],[2,3,"Witness in society, household and suffering; Christ's descent and baptism"],[4,5,"Suffering as Christians; elders; vigilance"]],
 people:[["Peter","'An apostle of Jesus Christ' and 'fellow elder', witness of Christ's sufferings (5:1).","The first pope strengthening his brethren (Lk 22:32)."],["Silvanus and Mark","Peter's helpers in Rome (5:12–13).","Mark 'my son' supports the tradition behind Mark's Gospel."]],
 themes:[["Hope","A living hope through the resurrection."],["Suffering","Sharing Christ's sufferings."],["Holiness","Conduct among unbelievers."],["The royal priesthood","The whole people offers spiritual sacrifices."],["Apologetics","Give a reason for your hope."]],
 christ:"Christ is the precious corner stone rejected by builders (2:6–7), the suffering Servant of Isaiah 53 (2:22–25), who descended to the dead and rose.",
 church:"1 Peter 2:9 grounds the common priesthood of the faithful (CCC 1141, 1268, 1546). 3:18–20 and 4:6 underlie the article 'he descended into hell' (CCC 632–635). 3:20–21 links the ark with baptism (CCC 1219). 3:15 is the charter of apologetics. 5:13 ('Babylon') is early testimony to Peter in Rome.",
 thomas:"Thomas uses 1 Peter 3:19 in discussing Christ's descent into hell (ST III q.52). Bede wrote a commentary on the Catholic Epistles; Augustine discussed 1 Pet 3:19 in his letter to Evodius.",
 keys:[["1 Pet 1:3–9","a living hope"],["1 Pet 2:4–10","living stones; a royal priesthood"],["1 Pet 2:21–25","by his stripes you were healed"],["1 Pet 3:15","a reason for your hope"],["1 Pet 3:18–22","the descent; baptism saves"],["1 Pet 5:8","the roaring lion"]],
 author:"Peter, through Silvanus (5:12). Some scholars date it after Peter's death."},

"2-peter":{era:"church",when:"Traditionally c. AD 64–67",genre:"Letter-testament",
 lede:"2 Peter, Peter's farewell, calls Christians to grow in virtue and knowledge, as 'partakers of the divine nature', and to reject false teachers who deny the Lord's return.",
 story:`<p>God has given us 'most great and precious promises: that by these you may be made partakers of the divine nature' (1:4). Add to faith virtue, knowledge, self-control, patience, godliness, brotherly love and charity. Peter knows his death is near. He was an eyewitness of Christ's majesty on the holy mountain (the Transfiguration). 'No prophecy of scripture is made by private interpretation' (1:20).</p>
<p>False teachers will come, as in the past; God's judgment on the fallen angels, the Flood and Sodom shows he knows how to rescue the godly and punish the wicked. Scoffers ask, 'Where is his promise?'; but 'one day with the Lord is as a thousand years', and God is patient, not willing that any should perish. In Paul's letters 'are certain things hard to be understood, which the unlearned and unstable wrest… to their own destruction' (3:16).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"Growth in virtue; Peter's testimony; Scripture"],[2,2,"False teachers and their judgment"],[3,3,"The Lord's coming; patience; Paul's letters"]],
 people:[["Peter","Apostle and eyewitness facing death (1:14).","Apostolic testimony handed on."],["Paul","'Our most dear brother' whose letters are counted with 'the other scriptures' (3:15–16).","Early recognition of Paul's letters as Scripture."]],
 themes:[["Divinisation","Partakers of the divine nature."],["Growth in virtue","A ladder of virtues."],["Authentic interpretation","Against private interpretation."],["Patience of God","Time for repentance."]],
 christ:"Peter's witness to the Transfiguration (1:16–18) confirms Christ's glory and the Father's voice: 'This is my beloved Son'.",
 church:"2 Peter 1:4 is a central text for the doctrine of grace as participation in the divine nature (CCC 460, 1265, 1996). 1:20–21 and 3:16 ground the Church's teaching that Scripture is interpreted within the Church, not privately (CCC 113, 119; DV 10).",
 thomas:"Thomas frequently cites 2 Peter 1:4 for grace as a participation in the divine nature (ST I-II q.110 a.3; q.112 a.1).",
 hard:[["Did Peter write it?","It was the most disputed New Testament book in the early Church; its style differs from 1 Peter. Many scholars see it as a testament written in Peter's name after his death. The Church received it as canonical (Councils of Rome, Hippo and Carthage; Trent)."]],
 keys:[["2 Pet 1:3–11","partakers of the divine nature; the ladder of virtues"],["2 Pet 1:16–21","eyewitnesses; no private interpretation"],["2 Pet 3:8–10","a thousand years as one day"],["2 Pet 3:15–16","Paul's letters hard to understand"]],
 author:"Peter according to the letter itself; authorship is debated."},

"1-john":{era:"church",when:"Ephesus, c. AD 90–100",genre:"Letter-homily",
 lede:"1 John answers a split in the Johannine churches over whether Jesus truly came in the flesh. Its tests of true faith are simple: confessing Christ incarnate, keeping his commandments, and loving one another, because 'God is charity'.",
 story:`<p>The author writes as an eyewitness of 'the Word of life… which we have seen with our eyes… and our hands have handled' (1:1). God is light; 'if we say that we have no sin, we deceive ourselves… If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just, to forgive us' (1:8–9). Jesus Christ is our advocate and 'the propitiation for our sins' (2:2).</p>
<p>Some have left, denying that Jesus is the Christ come in the flesh; they are 'antichrists' (2:18–22; 4:2–3). Believers are children of God now, and 'we shall be like to him: because we shall see him as he is' (3:2). 'God is charity: and he that abideth in charity, abideth in God, and God in him' (4:16). 'Perfect charity casteth out fear' (4:18). The letter distinguishes sin 'unto death' from sin not unto death (5:16–17).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,2,"Walking in the light; sin and forgiveness; antichrists"],[3,3,"Children of God; love in deed"],[4,4,"Testing the spirits; God is love"],[5,5,"Faith overcomes the world; eternal life; prayer and sin"]],
 people:[["John the Elder","Eyewitness author, writing as a father to 'my little children'.","Pastoral authority grounded in apostolic witness."],["The secessionists","Those who 'went out from us' (2:19).","Error about the Incarnation splits communities."]],
 themes:[["God is love","The heart of revelation."],["Incarnation","Jesus Christ come in the flesh."],["Assurance","'That you may know that you have eternal life' (5:13)."],["Sin and forgiveness","Confession and the advocate."],["Love of neighbour","'He that loveth not his brother… cannot love God, whom he seeth not' (4:20)."]],
 christ:"Christ is the Word of life made visible and tangible, the advocate with the Father, the Son who came 'by water and blood' (5:6).",
 church:"1 John 4:8, 16 ('God is charity') underlies CCC 221 and Benedict XVI's Deus Caritas Est. 5:16–17 grounds the distinction of mortal and venial sin (CCC 1854). 1:9 is linked with confession. 4:1–3 gives a criterion for discernment of spirits.",
 thomas:"Thomas cites 1 John 4:16 in the treatise on charity (ST II-II q.23) and 3:2 ('we shall see him as he is') for the beatific vision (ST I q.12 a.1). Augustine's ten Homilies on the First Epistle of John contain his famous 'love, and do what you will' (Hom. 7.8).",
 hard:[["What about the 'three witnesses in heaven' (5:7 in the Douay-Rheims)?","The 'Johannine comma' (the Father, the Word and the Holy Ghost: these three are one) is found in the Latin tradition but not in the Greek manuscripts before the late Middle Ages. In 1927 the Holy Office allowed Catholic scholars to conclude it is not original. The doctrine of the Trinity rests on many other texts."]],
 keys:[["1 Jn 1:1–4","what we have seen and handled"],["1 Jn 1:8–2:2","confess our sins; the advocate"],["1 Jn 3:1–3","children of God; we shall see him"],["1 Jn 4:7–21","God is love"],["1 Jn 5:16–17","sin unto death"]],
 author:"John the apostle according to tradition; written in the same circle as the Fourth Gospel."},

"2-john":{era:"church",when:"c. AD 90–100",genre:"Short letter",
 lede:"2 John is a brief note from 'the ancient' (the elder) to 'the lady elect, and her children' (a church), urging love and warning against welcoming teachers who deny that Jesus Christ came in the flesh.",
 story:`<p>The elder rejoices that some of the church walk in truth and repeats the commandment 'that we love one another' (v. 5). Many deceivers 'confess not that Jesus Christ is come in the flesh' (v. 7). Whoever 'revolteth, and continueth not in the doctrine of Christ, hath not God'. Do not receive such a teacher into your house or greet him. The elder hopes to come and speak face to face.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"The whole letter: truth, love, and warning against deceivers"]],
 people:[["The elder (presbyter)","The author, traditionally John.","Pastoral oversight."],["The elect lady","Probably a local church personified.","The Church as mother of the faithful."]],
 themes:[["Truth and love together","Love walks in the commandments."],["Abiding in the teaching of Christ","Doctrinal fidelity."],["Hospitality and its limits","Not supporting false teaching."]],
 christ:"Confessing Christ come in the flesh is the test of true faith (v. 7).",
 church:"2 John shows early concern for orthodoxy and the community's responsibility not to promote error. 'The doctrine of Christ' (v. 9) is a root of the idea of a deposit of faith to be guarded.",
 thomas:"The Fathers debated whether 'the elder' was John the apostle or another John; Papias mentions 'John the Presbyter'.",
 keys:[["2 Jn 5–6","love one another"],["2 Jn 7–11","deceivers and antichrists"]],
 author:"'The ancient' (presbyteros), traditionally John the apostle."},

"3-john":{era:"church",when:"c. AD 90–100",genre:"Short letter",
 lede:"3 John, the shortest New Testament book, commends Gaius for his hospitality to travelling missionaries, criticises the domineering Diotrephes, and recommends Demetrius.",
 story:`<p>The elder writes to his beloved Gaius, rejoicing that he walks in the truth: 'I have no greater grace than this, to hear that my children walk in truth' (v. 4). Gaius has welcomed travelling brethren; supporting them makes us 'fellow helpers of the truth'. Diotrephes, 'who loveth to have the pre-eminence', refuses the elder's authority and expels those who receive the missionaries. 'Follow not that which is evil, but that which is good'. Demetrius has a good testimony.</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"The whole letter: Gaius commended, Diotrephes rebuked, Demetrius recommended"]],
 people:[["Gaius","A generous host.","Hospitality as service to the Gospel."],["Diotrephes","A local leader who loves pre-eminence.","Ambition that divides the Church."],["Demetrius","Well spoken of by all.","Good reputation in Christ."]],
 themes:[["Walking in the truth","Joy of a spiritual father."],["Hospitality","Supporting missionaries."],["Authority and ambition","Service versus domination."]],
 christ:"Missionaries go out 'for his name' (v. 7): the Name of Christ is the motive of mission.",
 church:"3 John shows early church life: itinerant missionaries, house churches, conflicts over authority. It is a mirror for clergy and laity on humility and hospitality.",
 thomas:"Bede comments on 3 John, presenting Gaius as a model of generous charity.",
 keys:[["3 Jn 4","my children walk in truth"],["3 Jn 5–8","fellow helpers of the truth"],["3 Jn 9–11","Diotrephes"]],
 author:"'The ancient', traditionally John."},

jude:{era:"church",when:"c. AD 60–90",genre:"Letter of warning",
 lede:"Jude urges believers 'to contend earnestly for the faith once delivered to the saints' against immoral intruders, with vivid examples from Israel's history and Jewish tradition, and ends with one of the most beautiful doxologies in Scripture.",
 story:`<p>Jude, 'the servant of Jesus Christ, and brother of James', had meant to write about salvation but must instead warn against ungodly men who 'turn the grace of our Lord God into riotousness' and deny Christ. He recalls Israel's unbelief in the desert, the angels who left their dwelling, Sodom and Gomorrah, Cain, Balaam and Core. He mentions the archangel Michael disputing with the devil over Moses' body (v. 9) and quotes a prophecy of Enoch (vv. 14–15).</p>
<p>Believers must build themselves up in faith, pray in the Holy Spirit, keep themselves in God's love, and show mercy to those who doubt. The doxology: 'Now to him who is able to preserve you without sin, and to present you spotless before the presence of his glory with exceeding joy… be glory and magnificence, empire and power' (vv. 24–25).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,1,"The whole letter: contend for the faith; examples of judgment; exhortation; doxology"]],
 people:[["Jude (Judas Thaddeus)","'Brother of James', traditionally the apostle Jude.","Patron of desperate causes in popular devotion."],["Michael the archangel","Disputes with the devil (v. 9).","Source for the Church's veneration of Michael."],["Enoch","'The seventh from Adam' (v. 14).","Jude cites Jewish tradition (1 Enoch) without making that book Scripture."]],
 themes:[["The deposit of faith","Delivered once for all."],["Judgment on false teachers","Historical warnings."],["Perseverance","Keep yourselves in God's love."],["Doxology","God able to keep us."]],
 christ:"Jesus Christ is 'our only Lord and Master' (v. 4), who keeps the faithful for eternal life (v. 21).",
 church:"Jude 3 is a foundational text for the faith as a definitive deposit handed down (CCC 171; DV 4: no new public revelation). Its citation of non-canonical writings shows that Scripture can quote other sources without canonising them.",
 thomas:"Jude 3 fits Thomas's teaching that the articles of faith grow in explicitness over time but not in substance (ST II-II q.1 a.7).",
 keys:[["Jude 3","contend for the faith once delivered"],["Jude 9","Michael and the devil"],["Jude 20–23","pray in the Spirit; show mercy"],["Jude 24–25","the doxology"]],
 author:"Jude, brother of James, traditionally the apostle Jude Thaddeus."},

revelation:{era:"church",when:"From Patmos, c. AD 95 (or the late 60s)",genre:"Apocalypse, prophecy and letter (Douay-Rheims: Apocalypse)",
 lede:"Revelation (Apocalypse, 'unveiling') lifts the curtain on heaven to show persecuted Christians that the slain and risen Lamb reigns, that evil will be judged, and that history ends in the wedding of the Lamb and the new Jerusalem. Much of its imagery is liturgical.",
 story:`<p>Exiled on Patmos 'on the Lord's day', John sees the risen Christ among seven golden lampstands and receives letters for seven churches of Asia, praising, rebuking and promising (chs 2–3). A door opens in heaven: God on his throne, worshipped by elders and four living creatures crying 'Holy, holy, holy'. Only 'the Lamb standing as it were slain' can open the scroll with seven seals (chs 4–5).</p>
<p>The opening of seals, the sounding of trumpets and the pouring of bowls bring cycles of judgment, interrupted by visions of the sealed servants of God and a great multitude 'which no man could number' in white robes. A woman clothed with the sun gives birth to a male child whom a dragon seeks to devour; Michael defeats the dragon (ch. 12). A beast from the sea and one from the earth demand worship and the mark 666 (ch. 13). Babylon, the great harlot drunk with the blood of martyrs, falls (17–18).</p>
<p>Heaven rejoices: 'Blessed are they that are called to the marriage supper of the Lamb' (19:9). Christ rides out as the Word of God and conquers. Satan is bound for a thousand years, then finally defeated; the dead are judged. John sees 'a new heaven and a new earth' and the holy city, new Jerusalem, coming down 'as a bride adorned for her husband'; God will wipe away every tear (21:1–4). The river of life flows from the throne. The book ends: 'Come, Lord Jesus' (22:20).</p>`,
 outline:[[1,3,"The vision of Christ and the letters to the seven churches"],[4,5,"The heavenly throne room; the Lamb and the scroll"],[6,11,"Seven seals and seven trumpets"],[12,14,"The woman, the dragon and the beasts"],[15,16,"Seven bowls"],[17,19,"The fall of Babylon; the marriage of the Lamb; the Rider"],[20,20,"The thousand years and the last judgment"],[21,22,"The new heaven and new earth; the new Jerusalem"]],
 people:[["John","A prophet in exile 'for the word of God' (1:9).","Witness of Christ's victory."],["The Lamb","Christ crucified and risen, worshipped with the Father.","The central figure: the Lamb is the key to history."],["The woman clothed with the sun","Mother of the male child, persecuted by the dragon (ch. 12).","Read as Israel, the Church and Mary; the liturgy reads chapter 12 on the Assumption."],["The dragon and the beasts","Satan and the powers that serve him: oppressive empire and false prophecy.","Evil imitates God but is defeated."],["Babylon","The great city of luxury and persecution.","Any society that sets itself against God."]],
 themes:[["Christ's victory","The Lamb has conquered."],["Worship","Heaven is a liturgy."],["Endurance of the saints","Faithful unto death."],["Judgment","God judges evil justly."],["New creation","Everything made new."]],
 christ:"Christ is 'the faithful witness, the first begotten of the dead, and the prince of the kings of the earth' (1:5), the Alpha and Omega, the slain Lamb on the throne, and the Bridegroom. The Apocalypse ends where Genesis began: the tree of life, the river and God dwelling with his people.",
 church:"Revelation underlies the Church's teaching on the heavenly liturgy (CCC 1137–1139), the Last Judgment and the new creation (CCC 1042–1050), Christ's final victory over Satan (CCC 677), and the communion of saints. The Church rejects millenarianism (a literal earthly reign before the end; CCC 676). Its hymns ('Worthy is the Lamb') are prayed in Vespers; 19:9 is said before Communion.",
 thomas:"The Apocalypse commentaries once attributed to Thomas are not his. Augustine's City of God XX reads the 'thousand years' as the age of the Church, the dominant Catholic interpretation. Victorinus of Pettau wrote the earliest surviving Latin commentary.",
 hard:[["Is Revelation a code for predicting current events?","It is an apocalypse: symbolic vision addressed first to Christians under Rome, revealing the meaning of all history in Christ. The Church reads it as prophecy of Christ's final victory, not a timetable. Numbers like 666 and 144,000 are symbolic (666 is often linked to 'Nero Caesar' by Hebrew letter-values)."],["What is the 'rapture'?","The idea of a secret removal of Christians before a tribulation is a 19th-century Protestant development (J.N. Darby). The Church teaches one visible return of Christ in glory (CCC 668–682)."]],
 keys:[["Rev 1:9–20","the vision of the Son of man"],["Rev 2–3","the letters to the seven churches"],["Rev 4–5","the throne and the Lamb"],["Rev 7:9–17","the great multitude"],["Rev 12","the woman and the dragon"],["Rev 19:6–9","the marriage supper of the Lamb"],["Rev 21:1–5","all things new"],["Rev 22:20","Come, Lord Jesus"]],
 author:"John, identified by Justin Martyr and Irenaeus as the apostle; Dionysius of Alexandria noted differences in style from the Gospel. Irenaeus dates it to the end of Domitian's reign (c. 95)."}
});

;PACK_DONE("intro");
